window.BUILTIN_ENGLISH_ARTICLES = [{"title": "英文 T1", "content": "     I have no fear of that.  I think contact with other theater can only \nenrich our own work as we see ourselves in an international context.  \nBeckett, who wrote in France, is an example of that sort of enrichment.  \nThere is a history in Irish theater of our writers viewing Ireland from \nabroad.  I think it's a terrible thing that they felt they were forced to.  \nBut no one can say it's a bad thing.  Well, for instance, last summer's \nAbbey production of \"Hedda Gabler\" by Ibsen, a Norwegian, was hailed as a \nmajor reinterpretation of that work.  The Abbey's production of the play \nwas viewed in international terms.  That is part of a process that can \ncontinue.  For the Irish to have access to theater that is not just English \nor American is important to European theater, not just in terms of showing \nhow the Irish play those works, but in bringing them to Ireland.  It shows \nus ways of making theater available in other places.  I think all of that \nwill affect the climate in which we work.\n     Many in the United states, particularly staunch free-traders, still \nvigorously oppose efforts by the government to aid and abet U.S. business.  \nTheir favorite pose last week was to say Bush's trip \"demeans the presi-\ndency,\" as William Niskanen, a former Reagan administration economist, told \none reporter.  In this view, the president, all leader of the free world, \nshould devote his foreign trips to high-minded subjects like arms control, \nenvironmental protection and human rights.  The implication tection and \nhuman rights.  The implication is that issues of trade and commerce are \nsecondary, if not downright grubby.\n     The Long Island school's quietly eloquent founder, Henry Viscardi, \nJr., saluted them:  \"As permanently, totally disabled persons, you could \nhave remained at home to be supported.  Instead, you have sought the chall-\nenges of life.  You go forth with an optimism that accepts life's often un-\nfavorable odds-but will not stop hoping and trying.  What a lesson you re-\npresent for the young people of America!\"\n     The above findings might prove interesting to working parents who must \nfind day care for their babies.  Family care in a private home, with se-\nveral babies together, is probably the ideal way to care for babies under \nthree.  Dr. Benjamin Spock, well-known pediatrician and author of books \nabout babies, supports the idea.  He says the family day care is sounder in \ntheory than hiring a housekeeper or a babysitter.  Last spring, for the \nfourth successive year, an unusual graduation took place at a unique high \nschool in Albertson, N.Y. as the smiling seniors, wearing traditional cap \nand gown, lined up for their diplomas, all were in wheelchairs.\n     The electronic computer is contributing to advancements in science, \ntechnology, business, industry, education, and government.  A computer plan \nthe orbit of a satellite.  It controls the lift-off of a rocket.  The elec-\ntronic computer can also write checks, run machines, make predictions, han-\ndle payrolls, fight against crimes, maintain air safety, book tickets and \nkeep track of reservation, and even match job-seeking men with man-seeking \njobs.  A day seldom passes without another job being assigned to the elec-\ntronic computer.  Businesses have long used machines of one type or another \nin their accounting and sales departments.  The cash register, adding ma-\nchine, and simple desk or pocket calculator are all computers.  The tran-\nsistorized computer can run other machines.  It is the nerve center of \nautomation.  Automation has been defined as the use of a machine to run \nother machines.  In the past, we assumed that only a man could operate a \nrolling mill in a steel plant or keep things going in an oil refinery.  \nComputers have now taken over such responsibilities.\n     One summer afternoon, a farmer was walking through a forest.  He was \ncarrying a big bag of rice home.  When he was resting under a big tree, he \nsaw a lion running to him.  He was much afraid.  But the lion said, \"Please \nhelp me.  Let me go into your bag and they won't find me.  \"The farmer was \nvery kind and he helped the lion.  After the hunters went away, the lion \nwent out and said, \"I am hungry now.  I want to eat you for my lunch.  \"The \nfarmer was afraid again and didn't know what to do.  He said, \"don't eat \nme, Mr. Lion.  Please let me go.\"  The lion didn't want to let the farmer \ngo.  Just then an old man came.  The farmer told the old man about the \nstory.  The old man said, \"Please go back into the bag, Mr. Lion.  Then I \nwill know who is right.\"  After the lion went into the bag, the old man and \nthe farmer tied up the bag.  They hit the lion and killed the lion.\n     Once upon a time, there was a poor family.  They had one son.  This \nson often went out to steal things.  Every time he brought something home, \nhis mother was happy.  Once he brought her some apples.  She did not ask \nhim how and where he got them.  She just said, \"That's very good.\"  Many \nyears passed, the little boy grew up and became a full-time thief.  One day \nthe police caught him to jail.  The court said that he must die for his \ncrimes.  But before dying, he could have one last wish.  The son asked for \nhis mother.  He said that he wanted to see her tongue.  The mother came and \nstuck her tongue.  Then son cut his mother's tongue until it was bleeding.  \nEveryone was surprised.  They wanted to know why he had hurt his mother.\n     When I was walked down the street the other day, I picked up a purse.  \nThere was nothing inside it except some change and an old picture of a wo-\nman and a young girl about twelve years old, who looked like the woman's \ndaughter.  I gave it to a policeman in the station, who took down my name \nand address in case the owner might want to write and thank me.  That \nevening I went to have dinner with my aunt and uncle, who also invited a \nyoung woman whose face was known to me.  I was quite sure that we had not \nmet before.  She said that she had lost her purse that had not met before.  \nShe was the young girl in the picture although she was now much older.  \nAfter dinner, we went to the police station.  As the policeman gave the \npurse back, he said that I had found the purse and the person who had lost \nit.\n     In short, our universities and colleges are concerned only with the \ndevelopment of specialized skills and abilities, and do little to develop \nof character among students.  This is one of the crises facing education in \nthis country today.  Was sincerely hope that educational officials and uni-\nversity authorities will give serious thought to this problem.  An educa-\ntion reform movement is under way and gathering momentum in this country.  \nMost of the reforms being sought, we believe, are really superficial and \ntouch only the surface of the educational problems currently in existence.  \nIt is essential to probe deeper and find the real issues.\n     Each university and college has a student counseling center to aid \ndepressed students.  Generally speaking, however, the service does not seem \nto help very much.  Some fundamental changes should be made in college \neducation to make college students more emotionally stable individuals.  \nAbove all, the schools must stop neglecting their responsibility to teach \nvalues.  The program at local universities are on the whole too specialized \nand therefore fail to turn out well-rounded individuals.  The programs fail \nto help students grow emotionally and morally as well as intellectually.  \nCollege teachers are too busy with research duties to care about students' \nmoral and emotional lives.  And school officials too concerned with \nadministration to heed the personal problems of students.  The recent stu-\ndent suicides indicate that the cultivation of a positive outlook on life \namong students is a task much neglected.  Re-emphasizing this task is \nurgently needed to improve education here.\n     All the world's a stage.  \"So wrote william Shakespeare, the world's \ngreatest dramatist.\"  Shakespeare went on to say that all of us are actors \non this stage.  We are, indeed, actors in the great drama called history, \nwhich is written by the deeds of mankind.  The same is true of this most \nimoportant of all dramas-the one played by mankind.  You were born in the \nmidst of the \"play.\"  Much went on before you stepped into the present \nscene.  In order, therefore, to play your part intelligently and well, you \nmust know what men have been doing up to your time."}, {"title": "英文 T2", "content": "     Precisely what Dunkel wants to avoid is any modification of his large, \ncarefully balanced package, which proposes new rules governing trade in \nservices and high technology, intellectual property, dumping and the \nsettlement of trade disputes.  At apress conference Friday, the director \ngeneral warned negotiators they could not \"pick and choose\" from his text \nwithout unraveling the whole package.  But the EC seemed intent on reopen-\ning the agriculture chapter, no matter what the risks.  Early Saturday EC \nministers repeated their earlier objections to the Dunkel paper.  They \nordered their negotiators to bargain for \"substantial amendments and \nnecessary modifications\" in the draft.\n     In the early 1950s, party telephone lines were common in homes across \nAmerica, and even author James Michener shared his phone line with several \nfamilies in rural Bucks County, Pennsylvania.  One day, publisher Bennett \nCerf of Random House called Michener to discuss a complicated book contract \nthat Cerf hoped Michener would sign.  Cerf said that he needed a yes or no \nanswer immediately and proceeded to explain the details.  Michener listen-\ned, and then asked several questions.  Cerf followed with additional expla-\nnation and finally asked for Michener's answer.  Michener paused because he \ndid not fully understand the contract yet.  But after several seconds his \nsilence was interrupted by a third, unknown party, who whispered, \"michen-\ner, you're a fool if you don't say yes.\"\n     Suppose you were an actor in an infinitely lesser drama, a play writ-\nten by one man or woman.  Let us suppose also that you appeared on the \nstage for the first time in the middle of the second or third ace.  In that \ncase you would be well aware of the fact that to do a good job of acting, \nyou would need to know what other performers who had appeared on the stage \nin earlier scenes had said and done.  In other words, you would need to \nknow the story up to the time of your entrance.  The same is true of this \nmost important of all dramas-the one played by mankind.  You were born in \nthe midst of the \"play.\"  Much went on before you stepped into the present \nscene.  In order, therefore, to play your part intelligently and well, you \nmust know what men have been doing up to your time.\n     Such theaters (in which the talented young actors and actresses who \nentertain guests after dinner also serve as waiters and waitresses) have \nbecome increasingly popular throughout the country.  Recently, visitors to \nBoulder from Europe attended the theater and said, \"This is something we do \nnot have in our country.  It simply could not be!  In our country actors \nfeel above waiters, and waiters would not stop to do something like clean-\ning streets.  When we go home and tell people that the actors and dancers \nare the same people who bring your dinner to your table.\n     Many observers have commented on what seems to be the fact that fear \nplays a much smaller part than we should think it must in the life of an \nanimal which lives dangerously.  Terror he can know, and perhaps he knows \nit frequently.  But it seems to last only a little longer than the immedi-\nate danger it helps him to avoid, instead of lingering, as in the human \nbeing it does until it becomes a burden and a threat.  The frightened bird \nresumes his song as soon as danger has passed and so does the frightened \nrabbit his games.  It is almost as if they knew that \"cowards die many \ntimes before their deaths'the brave never taste of death but once.\"\n     I am a 17-year-old girl, and will graduate from senior high school \nnext year.  It has come time for me to make a decision about entering a \nhigher level school.  I am interested in foreign languages and crave for \nstudying in either America or Canada.  However, my mother is against it \nbecause the tuition is too great an amount for my family.  I have a part-\ntime job at a local convenience store to help save for school expenses. \nBut the tuition is just too much of a burden for my family.  I am helpless, \nfor I cannot decide what to do.  If you were me, what would you do, study \nin Taiwan or America?\n     In fact, if I were determined to be disapproving of the Messrs.  Cerf \nand Cartmell, one of my chief criticisms would be that they had included \ntoo few Irish plays in their collection.  It is my dogmatic opinion that \nSynge's Riders to the Sea is not only the finest drama in this volume, but \nis the most eloquently powerful one-act play that has ever been written.  \nLady Gergory's The Rising of the Moon is a delightful little play and I am \nglad that it has been included, but I wish that space could likewise have \nbeen found for the selfsame Lady Gregoty's The Gaol Gate and possibly for \nher Spareading the News; certainly for W.B. Yeats's stirring Cathleen in \nHoulihan.  I am a bit dubious about including St. John Ervine among the \nIrish playwrights, but I wish there had been room for his short work called \nThe Magnanimous Lover.\n     My name is Joe King and I am from Texas.  I have live in Taiwan for \none year.  Teaching English is my job.  After one year's teaching, I would \nlike to tell you something about English learning.  I know English is hard \nfor you to learn, but you'll enjoy learning the language when you become \ninterested in it.  However, if you really want to learn it well, you have \nto practice every day.  What I mean is that you have to spend much time on \nit and have to study hard.  Don't give up.  Try hard.  I believe you'll \nlearn it.\n     Bill was studying at a college.  Like most students, he didn't have \nmuch money.  His mother's birthday was coming soon, and he planned to buy \nher a present that should be nice, but not too expensive.  He went shoppp-\ning in a department store and bought a beautiful umbrella.  On his way back \nhome, he walked by a pond and saw may children playing in the water.  Su-\nddenly, he heard a strange sound from the pond and something caught his \neye.  A child was trying hard to get out of the water, but he could not.  \nBill thought of the umbrella and asked the child to hold it.  Finally, the \nchild was out of the water.  Bill felt quite happy and thought that his um-\nbrella was really a good choice.\n     There's no doubt that the Eastern commonwealth's road to a mutually \nsatisfying and stable union will be a much rougher one than that taken by \nits West European counterpart.  But in spite of the obvious obstacles, it \nmay not lag far behind.  Though they lack the strong market and mature de-\nmocracy of the West, the former Soviet republics have other advantages the \nEuropeans lack, such as a common language, integrated transportation, edu-\ncational and energy systems and unified industrial standards.  These are \nall reasons to believe that after the members of the Eastern commonwealth \npass the present stage of nationalist fever and economic turmoil, their \npace of integration will speed up.\n     Don't treat me cruelly, Moo Moo.  You don't understand me.  No man \never really understands a woman.  There are terrible depths to my nature.  \nI had a long talk with Dr. Esculapius only last week, and he told me I'm \ntoo introspective.  It's the curse of us emotional women.  I'm really quite \nworried, but much you care, much you care.  (A note of tears comes into her \nvoice) I'm sure you don't love me any more, Moo Moo.  No!  No!  Don't an-\nswer me!  If you did you couldn't speak to me the way you do.  I've never \nwronged you in deed or in thought.  No, never-never.  I've given up my \nhopes and aspirations, because I knew you wanted me around you.  And now.  \nNOW-(she can contain the tears no longer) Because I have neglected my \nbeauty and because I am old and ugly, you regret that Ulysses of Agamemnon \ndidn't marry me when you all wanted me, and I know you curse the day you \never saw me.  (She is breathless.)\n     As the proverb says, \"the voice of the people is the voice of God.\"  \nBut this proverb is by no means invariably true.  In any case, what it \nreally means is, I think, that the feeling or instinct of the people is \nusually right, if it is spontaneous.  On the other hand, the opinions of \nthe people at large especially when artificially stimulated by mass media \nare usually shallow and mistaken.  Here, it seems to me, is the greatest \ndanger of the democratic form of government:  in that important issues are \noften decided by popular prejudice, based on insufficient knowledge and de-\nficient judgment.  Whereas the opinion of a few persons, possessed of de-\ntailed knowledge and sound judgment, is surely far more reliable.  This is \na major reason for the aristocratic form of government."}, {"title": "英文 T3", "content": "     Sunday, for my part, is a precious period of time.  To be more accur-\nate, it is a luxury in that I am now in the last year of my college life \n-- my weekdays are occupied with a heavy workload.  Only on Sundays can I \nhave a change of pace and embark upon what I like to do.  I am an intro-\nvert.  Unlike many others who enjoy such outdoor activities as mountain-\nclimbing and swimming, I prefer to stay home listening to some lyrical mu-\nsic to ward off those troubling thoughts.  Once in a while, I will browse \nthrough a good novel should I have one at hand.  I take to translation.  If \nI happen to have read a well-written essay, I will render it into English.  \nI am quite satisfied with the way I arrange my Sundays.  It refreshes my \nenergy and enables me to face the challenge of another busy week with joy.\n     It is curious that, while swimming comes natural to most animals, men \nhave to learn to swim.  Such animals as dogs, horses, deer, and cattle can \nswim when they first go into the water, but a man will sink unless he has \nlearned the art.  And, when we consider how necessary it may be to most \npeople at some time in their lives to know how to swim, it is still more \ncurious that comparatively few people take the trouble to learn the art.  \nIt is even said that many sailors and fishermen, who are in daily risk of \ndrowning, are not swimmers.\n     It is no secret that I am not one of those naturalists who suffer \nfrom cities, or affect to do so; nor do I find a city unnatural or un-\ninteresting, or a rubbish heap of follies.  It has always seemed to me that \nthere is something more than mechanically admirable about a train that \narrives on time, a fire department that comes when you call it, a light \nthat health of a penniless man and provide for him the agencies of mercy, \nthe X ray, the precious radium, the anesthetics and the surgical skill.  \nFor, beyond any pay these services receive, there stands out the pride in \nperfect performance.  And above all, I admire the noble impersonality of \ncivilization that does not inquire where the recipient stands on religion \nor politics or race.  I call this beauty, and I call it spirit--not some \nmystical soulfulness that nobody can define, but the spirit of man, that \nhas been growing a million years.\n     At the end of 1989, the world saw the downfall of Communist govern-\nments in Eastern Europe.  Now people are watching the Soviet Union.  Could \nthe same thing happen there?  To many people, it seems unlikely.  Eastern \nEurope became Communist in the 1940's.  By contrast, the Soviet system is \nover 70 years old.  Few people in the Soviet Union even remember the days \nbefore Communism.  In 1917, Vladimir Lenin and the Communists seize power.  \nThey believed that capitalism was bad.  Workers were not treated well.  Too \nmany people were poor and illiterate.  If the people themselve owned every-\nthing, their lives would be better.  The Communists claimed to represent \nall the people.  They took control of banks, factories and schools.  Since \nthen, the Communist Party had controlled almost every part of the people's \nlives.\n     Clothmaking was greatly increased by the invention of a machine to \nremove the seed from the cotton which grew abundantly in the southern area \nof the United States.  This machine was the work of Eli Whitney, a young \nman from New England who had gone south to teach school.  While visiting a \nplantation, he heard a good deal of talk about the cost of separating co-\ntton fiber from the seed.  He was told that a man, working all day, could \nseparate only a pound of cotton from the seed.  Both Georgia and South Ca-\nrolina had offered a prize for a machine that could do the work, but no \none had claimed it.  Whitney became so much interested in the problem that \nthe quit teaching and put all his time into the effort of inventing a co-\ntton gin.  In 1793 he completed a machine with which a man could clean fif-\nty pounds of cotton a day.  He wrote his father proudly:  it makes the la-\nbor fifty times less without throwing any class of people out or business.  \nHe continued no work on his machine, improving it so that it would clean \nmore and more cotton.  But his hopes of making a great fortune with the co-\ntton gin were never realize because many people, after learning how, simply \nbuilt gins and paid Whitney nothing for his invention.\n     The New York outposts of the Japanese firms have been among the \nbiggest beneficiaries of that time-honored back-scratching formula.  As \nTokuro Isoda, president of Daiwa Securities America, Inc., notes, for years \ntheir main mission was to sell Japanese stocks to American institutional \ninvestors and U.S. stocks to the Japanese.  The American side of the \nequation was a pretty hard sell, partly because the depositary receipt \nmarket in some Japanese stocks here tends to be thin.\n     It's halfway through 1990.  Has your English improved?  Your teachers \nat Studio Classroom hope so!  If your English hasn't improved as much as \nyou would like, listen to \"Running the Language Marathon.\"  Maybe you can \nget some new ideas.  Does your pronunciation need help?  We give you some \nuseful tips in \"How to Improve Your Pronunciation.\"  Each month we try to \nhive you many interesting topics to study.  This month is no exception.  \nVisit the landmarks of the world with Victor in Basic, or travel with your \nAdvanced teachers to Antarctica.  It's the cool place to be.  Speaking of \nchanges, there's a big surprise for you in next month's Studio Classroom.  \nBeginning in July, Studio Classroom is going to be completely in color!  We \nhope you will enjoy all the beautiful pictures.  Let us know how you like \nour new look.\n     Their priests noticed that when a very bright star rose above the \nhorizon concurrently with the sun, they could expect the river to overflow \nwithin a matter of days.  The importance of the Nile in their lives led the \npriests to study more carefully the motions of the sun.  The pharaoh Ptole-\nmy ordered astronomical tables compiled, which were used until well into \nthe Middle Ages.\n     The precinct itself was painted in what appears a standard apple green \nno Big Apple, a very flaked and faded apple thought by experts from the \nMetropolitan Museum to have been applied circa 1924.  A policeman is on \npermanent guard outside because of the increase in terrorist attacks.  \nInside the blinds are drawn permanently on account of the habit of \nunfriendly neighbors in the apartments across the vacant lot of taking \npot shots.  Around the corner were, at a conservative estimate, a hundred \npushers:  this district has the worst drug problem in the city.  The \noperators move from one abandoned building to another.  There are enough to \nlast for years:  the controlled rent policy has brought about landlord \nneglect and the terminal spiral of building decay insultingly camouflaged \nby the city's new policy of popping up gaily painted blinds over the \nwindowless holes in the walls.\n     Bellow's life journey has left its physical and spiritual mark.  His \nwispy hair is white.  His handsome, almost feminine face, with its promi-\nnent nose and deep-set brown eyes, is marked by crow's feet and creases.  \nPouches of flesh hang below his chin.  He thinks and writes frequently of \nmortality nowadays, since the death of his cousin Louis Gameroff several \nyears ago and the critical illness last winter of Louis' younger brother \nMeyer, Bellow's last surviving cousin from Lachine.  Yet when Bellow \nlaughs, as he does often a hearty, joyous guffaw, back arched, head thrown \nback, the boyish gap between his front teeth exposed-the decades seem to \ndrop away, swept off in a wave of infectious exuberance and vitality.\n     Gifted children often look at things differently from the average \nchild.  Many like to spend time alone or just \"daydreaming.\"  Unless your \nchild is unhappy, try not to worry too much about the ways in which she or \nhe is different.  Precocious youngsters also often find themselves in a \nconflict between the approval they receive for their accomplishments and \nthe disapproval they feel for being different.  Help your children work \nthis through by letting them know of your pride in their accomplishments \nand of your love for them as individuals."}, {"title": "英文 T4", "content": "     Thanksgiving dates back to the days of the early New England colonists \nwho were having difficulty living in the New Land.  According to the le-\ngend, without the help of the native American Indians, the settlers would \nhave perished.  The Indians showed the colonists how to hunt wild turkeys \nand how to grow corn.  In the fall, the Indians and colonists gathered for \na feast to honor their friendship.  In reality, the colonists and Indians \nnever got along very well, and this first Thanksgiving dinner probably \nnever took place.  Even so, Americans today follow the tradition by invit-\ning friends and relatives for a turkey dinner on the fourth Thursday of \nNovember.\n     I live in a small country community which is very far away from the \nmain street of the village.  The junior high school which I went to is 10 \nkilometers away from my home.  There were only two scheduled trips of bus \nservices-one in the early morning and one in the afternoon-only to carry \ncommuter students to and from school along the route.  I was the only one \nin my community who went to school by bicycle.  My father wanted me to \ntake more exercise.  One car passed by me.  It was the commuter bus.  Of \ncourse it wouldn't stop to pick me up.  Those on the bus seemed to be very \ncomfortable.  How lucky they were, I thought.  Unexpectedly, about half an \nhour later, my mother came by motorcycle to help me.  It so happened that a \ngirl classmate of mine who had been on the bus recognized me.  As soon as \nshe got home, she telephoned my mother to tell her about my situation.\n     The early history of the human race is most clearly and readily \nstudied by observing the a stages of human technological progress.  That is \nwhy it was a long-standing convention to divide early history into broad \nperiods according to the hard materials-stone, copper, bronze, and iron, in \nthat order-from which cutting tools and weapons were made.  Then, as know-\nledge of the past increased, the broad periods were further subdivided.  \nThe Stone Age was divided into the Old period and the New period when it \nwas noticed that the technique of sharpening flint and other stones changed \nfrom flaking to grinding.\n     During early Roman history all physicians were either slaves or \nrepresentatives of lower Roman society.  Medicine was a Greek science, and \nmany Greek physicians, attracted by the prospect of great profit at the \ncapital of the empire, migrated to Rome to establish their practice.  As a \nconsequence, many doctors were foreigners, and as such were considered in a \nvery low position by the people of high social rank.  Frequently, a wealthy \nRoman supplied one of his slaves with a medical education for the sake of \ncovenience.  Having one's own physician was obviously an advantage not to \nbe overlooked, and slaves who had a knowledge of the healing art commanded \nthe highest prices in the Roman slave market.  Recognizing the importance \nof the medical profession, however, Julius Caesar conferred citizenship on \nall who practiced medicine at Rome to make them more desirous of living in \nthe city, and to induce others to come to it.  Despite this encouragement, \nmedicine never came to be considered the proper profession for the upper \nclasses.\n     Today, Taiwanese society has evolved to become one of Asia's booming \ncenters of trade, business, and industrialization.  As such, it is cons-\ntantly immersed in matters of a profit-oriented, commercial society.  And \nthis phenomenon has drastically altered the character of missionary work.  \nFor whereas before it was enough for missionaries to be priests or relig-\nious engaging the work of God's kingdom through catechism, administering \nthe sacraments, and other spiritual works, today's missionaries have to \nto learn to \"incarnate\" the Word of God even in the technical books of \nmodern science and contemporary psychology, in systems analysis and \ncomputer programs, in mass media, in business contracts and legal systems, \netc.  Even the local government has applied stricter regulations for allow-\ning missionaries into Taiwan, with preference given to those holding \ndoctorate degrees.  In other words, even missionary work is becoming more \nand more specialized.  So we ask, and shat them of the good old missionary \nactivity of talking directly to people about God, or of sharing one's \nexperience of Christ in his or her life as St. Paul did?  The ecclesiasti-\ncal scenario is tense; we walk on this tightrope of change.\n     English is the most widely spoken language in the world today, (which \nis) a fact that everyone knows.  Whatever one does, he cannot do without \nknowing something about English.  A good command spoken English enables us \nto travel around the world without difficulties.  If we can read English, \nwe may expose ourselves to the latest information about technology, cul-\nture, and so on, which can enrich our knowledge.  There are so many advan-\ntages about learning English that we should not miss any opportunity to \nmaster it.  It goes without saying that what we do today has much to do \nwith a bright future.  To put it differently, yesterday is gone, tomorrow \nis yet to come, and today is the only time within our control.\n     The background of the episodes, a strike meeting, is not an excuse.  \nEach of the committeemen shows in his episode the crucial moment of his \nlife which brought him to this very platform.  The dramatic structure on \nwhich the play has been built is simple but highly effective.  The form \nused is the old black-face minstrel form of chorus, end men, specialty men \nand interlocutor.  The strike committee on the platform during the acting \nout of the play let should be used as chorus.  Emotional, polictical, mu-\nsical, they have in them possibilities of various comments on the scenes.  \nThis has been indicated once in the script in the place where Joe's wife is \nabout to leave him.  In the climaxes of each scene, slogans might very \neffectively be used a voice coming out of the dark.  Such a voice might \nannounce at the appropriate moments in the \"Young Interme's\" scene that the \nUSSR is the only country in the world where Anti-Semitism is a crime again-\nst the State.  Don't hesitate to use music wherever possible.  It is very \nvaluable in emotionally stirring an audience.\n     The most amazing thing about crack cocaine is that it did not begin \nrotting America's urban landscape sooner.  It has been recognized as a \nscourge in cities-and none too few suburban and rural areas-for only five \nyears.  But the supercharged cocaine, sometimes called \"rock,\" wasn't real-\nly new.  References to the recipe that used heat and baking soda to turn \ncocaine hydrochloride, or powder, into the smokable form of freebase called \ncrack appear throughout the 1970's in underground literature, media inter-\nviews and congressional testimony.  It did not catch on back then, resear-\nchers believe, because it was not as pure as other, more processed forms \nof freebase.  Freebasers, who fancied themselves connoisseurs in those by-\ngone days, called it \"garbage rock.\"\n     The working group's recommendations, which were presented to an OSTP \nadvisory committee on 30 November, are described by one university official \nwho has seen them as \"an ingenious piece of bureaucratic writing that will \nalmost certainly solve the problem.\"  In a brief open session of the OSTP \ncommittee meeting, Pettifor said that the regulations would exempt from the \nlicense requirements information that is publicly available in books, \nscientific journals, and conference proceedings; fundamental research, \nwhich is defined to include virtually all university research; educational \nmaterials' and patent applications."}, {"title": "英文 T5", "content": "     I sit a mutated tropical bird?  A monstrous preying insect?  A \nshuttle craft at rest on its launching pad?  A sculpture derived from a \nfuturistic scrap yard?  A giant-sized plough, threatening to gobble up the \nbuildings opposite, and everything beyond in its path?  Or is it simply \nthat the aliens have landed... is this an unnatural encounter of the \nthird kind?  Perhaps when all is said and done, it is someone's idea of a \npractical joke.  After all, it was unwrapped to the public gaze on April 1 \nlast year, as if deliberately daring to make a fool of everyone.  Archit-\nect Makoto Sei Watanabe (he added the unusual middle name because his best \nfriend was also Makoto Watanabe and everyone was always getting them mixed \nup) smilingly shrugs off these and the many other visual associations that \nhave been made of his controversial masterpiece, the Aoyama Technical \nCollege in central Tokyo.\n     \"It's supposed to be ambiguous,\" he explained in surprisingly good \nEnglish, considering he only studied at university and has never worked \noutside Japan (though he'd like to.)  \"It's full of metaphors.  The norm \nthese days is what I call 'architecture by limitations'; architects are \nsupposed to work within an approved set of rules.  Well, I set out to \ndeliberately break every one in the book.\"  At the end of 1988, he noted \nin a specialist publication that the college was holding an international \ncontest for the design of its new headquarters.  Of the 87 entries, it was \nWatanabe's design on which the judges' eyes focused.  A trio of Austra-\nlian architects, who ran second, designed the new Australian Embassy which \nrecently opened in Tokyo.  The entrance is on the second floor, with four \nlevels of classrooms and lecture halls.  The top storey--the area that \nelicits the most comment--is a gallery.  The dome on the roof?  A water \ntower.  The scarlet rods, like the handle of a lawn mower?  Lightning \nconductors.  It's constructed from aluminum and stainless steel, in three \nfinishes, plus a plethora of both bold and subtle colour.\n     Watanabe gave himself enormous problems turning his vision into a \npractical reality.  To reproduce anything in 3-D is difficult, and \nelevations and plans communicate little.  A model can give an impression, \nhe says, but is still not precise.  And precision is the main element in \nthis unique building.  A second difficulty was that since every part and \ndetail was original, he could not fall back onto mass production.  Japan \nhas got the erection of a lot of its buildings off to a fine art--or \nrather onto the factory production line.  Making basic components that can \nthen be slotted together in a large but still limited number of permuta-\ntions helps keep costs down and maintains, in this society at least, a \ncomforting conformity.  But Watanabe claims he was able to custom-order \nall the parts he needed at reasonable cost; his building hasn't been that \nmuch more expensive and is so much more rewarding.  After all, it makes \npeople think.\n     \"I used the computer to work out all the elements, from the compo-\nnents of the basic structure, to all the exterior and interior detail, and \neven the furniture.  The technique I utilised--computer graphics--is \nnot new, but it hasn't been explored in architecture very much to date.  \nThen I had to spread the work around to get what I wanted; the dome was \nmade in one prefecture, the metal panels in another.  I went to small \nhighly specialised companies to get the quality and finish I required.\"  \nThough appearing 100 per cent futuristic in its totality, Watanabe says \nthis is deceptive.  He deliberately incorporated traditional conventions \nin the lower levels of the building, while experimenting with ideas that \nhardly existed except in his imagination higher up.  He aimed to confront \nexisting architectural vocabulary with a total lack of cliche, allowing \nthe two concepts to meet, somewhere rather above the middle.  He purposely \nleft the structure open to interpretation, piling lots of ideas on top \nof another, like a modern-day Gaudi.  Or maybe as only Makoto Watanabe can \ndo.  Yet he aimed for logic in the overall design, and must have achieved \nhis desired effect, because he won the commission.  It took 3.5 months to \ndesign, then 10 months to build and furnish.\n     Ask him if he regards himself trendy architect, and he'll suddenly \nlook critical and rather severe.  This is quite a feat for a slight, \nfriendly but shy boyish-looking 38-year-old, but he acts out his response \nwell.  He hates trendy things.  A trend is when an idea has reached the \ncrest of a wave, from where it's creatively downhill all the way.  No, he \ninsists, this building is new--there's nothing like it anywhere else in \nthe world today.  It appears successful, too, having been featured in many \npublications, from the men's magazine Brutus, to an eight-page coverage in \nthat most illustrious of accolades The Japan Architect:  90, published by \nthe Japanese Institute of Architects.  Even more important, young people \nseem to like it.  After promotional posters appeared in trains and busses \nadvertising the fact that it was open for business, the college received \ndouble the number of applications to the year before.  The secretary to \nthe principal, however, dictated it was policy for staff not to discuss \ntheir feelings about the building; and that if I wanted a response from \nany students, I would have to fill out a form.  Such is bureaucracy in \nJapan.\n     Born in Yokohama in 1952, and a graduate of the Master Course in \nArchitecture at the National University in the city, Watanabe spent three \nyears working with Masato Ohtaka, and then five with Arata Isozaki, under \nwhom he worked on the Science Centre at Tsukuba.  He established his own \nthree-man practice, Architect's Office (03-829 3221, fax 3837) in 1984, \nand has since won any number of design awards.  \"A matter of keeping my \neyes open and ears flapping,\" he explained.  He always loved art--drawing, \npainting and sculpture--even as a child.  But he was drawn by science, \ntoo, so architecture seemed a natural route to choose at 17 or so.  He \nstill favors the surrealists, like Dali and Magritte (which seems in \norder somehow) yet claims to be most influenced by Italian Renaissance and \nBaroque architects like Bernini and Borromini.\n     Ask where in the world today architecture is generally the most \ninteresting and adventurous and he'll smilingly not hesitate a second:  To-\nkyo, of course.  \"Am I critical of other projects?  Well, naturally.  But \nI'm never rude and judge my words carefully.  It's a small world after \nall, and human relationships are all-important in Japan.  \"The Sprial \nBuilding where we are now sitting, in Omoto-sando?  I often work here with \nartists.  I find it very calm, very quiet.  \"The Asahi Beer building in \nAskausa?  Interesting.  I used to work near the site.  Personally, I'm not \nwild about it and feel no great sympathy.  But I do understand the \narchitects' ways of thinking."}, {"title": "英文 T6", "content": "     Insurance executive Clement Cho Kwok-yin is one of a small but growing \ngroup of Hong Kong residents who have chosen to live on a boat.  He \nswapped his comfortable family apartment for a cramped home afloat because \nhe loves the tranquility and freedom of marina living.  \"I love boats and \nI would need to have a multi-million-dollar house in a prime spot to get \nviews like these,\" he says, sweeping a sun-tanned arm towards the \ncolourful flotilla tied up at Aberdeen Marina on the southern side of Hong \nKong island.  \"Boating is great for my wife and children, who come over \nseveral times a year from New Zealand where they are fulfilling residency \nrequirements.  We love to go cruising around the islands.  I give my two \nsons important jobs in advance of a cruise, such as preparing the safety \nequipment, and that's how they learn how to take care of themselves.\n     \"At the same time, I can keep fit by doing regular boat maintenance.\"  \nCho regrets that Hong Kong waters are \"a bit polluted, especially during \nthe summer.\"  That aside, however, \"there is no better life-style than \nboating.  I can recommend it to everybody,\" he says.  Clement Cho is among \na new wave of sea-faring Asians.  Buoyed by the tide of wealth in the \nregion, the nouveaux riches of Asia have developed a taste for brisk winds \nand salt spray.  Where the Bahamas and the Costa del Sol were names that \ntripped off the tongue in the boating world, Hong Kong, Singapore, Phuket, \nLangkawi, Puerto Galera and Tokyo have become the latest buzzwords in the \ncruising vocabulary.\n     And the images flashed around the world of US President Bill Cinton \nyachting with Jackie Onassis can only boost the appeal of the sport to \nmore and more Asians--as a symbol of money and power.  Sailing is now \nseen as the choice pursuit of people who have arrived.  Further enhancing \nthe region's boating reputation is the growth of races such as the Tokyo \nCup, Osaka-Melbourne, Sydney-Hobart, Hong Kong-Macau, Pedro Blanco, the \nChina Sea Race and Thailand's King's Cup Regatta.  But it didn't just \nhappen overnight.  From the days of the Bugis people's migration from \nSulawesi to Selangor and then to Riau, the Acheh sultanate of Sumatra, the \nhistoric voy-ages of the Chinese admiral Cheng Ho and the Spanish armada's \nsweep through the Philip-pines, the China coast and island-studded waters \nof Southeast Asia have been busy sea lanes.\n     Love of the sea runs all the way from the humblest sampan fisherman \nto kings and em-perors.  A number of Asian royals, including the Crown \nPrince of Johore, are enthusiastic sailors.  For instance, King Bhumibol \nAdulyadej of Thailand won the OK Dinghy class gold medal in the 1967 \nSoutheast Asian Peninsular Games.  And over the years, Asian sailors have \npulled off some incredible maritime feats.  In 1974, Japanese yachtsman \nYoh Aoki sailed the tiny 20ft Ahodon III around the world, becoming the \n37th person to make a solo voyageround the world since American Joshua \nSlocum first did it in 1898.  Aoki was followed a few months later by \nfellow Japanese Ruysuke Ushijima in Gin Gitsune and Kenichi Horie in \nMermaid II, completing the 40th and 41st solo circumnavigations.\n     Japan also cast a large wind shadow during the last America's Cup \nwhen the 12-metre Nippon gave the competitors a bit of a sumo shake.  To \nput more wind in their sails, Yamaha, the biggest boat-builder in the Land \nof the Rising Sun, is gearing up to sponsor a Japanese entry in the \nupcoming Whitbread around-the-world yacht race.\n     Hong Kong, meanwhile, will host an around-the-world challenge race in \n1997, just before the Fragrant Harbour returns to Chinese rule (see box \nthis page).  And it's not just Asians, but Asian concepts that are setting \nthe seas on fire.  British adventurer Tim Severin is crossing the Pacific \nOcean on a bamboo raft to prove that Chinese sailors may have \nsuccessfully sailed from Asia to America, 1,700 years ago.  And Hong \nKong-based physician Dr Wayne Moran hopes to retrace Marco Polo's voyage \nto Europe on the replica deepsea junk Cocachin.\n     Taking on a more modern challenge, British millionaire-adventurer \nRichard Branson hopes to break the offshore powerboat speed record \nbe-tween Port Klang in Malaysia and the holiday island of Langkawi.  \nMeanwhile, Malaysia and Thailand are turning into a pleasure boat paradise \nand hope to become to Asia what the Caribbean is to America--a \nwarm-water playground.  To bolster this effort, Kuala Lumpur has dropped \nthe import duty of up to 35 per cent on boats under 26 tonnes, and is \nwooing Euro-pean and American boat-builders with gener-ous incentives.  \nTourism minister Datuk Sabbaruddin Chik said Malaysia might also get large \ncharter yachts currently cruising the Mediterranean and the Caribbean to \nestablish their base in the country.\n     Brooke Yachts, a UK-based company which has supplied boats to \nHollywood stars and the Saudiroyalfamily, is rumoured to be setting up \nshop in Johore.  According to marina development con-sultant Muhaimi \nMustapha, Malaysia accounts for nearly one third of the $3 billion marina \nindustry in Southeast Asia, with at least six operating by the end of this \nyear and about 20 within the next five years.\n     Thailand, in particular Phuket, is also get-ting an international \ncruising reputation even though the government has imposed a high import \nduty (35 per cent) on some yachts and spare parts.  Three fully-fledged \nnew marinas are planned for the next three to five years.  Phuket's fa-mous \nKing's Cup Regatta is based on the popular Antigua Week in the Caribbean, \na family-styleevent.  Meanwhile, in Singapore, proof that boating is one \nof the Lion City's fastest growing lei-sure activities is evident in the \nincreasing number of boat owners.  According to the Port of Singapore \nAuthority, the number of licensed pleasure craft has swelled from 4,096 \nthree years ago to 4,945 in December last year.  Malaysia, by comparison, \nhas about 3,000 boat owners.  Meanwhile, the Philippines has nowhere to go \nbut up.  The nation of more than 7,000 islands, with idyllic boltholes \nsuch as Boracay, Puerto Galera in Mindoro and Puerto Princesa in Palawan, \nis becoming an island-hoppers' paradise.  Many avid yachtsmen are starting \nto cruise the Philippines fulltime rather than sail on to other parts of \nthe world.  But the world's fastest-growing yachting nation of all is \nJapan.  According to the Japan Marina Association, there are 450 marinas \ndotted around the country's 34,000-kilometre coastline, with 62 more under \nconstruction.  Nearly a quarter of a million Japanese own boats, which \noccupy about 80 per cent of available marina space; in Tokyo and Osaka, \nthe figure is zooming up to 90 per cent.\n     In the Philippines, although a few beach resorts have put up their \nown small marinas and yacht clubs, the Manila Yacht Club, which was formed \nby five expatriate yachtsmen in 1927, remains the nation's largest."}, {"title": "英文 T7", "content": "     NAME THARMALINGAM HAS BEEN leading parties of bird-watchers and \nnature enthusiasts to Taman Negara for almost 14 years.  He is \nco-director of Kingfisher Tours, a wildlife tour company based in \nKuala Lumpur.  Most of his clients are well-heeled Westerners, but \nthere is an increasing number of locals.\n     The passions that bird-watching can rouse never fail to amaze him \n-- he has known dedicated \"birdies\" to charter helicopters just to get \ninto remote regions where a single rare species might exist.\n     \"For me, Taman Negara's importance is as one of the premier bird-\nwatching spots in Malaysia.  There are 350 species within its boundaries \n-- one of the highest counts in the world.  \"Of course, there are many \ninteresting animals, too, and these days, with rainforests such as you \nfind in Taman Negara disappearing fast, the park is their best hope of \nsurvival.\n     \"It's very sad that several of the bird species I've seen over the \nyear are now probably extinct, or very close to it.  \"When I take people \naround the park, I tell them that this is not a tourist spectacle but \nthe real virgin forest, exactly as it is.  Their reactions are a bonus \nfor me--complete fascination and excitement at the diversity and variety \nof life forms.\"\n     THE 4,343 SQUARE KILOMETRES OF the park are criss-brossed by trails \nthat all eventually lead back to park headquarters at Kuala Tahan.  Some \ntake just half an hour, while others require more effort.  For example, \nthe round trip to the summit of Gunung Tahan, Malaysia's highest peak, \ntakes eight days and is suitable only for the fit and hardy.\n     In between these extremes are distances to suit all physiques, though \nthe going is invariably hot and sticky--humidity rarely drops below 90 \npercent.  In fact, the air is so perpetually damp that it hardly seems \nworth carrying waterproofs.\n     A great advantage of Taman Negara is that almost as much wildlife \ncan be seen close to Kuala Tahan as in the more remote areas.  The last \nsighting of a tiger was on the path by the Tahan River, just 30 minute's \nwalk from headquarters.\n     A further plus is that wild animals, like most humans, prefer to lead \norderly lives, which is quite convenient if you want to see them.  The \nflying squirrel leaps from the same tree at the same time every night and \na certain herd of elephants returns every two months to its favourite \ncoconut palms because that is how long it takes the plants to recover from \nbeing dined upon.  The park rangers, in particular, are a mine of such \ninside information.  Want to see a scorpion?  There's a certain tree \nwhere one likes to sun himself.  A slow Loris?  Halfway up Teresek Hill \nat about ll pm.  Flying Foxes?  Behind chalet nine at dusk.\n     However, in the end, quite what is seen in the park depends very \nmuch on how you search.  One man's park is full of birds and animals, \nanother's is not.  The Wildlife Department guide to Taman Negara lets \nthe visitor in on a simple secret--walk slowly and silently.  There are \nno red lights to beat or deadlines to meet in the jungle, so relax and \nlinger--what could take two hours should take four.\n     Accessible from Kuala Tahan are a number of hides located near \nsalt-licks.  These vantage points open up an extra dimension-the \nrainforest by night.  Sambar deer, barking deer and tapir are seen \noften, while elephants, tigers and sun bears are less common.\n     To view the animals a powerful flash-light is needed--they \ncan be hired from headquarters.  A good pair of binoculars is also useful \nas the hides are generally quite a distance above the salt-lick.  The \nsame flashlight is handy for night walks and cave exploration.  There \nare a number of the latter in the park:  each has its own character and \nall have lots of bats.\n     Caves are certainly not to everyone's taste but there is a great \nsense of achieve-ment to be had when you make it through the \"crawling\" \nsection of Gua Telinga cave--\"crawling\" because you do and it is--with \nvarious small creatures which this particular visitor did not hang \naround to examine closely.\n     A less-strenuous activity, and one everyone can enjoy, is a river \njourney.  Perhaps the most atmospheric is the two-hour ride up the \nTahan River to the rapids of Lata Berkoh.  The water is tea-coloured by \nnatural dyes and tannins and huge near trees provide shade for the fish \nto laze in.  This is a jungle river untouched by man.  A lesser fish \neagle-eyes your boat warily from his favourite perch; a stork-billed \nkingfish-er cries a raucous warning and bolts down-stream while a monitor \nlizard slips quietly into the water.\n     The rapids themselves are lovely, and there is a camp site and a \nlodge should you want to stay the night.  The journey up the much larger \nRiver Tembeling is not quite so peaceful.  The fast-moving choppy waters \noften rise up and drench travellers--prepare to get wet.  But the trip \nis worth the soaking for it opens up a whole new area of the park.\n     There are fishing lodges that are rarely visited and some of the best \nbird-watching to be had.  The trails are less well-trodden and it's not \nunusual to spot tracks of tiger, elephant and, with more luck, the rare \nSu-matran rhino.  However, don't build up any hopes for a sighting of this \nhighly-secretive beast--no one has ever seen any of the estimated 15 to \n20 individuals that roam the park's more remote parts.\n     Much more easily found are the troops of dusky leaf monkeys.  Gibbons \nare plentiful, too, but are usually only heard--theircurious looping \ncries echoing all around but never so much as a shaking leaf to \nad-vertise their presence.  It is a wariness based on a healthy sense \nof self-preservation as the people of the rainforest, the Orang Asli, \nstill occasionally hunt them with blow pipes.  By the trails, tell-tale \ncuts in the rough bark of the era berths tree show where the hunters \nobtain the poison-ous sap for their lethal darts.\n     It is a reminder that this is not a zoo."}, {"title": "英文 T8", "content": "     At the height of the low-cost textiles crisis last year, a Singapore \nstatesman angrily suggested that the advanced nations should dismantle \ntheir textile industry and leave it entirely to the developing countries.  \nSure, a western wag said, but we could cripple them simply by denying \ndyestuffs.  For, without colour, there won't be fabrics; without fabrics, \nthere won't be fashion; and without fashion, there won't be dollars.  This \nmay be a highly cynical view, but it underlines how critical colour is to \nAsia's gigantic textile industry.\n     Yet, hardly any developing country in Asia, with the notable exception \nof India, Pakistan and Taiwan, manufactures dyes.  Even as Asia's textile \nindustry grew to incredible proportions during the sixties, its dyes imp-\norts nearly doubled from US$35 million to almost US$70 million a year.  \nThe wag who quipped about the importance of colour is forgotten, but the \npoint he made has certainly stung Asian planners.  Already, the Asian Ind-\nustrial Development Council (AIDC) has started preliminary studies to \nfind out whether the developing countries in this region can make a beg-\ninning in the production of dyestuffs.\n     With rare exceptions, almost all dyes in modern use are synthetic.  \nTill the advent of petrochemicals, they were popularly called coal tar \ndyes because they were synthesised from such hydrocarbons as benzene which \nwere derived directly from coal tar.  These hydrocarbons are converted in-\nto intermediates like aniline and naphthols, and then combined to form a \nfantastic range of colouring substances.  Consequently, the manufacture of \ndyestuffs is highly research-oriented and capital-intensive.\n     Monopoly.  Ironically enough, the production of dyestuffs is closely \nassociated with the production of explosives.  For this reason, this ind-\nustry enjoyed (and, in some ways, continues to enjoy) a strategic position \nin West Germany, the United Kingdom, the United States and Japan.  Before \nWorld War I, for example, Germany was an undisputed leader in the product-\nion of coal tar chemicals and of dyestuffs based on them.  She was also \nthe most important supplier of intermediates to other manufacturers.  But \nthe two World Wars which the Germans precipitated underlined their critical \ndependence on German supplies and spurred them on to greater research and \ngreater production.  In the early twenties, their total production was \nbarely a few thousand tons.  Thanks to sustained research and investment, \ntheir output soared during the sixties.  By 1968, they were manufacturing \nand marketing considerably more than they ever did:  some 115,000 tons in \nthe U.S., 78,000 tons in West Germany, 41,000 tons in Japan and 37,000 \ntons in the U.K.\n     This period also saw the emergence of minimal facilities in India, \nPakistan and Taiwan.  Typically, India produces over 5,000 tons of dyes-\ntuffs (mainly, sulphur black, vat, dyes and azo dyes), Pakistan 2,000 tons \n(mainly, direct dyes,) and Taiwan 1,500 tons.  The development of the dye-\nstuffs industry in any country (more strongly so in any developing country) \nfollows there stages:  first, production from imported intermediates; sec-\nond, production of intermediates; and, third, production of basic organic \nchemicals from which intermediates are derived.  Projects.  On the assum-\nption that few developing countries possess the requisite research facil-\nities, knowhow or capital, experts suggest that Asian nations must be mode-\nst in their initial ambitions.  The problem is, however, complicated by the \nfact that new plants must be versatile enough to produce as wide a range of \ndyes as possible--and thus give textile mills (and other colour users) the \namplitude of choice they require to survive.  Mindful of these consider-\nations, United Nations and industry experts have framed three possible \npilot projects of interest to intending investors.\n     The first project is designed to produce 750 tons of direct dyes, 400 \ntons of direct dyes, 400 tons of acid dyes, and 600 tons of sulphur black \nannually.  It calls for a total investment of US$4 million, some 65% of it \nin foreign exchange.  Its products will yield a gross turnover of US$2.7 \nmillion and (allowing for a tax holiday of five years) a net profit of \naround US$610,000.  Thus, the pay-out time will be about five years.  The \nsecond project is designed to manufacture 1,200 tons of naphthol dyes a \nyear.  This requires a local investment of US$1.2 million and a foreign \ncomponent of US$2.1 million.  With an estimated turnover of US$4.2 million \nand a profit of US$720,000, the payout time will be less than four years.\n     The third project aims at an annual output of 1,000 tons of organic \npigment dyes.  It will cost US$2.5 million (half of it in foreign exchange) \nto set up.  With gross sales of US$3.6 million and a net profit of \nUS$327,000, the pay-out time will be little more than four years.  For the \nmore daring, a fourth project offers a very promising sideline.  Designed \nto produce 250 tons of optical bleaches, this project envisages a modest \ninvestment of less than US$1 million, which can be recovered in three \nyears at most.\n     Market.  AIDC experts, who are screening these projects, say that the \nproduction of dyestuffs from imported intermediates could be profitable in \nthe developing countries of Asia.  An additional attraction is that all \nthese plants will not only provide a fairly steady and large supply of es-\nsential colours but also result in substantial foreign exchange savings \nthrough import substitution.  The latter advantage is based on a very rou-\ngh estimate of current levels of consumption.  For instance, Hong Kong \nimports over 3,500 tons a year, Taiwan and South Korea together import \nsome 2,500 tons, and the Philippines, Thailand and Indonesia together buy \naround 5,000 tons.  Of course, their production costs can be substantially \ntrimmed down if two or more countries are willing to enter into joint \nventures and thus enjoy economies of scale.\n     Admittedly, these projects can only underwrite very limited objec-\ntives:  to give Asian fabric and garment producers some facility of oper-\nation, and to provide their traditional dyers and printers a ready, modern \nmedium to work in.  It would be wholly imprudent to jump to the conclusion \nthat the developing countries can set up a vibrant dyestuffs industry or \nacquire the requisite technology on their own.  For, the evolution of \ncolouring substances, and dyes that adhere to the bewildering range of \nfibers in use now, is wholly the product of patient and prolonged re-\nsearch."}, {"title": "英文 T9", "content": "     War is a struggle that destroys a soldier's mind, thoughts, friends, \nand family.  Young, idealistic men patriotically go off to fight but learn \nquickly about the harsh reality of war.  Paul Baumer, the main character in \nAll Quiet on the Western Front has suffered the \"struggle\".  After ex-\nperiencing the error of war, Paul view of life changes not only does he \nfeels alienated from his family but also alone in a world of death and \ndestruction.  It is through this characterization of Paul who demonstrates \nhis theme the destructiveness.  The meaningless, useless war does no good \nto everyone who is alive.\n     Paul's thoughts change once he joins the military.  \"Killing each \nseparate louse is a tedious business when a man has hundreds\"(75).  When \nPaul is in the camp on the moors with the army, he often images about the \nwar and what it does to other people.  Killing innocent people is not too \ndifficult to the soldiers.  \"The little beasts are hard and the everlasting \ncracking with one's fingernails very soon becomes wearisome\"(77).  Paul \nrealizes that the \"beasts\" are the symbolic enemies who stand ready to \nattack at all time.  These enemy soldiers are compared to the everlasting \ncracking fingernails, which represent the crackings in their lives.  Each \nday Paul's feelings are changed as his friends die.  \"I am young, yet I \nknow nothing of life but despair, death, fear, and fatuous superficiality \ncast over an abyss of sorrow.  I see how peoples are set against are \nanother, and in silence, unknowingly, foolishly, obediently, innocently \nslay on another.  I see that the keenest brains of the world invent weapons \nand words to make it yet more refined and enduring\"(263). Paul's thoughts \nare getting more understandable than before, because he knows the darkness \nof the war; he totally understands the situation of a role in the miserable \nwar.  Once the war ends, all the soldiers will no longer have an occupa-\ntion; they can no longer be fit to do anything but a soldier.  Paul's \nthoughts have been destroyed by the war, and he cannot think about anything \nelse except despair, death, and fear.\n     The brutal war worries Paul whole family only the emptiness of separa-\ntion when he is gone.  \"It is the kitchen door that was opened, they are \ncooking potato-cakes, the house reeks of it, and to-day of course is \nSaturday; that will be my sister leaning over.  For a moment I am shy and \nlower my head, then I take off my helmet and look up.  Yes, it is my eldest \nsister(157).\"  Paul no longer fits into the family unit, his experiences at \nwar leave him shy and crazy for the normality of their lives.  \"She looks \nat me.  Her hands are white and sickly and frail compared with mine.  We \nsay very little and I am thankful that she asks nothing.  What ought I to \nsay?  Everything I could have wished for has happened.  I have come out of \nit safely and sit here beside her.  And in the kitchen stands my sister \npreparing supper and signing(159).\"  Paul has been waiting for this moment \nsince joining the military, now he can finally talk to his dear mother and \nbe gathering together harmony of his family.  However, the war has \nseparated Paul from his mother.  His pale, sick mother does not want to ask \nanything about the war but watches her son and hoping that he will come \nback alive.  \"Before my mother's tremulous anxiety I recover my composure.  \nNow I can walk about and talk and answer questions without fear of having \nsuddenly to lean against the wall because the world turns soft as rubber \nand my veins become brimstone(161-2).\"  Once Paul tells his mother that he \nis well, he does not have to worry about the rest of the conversations, \nbecause all he wants is make his mother happy and feel safe.  However, his \nattempts to ease his mother worries causes him to feel alienated; he has \nno one to share the horror of the war.  He is left to face it alone.\n     Paul will have a difficult time surviving without his comrades.  \n\"There I drop down on my knees, but have still enough strength to fall on \nto the side where Kat's sound leg is.  After a few minutes I straighten my-\nself up again.  My leg and my hands tremble.  I have trouble in finding my \nwater bottle, to take a pull.  My lips tremble as I try to think.  But I \nsmile-Kat is saved.\"  Paul is very nervous about Kat's wound, because \nhe does not want to lose his last friend in the army.  The only good thing \nto come out of this horror is the friendship.  \"On the way without my \nhaving notices it, Kat has caught a splinter in the head.  There is just \none little hole, it must have been a ver tiny, stray splinter.  But is has \nsufficed.  Kat is dead(290-1).\"  Kat's death makes Paul feel even more iso-\nlated.  Now, he is truly alone.  \"Do I walk?  Have I feet still?  I raise \nmy eyes, I let them move round, and turn myself with them, one circle, one \ncircle, and I stand in the midst.  All is as usual.  Only the Militiaman \nStanislaus Katczinsky has died(291).\"  The death of Katczinsky destroys the \nlast thing that is important to Paul rather than his family.  The war has \nleft him nothing.\n     A war determines the fate as if some 3-year-old children are playing \nsandcastles, so innocent, so easily to destroy. War destroys all that men \nhold dear. Paul Baumer learns to distrust all that he believed in; he fells \nalienated from his family; and ultimately he is left without hope.  The \ndestructiveness of war strips men of everything leaving them with nothing.\n     Four people-the Belgian bus driver, two women from the United States \nand a Canadian man-died at the scene, said a police spokesman in nearby \nScheifling.  An American man died later in the hospital, he added, speak-\ning on condition of anonymity."}, {"title": "英文 T10", "content": "     President Lee Teng-hei and his entourage arrived in Pretoria last \nnight for the upcoming inauguration of South Africa's first black \npresident, Nelson Mandela.  Lee took a China Airlines chartered flight to \nSouth Africa, on the third and probably most-important leg of his global \ntrip, following a five-day visit to Nicaragua and Costa Rica in Central \nAmerica.  Lee's presence at Mandela's swearing-in ceremony highlights the \n71 year-old ROC president's aggressive efforts to promote Taiwan's presence \nin the international arena.  Official delegations from more than 100 coun-\ntries will attend the presidential inauguration ceremony of South Aftrica.  \nLee's presence indicates Taipei's Temporary victoiry in its ongoing po-\nlitical struggle with Beijing, which only sent a low-key unofficial repre-\nsentative from the private mainland China's African Friendship Association.\n     Beijing, which regards Taiwan a renegade province, has consistently \nsought to undermine Taiwan in the international diplomatic arena.  Lee, of \ncourse, will take the opportunity to consolidate Taiwan's official \nrelations with the new South African government.  President elect Mandela \nhas called Beijing an old friend he will not forget.  \"Given the status of \nour ties with the ROC in the past... I think it is a good thing that \nPresident Lee will be present when we change governments, to show the \nROC also regards its ties with South Africa,\" South African ambassador to \nthe ROC Johannes Lodewikus Viljoen told local reporters yesterday.  South \nAfrica is the largest among 29 nations diplomatically allied with the ROC.  \nBeijing is trying to induce such nations to switch their official recogni-\ntion to the mainland.  Right after his arrival, Lee and his entourage went \nfrom the Johannesburg airport to Pretoria City Hall, where Pretoria Mayor \nNico Stofberg hosted a banquet in honor of all the foreign delegates.\n     To strengthen friendly relations be tween the two nations, the ROC \nembassy in pretoria has arranged for president Lee to meet with Mandela and \nhis predecessor F.W. de Klerk, as well as with the new governors of south \nAfrica's nie provinces.  Analysts here said South Africa is the last major \nROC diplomatic ally considered likely to officially recognize both Beijing \nand Taipei, a prece dent for dual recognition of both Chinese governments.  \nBeijing succeeded in pressuring both Saudi Arabia and South Korea into \ndropping their recognition of Taipei when they established diplomatic ties \nwith the mainland.  The Atomic Energy Council yesterday denied withholding \nearly information on radiation contamination in the Minsheng Villas \nhousingproject, but failed to answer lingering questions.  AEC chairman \nHsu Yih-yun made pubic previously withheld records of the Minsheng Villa \ncontamination and declared the councils handling of the case to have \nbeenabove the board.  But questions persisted as to why information on the \naffair was not released previously.  Hsu said conflicting versions of \nevents were behind the non-disclosure.\n     But the head of the Minsheng Villa residents' committee, Wang Yu-lin, \nhas accused the AEC of suppressing the true results of those tests.  At a \npress conference last Thursday Wang accused officials of faking the X-ray \nmachine's reports to hide an earlier cover-up.  Three AEC officials are \nunder invesitigation for repressing evidence of radiation contamination at \nMinsheng Villa discovered in 1985, nearly eight years before residents \nlearned of the dangers.  According to AEC Vice Chairman wang Manchao, \ntesting procedures were designed to detect radiation leaks from the machine \nand did not measure dangerously high background radiation levels.\n     The Kaohsiung side alleged that the Saturday vote was illegal, as the \nstanding sub-committee's decision should be final according to the group's \nnormal practice.  \"we hope the Control Yuan can investigate the vote-vote \nis necessary, the Olympic committee should arrange one,\" Kaohsiung Legis-\nlator Huang Chaoshun told reporter after meeting with Control Yuan member.  \nWhereas Mayor Huang Tachou denied any irregularities, the Olympic committee \nruled out the possibility of a revote.  Officials with Taipei's Education \nDepartment, which oversees sports activities, said Huang had lobbied the \ncommittee members in the last week be fore the committee cast their votes.  \nBut the mayor never promised any subsidies.  The officials alleged that it \nwas the Kaohsiung side that had off ered \"bribes.\"  But the Taipei mayor \nhas been unable to win full support from his own front.  Although the comm-\nittee's decision is unlikely to be reversed, some Taipei city councilors \nquestioned the city's ability to host such large scale sports events.\n     Five councilors have threatened to withhold funds for the games \nbudget, which may range from NT$120 billion to NT$150 billion, four to five \ntimes the annual budget for the Education Department.  The councilors \npoilted out that the event will worsen the City's debt, which already \nstands at more than NT$100 billion.  The construction of the sports park in \nKuantu needs some NT$100 billion, which means Taipei will have to pay a \ndaily interest of NT$20 million if the money is loaned from banks, said \nChang Ling.  Chang questioned whether it isworthy to host an Asian Games \nthat may be boycotted by mainland China, the best Asian sports nation.  But \nthe City'streasurer, Liao Cheng-chin, said the city may cover the expense \nby issuing a lottery.  Although the road to hosting the game is still long, \nEducation Department head Lin Chao-hsien said Taipei stand a great chance.  \n\"Of the four Asian little dragons, South Korea has hosted the games in the \npast.  Hong Kong and Singapore ande not interested.  Therefore we stand a \ngreat chance.\"  said Lin. A well-lit model home promoting sales of similar \nmodels in the Taipei suburb of Neihu becomes a local night spot at-\ntraction.  A deueloper reportedly spent more than NT$1 million on the decor \nof the model home.  Kaohsiung police said yesterday they found a charred \nmale body and a gasoline bottle on a beach near the Chichin tunnel."}, {"title": "英文 T11", "content": "     A special fact-finding delegation sent to the mainland by Taipei said \nyesterday it had learned nothing new from briefings with beijing \nauthorities on the Qiandao Lake incident.  \"The briefings given by experts \nfrom the Association for Relations Across the Taiwan Strait were basically \nconsistent with written material we received earlier, with a little more \noral description\" said Shi Hwei-you, deputy secretary-general of the \nStraits Exchange Foundation (SEF).  Taipei sent the delegates to the east-\nern mainland city of Hangzhou in an attempt to find out the truth behind \nthe Qiandao Lake boat fire in which 24 Taiwan tourists were killed.  Shi \nhas requested permission to see and interview the three mainland suspects \ncaught in connection with the robbery-arson-murder case, and to take some \nphysical evidence back to Taipei.  But the ARATS was noncommittal on the \nrequest, indicating it would have to get permission from higher author-\nities.  \"We will determine whether the handling of the case is appropriate \nafter visiting the accident site and examining the relevant evidence,\" said \nShi, who is heading the delegation.  The delegation includes victims' \nrelatives, lawyers, and forensic and criminal investigation experts.\n     This was the first time since the March 31 boat fire that SEF \nofficials and experts were allowed to visit the mainland to learn about the \nmainland's in vestigation into the case.  \"Only by doing so can the fragile \nTaiwan-mainland ties return to normal,\" the semi-official Central News \nAgency cited Shi as saying.  Shi's ARATS counterpart, Lii Yafei, said peo-\nple in the mainland feel as sad as the people in Taiwan about the accient, \nand that the mainland authorities have tried their utmost to solve the \ncase, the CNA reported.  But despite these good intention, mainland author-\nities at yesterday's briefings turned down a request by the delegation to \nsee autopsy reports, and instead showed them slides and taped records about \nthe condition of the bodies.  In a question-and-answer session after the \nbriefings, the SEF delegation pointed out about 100 suspicious points in \nthe case, however, the ARATS' responses were only sufficient for a \n\"preliminary understanding\" of the case, Shi told reporters.  The special \ndelegation is expected to travel today to the site of the accident, some \n200 kilometers from Hangzhou, where it hopes to find more information of \nthe cause of the tragedy.  The delegation will return to Taipei Saturday.\n     Taiwan's top labor policy official yesterday strongly defended the \nrecent deportation of two Thai workers for their participation in an \nillegal strike, but indicatedthe ROC will continue to import foreign labor \nas long as the necessity is clear.  The chairman of the Council of Labor \nAffairs, Chao Shoupo, said two years into its experiment with recruiting \nfor sign labor that the government must exercise a firm hand with imported \nworkers.  He called illegal and unregistered foreign workers in Taiwan a \n\"time bomb for the social order.\"  \"I'd like to very seriously remind \nforeign workers that ROC law appilies to all workers, foreign and \ndomestic,\" he said.  \"We are not inviting problems.\"  In a meeting with the \nEnglish language press to head off \"misunderstandings\" between foreign \nlabor and the government, Chao said labor-exporting nations including \nThailand and the Philippine must educate their citizens about ROC law \nbefore coming to Taiwan.  He said countries of origin were entirely \nresponsible for such orientation programs and other such services for \ntheir nationals' stay in Taiwan.\n     Chao said the two Thai workers, deported after leading a strike of \nsome 600 Thailaborers at a factory in Taichung on Feb. 24-25, had illegally \ninitiated the labor action.  He said foreign workers have as much right to \njoin unions and strike as local workers, but that such action must be \nundertaken through a union or addressed as a labor dispute for arbitration \nby the CLA.  He added the employer had exercise his option not to renew the \nworkers' contracts and work permits.  \"Legally, therefore, neither the \ngovernment nor the employers were guity of wrong doing,\" Chao said.  \nBut the chairman also took pains to emphasize that the ROC's stance on \nforeign labor was little different from that of the United States and other \nCountries that operate under a \"double standard\" for foreign workers.  \nChao said the CLA was pushing a revision of the labor standards law to \nextend for eign workers' permits form two years to three.  But he also \nsaid a freeze on the importation of Thai workers remains a possibility in \nthe ongoing dispute over the practices of employment brokers.\n    Chao vehemently be nounced brokers which charge foreign workers \nexorbitant fees to locate jobs in Taiwan.  He said indenturement to broker, \nwho are allowed by Thai law to deduct up to NT$38,000 from workers' \nsalaries, had a harmful effect on morale and prompted foreign workers to \njoin the lucrative illegal labor market.  Chao said foreign laborers help \nboost the local economy but said the integration of those workers into \nTaiwan's society has never been the CLA's intention.  Roughly 8,000 foreign \nlaborers have joined the ROC workforce since the opening of the labor \nmarket in 1992 in a bid to ease a critical labor shortage.  Chao reiterated \nthat for eign labor must not infringe upon job opportunities for Taiwan \nWorkers, cannot be allowed to lead to immigration or creation of Social \ndisorder, and must not interfere with the industrial upgrading and \nproduction automation.  But in an addendum to these underlying principles, \nChao said the government held a deliberately \"strice\" policy to ward \nblue collar foreign workers while it was \"relaxed\" to ward those \ncategorized as white collar.  Chao based his reasoning on the relative \nnumbers and educational back ground of either category, and said he \nbelieved white collar workers will not bring \"other problems,\" such as \ncrime and social instability, into Taiwan.  Chao Championed labor condition \nin the R.O.C. Unlike many countries, he claimed foreign laborers, whose \nprimary attraction is a willingness to work for considerably lower wages \nthan Taiwan workers, were protected by local labor law.  Except in the case \nof housemaids and nursing aides, the ROc demands foreign workers receive \nthe minimum monthly wage of NT$13,350."}, {"title": "英文 T12", "content": "     An employee of the national Assembly wears sunglasses to hide her \ntearful eyes irritated by sulfur gas-as she works inside the meeting hall \nyesterday.  The Yangmingshan area is full of Sulfuric spings, and there's \none just outside the meeting hall every time the assembly meets there many \ndeputies and employee suffer from sore eyes.  The state-run Chinese \npetroleum Corp. will pay part of its business taxes to the Kaohsiung city \nand county governments if it sets up subsidiaries in Kaohsiung, Economic \nMinister P.K. Chiang said yesterday.  Chiang said the CPC has no intention \nto move its head quarters to Kaohsiung from Taipei, adding that the CPC had \nbetter continue paying business taxes to the Taipei City Government be fore \nestablishing subsidiaries in Kaohsiung city and county.  Chiang made the \nremarks in response to the controversy over which city government the CPC \nshould pay business taxes to The controversy was rekindled by Kaohsiung \nCity's recent failure to win the ROC's choice in a bid to host the 2002 \nAsian Games.  The minister said that based on a conclusion reached during a \nrecent coordination meeting to settle the controversy, it will be totally \nup to the Ministry of Economic Affairs and the CPC to determine whether to \npay all its business taxes to Taipei City Government or split the tax \npayments to Taipei and Kaohsiung governments.\n     But whether Chiang's brainchild can be accepted remains to be seen.  \nThe Kaohsiung City and County governments have repeatedly pressed CPC to \npay business taxes in Kaohsiung, since the company uses Kaohsiung's \nresources and Kaohsiung's environment.  Following the selection of Taipei \nover Kaohsiung on Saturday as the ROC's choice in a bid to host the 2002 \nAsian Games, both Kaohsiung city and county governments have geared up \nfurther to force CPC and other state-run enterprises with plants in \nKaohsiung to pay business taxes in Kaohsiung.  The Kaohsiung county \ngovernment, for in stance, yesterday instructed its taxation units and \nenvironmental protection department to collect business taxes and pollution \nfees from those enterprises whose headquarters are located in Taipei but \nhave maintained manufacturing plants in Kaohsiung County, starting today.  \nThe Kaohsiung City Government will also step up collection of fines from \nCPC for pollutants generated by the firm's Kaohsiung and Talin oil \nrefineries.  Earlier last month, the Kaohsiung City Council threatened to \nstage a massive protest at the two refineries if the CPC failed to respond \npositively to the tax payment issue before the end of June.  But a member \nof the Taipei City Council Also said yesterday that it would be unfair to \nTaipei if the CPC moves its headquarters to Kaohsiung and pays business \ntaxes in Kaohsiung, given the fact that Taipei residents are the most \nnumerous consumers of CPC's oil products.\n     But, during the first all-race presidential campaign, Mandela told \nreporters from Taiwan that he would cherish the longstanding relations be \ntween Pretoria and Taipei.  In the short-run, Mandela's government is not \nexpected to establish formal ties with Beijing, according to the analysts.  \nMeanwhile, local reports said the president of an unidentfied African state \nwill meet Lee in Pretoria to discuss establishing official ties.  When \nspeaking in San Jose Costa Rica, on Sunday, Lee stressed Taiwan would \nactively participate in international affairs as a sovereign nation The \nROC is a sovereign nation and the world should recognize that China is \ndivided and ruled by separate governments, Lee said.  An unsuitable loca-\ntion of workplace has replaced poor pay as the foremost reason for people \nto change their jobs, according to an investigative re port released yes-\nterday by the Directorate General of Budget Accounting and Statistics.\n     The report showed that 22.1 percent of local employees polled changed \ntheir jobs mainly for a better location of work-place, while a lower number \nof 19 percent changed jobs mainly to seek higher monthly pay.  The figures \nindicated that location of the workplace, rather than the rate of pay, is \nthe top consideration made by local employees when choosing jobs, DGBAS \nofficials said.  Meanwhile, a DGBAS study report on manpower utilization \nindicated that as many as 1.54 local employees were not properly utilized \nin 1993.  Of that number, some 488,000 employees failed to have a match \nbetween their levels of education and their job responsibilities.  This \ndemonstrated a waste of educational resources, the officials noted.  DGBAS \ntallies also showed that a total of 170,000 local residents are willing to \njoin the domestic job market in the upcoming year.  This potential labor \nforce, coupled with the release of a massive amount of labor from the \nagricultural sector expected upon Taiwan's entry into the General Agreement \non Tariffs and TRade, will lead to a sharp increase in labor suply and ease \nthe labor shortage presently plaguing the construction and manufacturing \nindustries.  A deep-sea fishing boat carrying six anglers and three crew \nmembers on a fihing trip off the coast of southeast Taiwan remained \nunaccounted for, police in Taiung said yesterday.  Redhead No. 1 failed to \nreturn to its native village at Lanyu, or Orchid Island, an off-shore \nisland southeast of Taiwan, at 9 A.M. yesterday as scheduled, said owner.\n     The Taitung county police department requested the Navy's seagull Unit \nto help locate the miss in boat, but rescue workers said a search \nyesterday amounted to nothing.  The boat company's operator said the Navy \nfound four small freezers on a rocky seashore at Little Lanyu, but found no \ntraces of the fishermen or the crew.  At least three men broke into a \nchanghua company and carted away a safety deposit box containing more than \nNT$1.9 million in cash, police said yesterday.  An alarm system installed \nin the firm in center Taiwan did not go off during the Burglary, said the \npolice, who did not rule out company workers as suspects in the theft.  The \nburglars forced their way into the office with iron bars and screwdrivers, \nwhich were found abandoned at the company along with some suspicious."}, {"title": "英文 T13", "content": "     In the course of the centuries, the hill underwent various transform-\nations and alterations, until it took on its present aspect which is mainly \nthe result of Michelangelo's plan.\n     The geometric pattern on the pavement of the square, conceived in the \nform of expanding and crossing elliptical shapes was also designed by him, \nand harmonises well with the architecture of the whole.  In 1535, Miche-\nlangelo took the statue of Marcus Aurelius from the Lateran and set it up \nin the center of the square; it is the sole remaining example of the eques-\ntrian statues of the Roman imperial period.  There are various theories and \nlegends to explain the reasons which made it possible for this monument to \ncome down to us.  The main reason, however, is to be found in the fact that \nit was long thought to represent the Emperor Constantine, the first to \nallow the practice of the Christian religion.\n     The Computation Center is principally housed in the Computer Building \nlocated in the northeast section of the campus near the East residence \nhalls, and operates branch stations in the Hammond, Boucke and Willard \nBuildings.  The Computer Building is open 24 hours a day, and the branch \nstations are open during the day and evening hours, 7 days a week.\n     The main computer at the Computation Center is an IBM 3081 Processor \nwith sixteen million bytes of memory, three billion bytes of disk storage, \nand 12 tape drives.  The System 370/3081 is used primarily as a batch pro-\ncessor, supported by OS/VS2 MVS with JES 2.  Batch jobs may be entered re-\nmote batch stations, or they may be entered at a terminal through use of \nINTERACT, an interactive text editor with a flexible file storage system.\n     In the world of high finance, where billion-dollar deals can be struck \nbetween cocktails and dessert, the hottest play these days is once obscure \ntransaction known as the leveraged buyout.  In such operations, corporate \nofficers are turning publicly held firms into private businesses that are \nfree from the demands of short-term investors and the unwanted attention of \ncorporate raiders.  In the process, many of them make vast profits for \nshareholders and themselves.  One of the largest and most ambitious buyouts \nyet was proposed last week by executives of R.H. Macy / Co. (discal 1985 \nsales:  $4.4 billion), the eleventh-biggest U.S. retailer.  Led by Chairman \nEdward Finkelstein, a group of top officers offered $70 a share, or $3.58 \nbillion, for Macy's stock, which had been selling for about $50 a share.  \nThe Macy's executives were working last week with the Wall Street firm of \nGoldman Sachs to line up virtually all that money.  The high buyout price, \napparently designed to repel rival offers and avoid a bidding war for the \ncompany, drove up the value of other retailing issues as investors specu-\nlated that a wave of buyouts was about to break over the department-store \nindustry.  Indeed, merchandising firms are much in vogue with acquisition-\nminded managers.  One day after the Macy's announcement, officers of House \nhold International agreed to pay $700 million for the Chicago-based cong-\nlomerate's retailing units, which include Coast-to-Coast hardware and the \nBen Franklin variety chain.  Even small Wieboldt Stores, a 102-year-old \nChicago concern, last week announced a $37.4 million deal that turned the \nfirm into a private company.\n     Market penetration can be accomplished, in some cases, by getting the \nconsumer to try a product through the distribution of free samples, direct \nmail advertising, or door to door promotion.\n     Packaging is an important part of product identification, especially \nin the cases of food and household items.  An eye-catching package will \nstand out on a store shelf, giving it an edge over its competitors.\n     Have you ever heard of the Golden Gate Bridge in California?  Have you \never seen the picture of it?  Perhaps someone in your family has seen this \nbridge.  It is one of the first sights people see if they are coming to the \nwest coast of the United States.\n     Suppose you are sailing into San Francisco, you are near the coast of \nCalifornia.  The bridge is straight ahead of you.  At first, it looks like \na piece of steel lace across the bay from north to south.  Then your ship \nsails closer, and the bridge appears larger and larger.  At last, your ship \npasses under the bridge.  If you look behind, you can see this huge struc-\nture from the bay.  On your right, you can see the city of San Francisco.  \nOn your left, you can see the cities of Berkeley and Oakland.\n     The main section of the Golden Gate Bridge is a suspension span.  In a \nsuspension bridge the span does not rest on posts.  It hangs from cables.  \nHuge towers support two long steel cables.  The cables are over three feet \nthick.  Smaller cables hang vertically every few feet from the large cables \nand support the main span of the bridge.  The towers, the cables, large and \nsmall, and all in red, together with the blue water below as well as the \nflying sea gulls constitute a combination of beauty.\n     The suspension span of this bridge is 4,200 feet long between the tow-\ners.  It is the longest suspension span in the world.  The floor of this \nbridge is over ninety feet wide, and six lanes of cars--three in each di-\nrection--can pass over it at the same time.  There are also side walks.  It \nis a very long walk across the bridge, but if you would like to walk, you \ncan watch the ships below.  You can see ships from all over the world \nsailing into and out of the bay.  The bridge is 220 feet above the water, \nand even the biggest ships can easily sail under it."}, {"title": "英文 T14", "content": "     Mickey Mouse celebrated his 50th birthday in 1978, and there were \nspecial events all over the country.  There was even a party at the White \nHouse.\n     Walt Disney and his partner Ub Iwerks created Mickey in 1928.  At \nfirst, Mickey looked a little like Oswald the Rabbit, an old Disney charac-\nter.  This funny-looking little mouse was called Mortimer.  Disney's wife \ndidn't like the name, and her friends didn't either, so Disney changed it \nto Mickey.  After 1938, Mickey's looks changed too.\n     By 1929, Mickey Mouse was as popular as Coca-Cola.  Mickey Mouse clubs \nstarted up all over the United States.  By 1931, the Mickey Mouse Club had \na million members.  Mickey Mouse was popular all over the world.  Italians \ncalled him Topolino.  In London, Madame Tussaud's Wax Museum made Mickey in \nwax.  From the White House in Washington, Eleanor Roosevelt wrote a letter \nto Walt Disney.  She told him that President Roosevelt loved Mickey Mouse:  \n\"We are all most grateful to you for many delightful evenings.\"\n     Why do people all over the world, young and old, like Mickey Mouse?  \nMaybe it is just because he is a nice guy.\n     Microwaves are a type of electromagnetic radiation; they are a very \nmild form of electrical or magnetic wave that moves through space.  Unlike \nX rays and gamma rays, which are very powerful waves of radiation, micro-\nwaves are rather weak and are much more like the waves of radiation used in \nradio broadcasting.\n     In microwave ovens, the use of microwaves with which most people are \nfamiliar, the waves are produced by an electronic tube called a magnetron.  \nMicrowaves produce heat in any food placed inside the oven by causing the \nwater in the food to vibrate rapidly and thus heat up.  Food that have more \nwater in them take less time to cook and probably have more of their nu-\ntrients left intact when cooked in a microwave oven.\n     Microwaves do not pass through metal, so the microwaves are retained \ninside the oven.  Microwaves pass immediately through glass, paper, and \nplastic with no effect on these materials or on the microwave oven; nothing \ninside a microwave oven is heated except the food itself, so the cooking \nprocess is much efficient than in conventional ovens.  Sometimes a pan or \ncontainer is heated because it is touching the hot food, though; some users \nof microwave ovens have been burned by hot food, by hot pans, or steam es-\ncaping from the food.  No documented case of radiation burns from a micro-\nwave oven has ever been reported.\n     Japan is perhaps the most seismically active country in the world with \nover 10,000 earthquakes hitting each year.  Of these 10,000, one thousand \nare strong for people to feel.  Recently, Tokyo was hit by two earthquakes \nregistering over 6 on the Richter scale two nights in a row.  Japan expects \neven larger earthquakes in the future, so the Japanese government, scien-\ntists, and people are making numerous preparations.\n     The 1980 quake in Algeria was 7.5 on the Richter scale and devastated \nthe country, killing over 200,000 people.  The residents of Tokyo remember \nthe earthquake of 1923, which killed 100,000 Japanese.  These instances of \nthe devastation an earthquake can cause are foremost in the Japanese people \nminds and are the reasons for their extensive preparations.\n     More than any other earthquake-prone country, Japan is prepared for a \nstrong earthquake.  For example, the Japanese government spends as much on \nearthquake preparations as it does on national defense.  The city of Tokyo \nis destroying thousands of wooden homes and replacing them with concrete \nstructures.  The city hopes this will help eliminate destruction by fire \nafter a major earthquake.  Furthermore, the city is storing food, blankets, \nbaby bottles, and 400,000 tons of water in preparation for the expected \nquake.  The water (stored in earthquake-proof storage houses) is sufficient \nfor all 12 million Tokyo residents for 10 days.\n     The Bill of Lading (its abbreviation is B/L) is an essential document \nin international trade and shipment.  It is important in law, in finance, \nand in insurance.\n     A clean Bill of Lading means that the ship owners agree that the goods \nwere received on board in good condition.  If the goods are not in good \norder and condition, the Bills of Lading are described as \"dirty,\" \"un-\nclean\" or \"foul.\"\n     A Bill of Lading is a negotiable document.  An importer can endorse \nthe Bill of Lading and sell the good in this way, even before they arrive.  \nAn importer can endorse a Bill of Lading by signing across the back.  In \nthis way, ownership of the goods can be transferred to another buyer simply \nby handing the endorsed Bill of Lading to him.  A Bill of Lading can state \nthat the goods must be delivered to a certain person or company, or it may \nstate \"to order\".  (This phrase is also printed on checks and Bills of Ex-\nchange.)  A Letter of Credit may demand that the Bill of Lading should be \nmade out \"to order\" and endorsed \"in blake.\"  This means that the sellers \nplace no restrictions on the right to transfer ownership.\n     International trade develops because certain countries are able to \nproduce some goods more efficiently than other countries.  They exchange \ngoods to satisfy their needs and wants.  Efficient production may be the \nresult of several factors.  A certain climate in a particular country may \nallow that country to grow agricultural products in abundance.  For ins-\ntance, the climates in the United States and Canada are suitable for pro-\nduction of large amounts of wheat.  Natural resources such as oil or coal \nare abundant in other countries.  Countries with a large pool of unskilled \nlaborers are able to produce products which are labor intensive more cheap-\nly than countries with highly paid, skilled labor forces.  Another factor \nis geographical location.  Countries like Singapore and Panama engage in \nbanking and trading because they are located on world trade routes."}, {"title": "英文 T15", "content": "     The expression \"a thriving business\" is one that you may have heard \nmany times.  Think for a moment what it means; a company that is healthy, \nshows a profit, and goes forward with the times.  A company that does not \nthrive can not exist very long.\n     Communication has become one of the most important aspects of modern \nbusiness life.  No matter what the size of the company, communication is \nmost significant.  Even the smallest enterprise would be hard hit if the \nowner could not convey correct orders to his suppliers or communicate con-\nvincingly with his customers.  Medium-sized companies and larger ones, with \ntheir multiplicity of employees, rely even more heavily on the art of com-\nmunication.  Every level of a firm is involved.  The board of directors \nmust clearly communicate its policy decisions to executives, who in turn \nmust communicate with supervisors.  Supervisors have departments.  Com-\nmunication must be effective if a firm is to function as an organic whole.\n     The first coin was made in China.  Some Chinese businessman must have \ngotten tired of carrying a heavy load of fishhooks and knives and shirts to \npay a large bill.  So he made a model of the top part of a shirt on metal.  \nIt was round and had a hole in the center.  The money was round, according \nto one story, so that it could roll from place to place.\n     Stone money was one of the most curious kinds.  It was made on the is-\nland of Yap in the Pacific.  A stone \"coin\" measured 5 feet in diameter and \nwas 7 inches thick.  There was a hole at the center so that the \"coin\" \ncould be carried on a pole by two men.  It must have been heavy to carry \naround.  A stone coin was worth 10,000 coconuts or one wife.\n     Different countries have used different metals and designs for their \nmoney.  The first coins in England were made of tin.  Sweden and Russia \nused copper to make their money.  Some of the Swedish copper money weighed \nmore than 30 pounds.  It must have been inconvenient.  When people wanted \nto pay a big bill, they had to use an oxcart.\n     The Letter of Credit (its abbreviation is L/C) is essential in the fi-\nnance of international trade.  It is a reliable and safe method of payment, \nand it protects the seller as well as the buyer.  The seller (or exporter) \nneeds the Letter of Credit because the buyer may be a new customer, and the \nexporter may know nothing about his credit status.  Even if the buyer is a \nregular customer, his credit with his bank may change from time to time and \nthe seller's bank may not have enough information from the buyer's bank to \narrange a banker's transfer, which is the other major method of financing \ninternational trade.  A banker's transfer is usually made through sending a \ntelegraphic message, that is an order, called a telegraphic transfer, sent \nfrom a bank by telegraph or cable to an overseas bank to pay money to an \nexporter in the overseas country.  The overseas bank is usually a corres-\npondent or agent bank, although sometimes banks have a branch in the over-\nseas country.\n     Expanded academic exchanges between Taiwan and the mainland should be \nallowed and encouraged.  They benefit both sides.  Scholars and scientists \nin Taiwan have the advantages of greater academic freedom and financial re-\nsources than their counterparts on the mainland.  The academics on the \nmainland, on the other hand, especially those who study humanities subject, \nhave access to valuable data not easily available in Taiwan.  The condition \non the two sides, therefore, complement each other.\n     The prosperity of our society has greatly heightened the life expect-\nancy of our people in recent decades, with the result that the proportion \nof aged people in the population has increased considerably.  The propor-\ntion, according to the authorities, will grow to 8.4 percent by the year \n2000.  Problems stemming from the presence of a large number of senior \ncitizens have begun to surface.\n     All levels of government in Taiwan recently imposed restrictions to \nforce local KTV, MTV and sauna bath center to close at 3 a.m.  In the first \nfew days of this new policy, police did not encounter many violent reaction \nfrom business owners.  But as expected many operators were strongly dis-\nsatisfied with the new measure and simply gave the impression that they had \nstopped business only to continue to provide service to customers.\n     Management buyouts are evolving into offensive weapons too.  \"They can \nbe viewed as offers made by internal raiders.\" notes Alfred Rappaport, pro-\nfessor of accounting at Northwestern University's Kellogg School of Manage-\nment.  In one snarled battle, investors, led by the former chairman of a \nBeatrice acquisiton, offered nearly $5 billion for Beatrice, which last \nmonth reject the bid.  Now Beatrice may be turning to outsiders for help.  \nSaid one Chicogo lawyer:  \"Their investment bankers are burning up the \nphone lines looking for a white knight.\"\n     Beatrice last week received word of a different kind of proposal that \nthe firm may wish to ponder.  It came from Warren Avis, founder of the Avis \ncar rental company.  The entrepreneur, who sold Avis in 1954, said he wants \nto buy it back from Beatrice, which has owned the business for little more \nthan a year.  Avis said he has assembled a group of private, international \ninvestors who hope to acquire the auto concern, which operates in more than \n100 countries and is worth an estimated $400 million.\n     Management buyouts are hot and growing hotter because they have so far \nproduced countless winners and few if any losers.  A typical deal involves \nbankers, institutional investors, Wall Street firms that specialize in \nbuyouts, and the managers of acquired companies.  To start a transaction, \nthe executives turn to Wall Street experts to arrange financing.  \"We look \nfor three things,\" says Leonard Shaykin, managing partner of Adler / Shay-\nkin, an investment house that specializes in buyouts.  \"They are consistent \npast profits, predictable future and quality management.\""}, {"title": "英文 T16", "content": "     Dear President:  I think that you should build a rescue mission for \nthose poor, homeless black people a place to live after their freedom.  \nAlso try to find some jobs for them so they can earn some salaries to have \na living.  Please take this seriously, because I really hope that those \npeople can have a happy lifetime since they already had a miserable life \nbefore.\n     Dear Adam:  Guess what?  I have talked to the President of the United \nStates of America, Abraham Lincoln about your misfortune.  President \nLincoln will have warrants and search everyone's house and find out other \npoor men like you, before you and others can find a job, you and others \nwill live in a rescue mission that I have suggested to the President \nLincoln.  So please do not worry about yourself, your second half of life \nwill be like normal people.\n     I think that President Lincoln did the right thing to save all the \nblack people back to the 18th century.  If President Lincoln didn't make \nsuch a concise choice, maybe there will still be slavery problems going on \nin this futuristic world of 21st century.  I guess that those black people \nreally loved Lincoln's 14th and 15th Amendment .  I hope there will not be \nany slavery or slavery-like activities in the future, we should all get \nalong.\n     If anyone who needs to buy a brand new bike or any other useful \nfurniture, please contact me, Adam White, 883-9393, thank you!  \n     President Lincoln, was born in August 23, 1824.  His luck in June is \nvery lucky, he did everything so fast, everything that he wanted was there \nfor him.  His goals all came true, he had a good time with his family, he \ndidn't have to worry much about the events that happened in United States \nof America.\n     Adam White, was born in December 11, 1832.  His luck in June is also \nvery lucky that the President just gave warrant to search everyone's house \nand took out all the black people who were secretly hidden under the \nbasement, in the secret closet and so on.  Adam began with a new wonderful \nlife in June, he started to work and had his own house.\n     Nervousness and the pressure surround my body and my mind.  The \nVietnam's mighty artillery is ready for the fire as the neutral whistles.  \nAt this moment, I can feel the sharp confidence of my the enemy, but they \npretend nothing really matters except the deep concentration of their \nartillery.  Every soldier on my side waits for each other's communication \nand encouragement.\n     Longs beeps, and then a canon is sent from the opposite side of the \nfield.  It sends us into the heat of the battle and the misery of the \ncompetition.  The round, white, and shinny leather object comes toward me \nfirst; I do not panic, I store all my energy in my hands to defend myself.  \nNext, our most powerful soldier, whose arms as strong as Hercules, passes \nthe bomb to our captain.  Without any consideration, the captain jumps up \nlike Michael Jordan and spikes the bomb back to Vietnam.  The captain's \nefforts and resources prove inadequate; he cannot withstand the harshness \nof the enemy's attack.  Finally, we win the first round of the battle, but \nnobody knows how many more rounds we will have to suffer.\n     My mind refocuses during the battle.  The smell of sweat in the air \ntortures me as if I am in hell.  This is our last chance to conquer our foe \nand win the battle.  Our commander gathers our soldiers and has a fast \nmeeting.  The strategy is clear; everyone has to remain calm and concen-\ntrate on the white bomb when our artillery fires.  After he fires, the \nenemy becomes nervous and loses their tempers.  Finally, they lose this \nWWI battle and cry out for their country.  It's good that we are not like \nthe enemy, because half of our soldiers are wounded.  We shake hands with \nthe weak enemy with humble pride pity on our countenance, but hidden inside \nour hearts are the greatest happiness.\n     On November 2, 1863, several months after the battle of Gettysburg, \nDavid Wills invited President Lincoln to make a \"few appropriate remarks\" \nat the consecration of a cemetery for the Union war dead.  In early July, \nPennsylvania governor Andrew Curtin had charged Wills, a successful local \ncitizen and judge, with cleaning up the horrible aftermath of the battle:  \nwounded soldiers crammed into every available building, and thousands of \nswollen dead strewn among hundreds of bloated dead horses.  Abraham \nLincoln, the President of the United States had an eloquent address at \nGettysburg on November 19, 1863.  Adam White, the slave who first asked \nPresident Lincoln a favor to save all the black people.  The President \nLincoln had declared two very important amendments to black people.  The \n14th Amendment, which guaranteed black Americans all rights of citizens, no \nslavery allows, all people who are born in the U.S. are American citizens \nfor their lifetimes.  The 15th Amendment, which guaranteed black men the \nright to vote.  President also let the native-born Americans, flocked to \nthe cities in search of jobs.  President Lincoln said \"I, Abraham Lincoln, \nPresident of the United States, by virtue of the power in me vested as \nCommander-in-Chief do order and declare that all persons held as slaves \nwithin said designated States and parts of States are, and henceforward \nshall be, free; and that the Executive Government of the United States, \nincluding the military and naval authorities thereof, will recognize and \nmaintain the freedom of said persons.  And I hereby enjoin upon the people \nso declared to be free to abstain from all violence, unless in necessary \nself-defense; and I recommend to them that, in all cases when allowed, they \nlabor faithfully for reasonable wages\".\n     John Lennon didn't invent it rock and roll, nor did he embody it as \nJohn toweringly as figures like Elvis and Little Richard, but he did more \nthan anyone else to shake it up, move it forward and instill it with a \nconscience.  As the most daring and outspoken of the four Beatles, he help \nshape the agenda of the Sixties - socially and politically, no less than \nmusically.  As a solo artist, he made music that alternately disturbed and \nsoothed provoked and sought community.  As a human being, he served as an \nexemplar of honesty in his art and life.\n     Lennon did what he needed to do to make his life complete.  He did not \nstrive for perfection, nor did he try and make everyone happy.  Lennon \nremarked at one time, \"I don't intend to be a performing flea any more.  I \nwas a dreamweaver, but although I'll be around I don't intend to be running \nat 20,000 miles an hour trying to prove myself.  I don't want to die at \n40.\" Again, Lennon connected with society by sharing the same types of \nviews as the people, like this one.  Ironically, Lennon did die at 40 and \nthe death affected the world.\n     Lennon's life was a great journey but his death affected society \ngreatly.  Lennon's death emotionally shook thousand and traumatized America \npeople and still plays a role in lives today.  We can only \"imagine\" where \nwe be if Lennon were with us today.  Spencer established, \"We are a better \npeople because of Lennon but lost without him.\"  J. Lehmann; June 1997."}, {"title": "英文 T17", "content": "     Born of the Sun and one of the Atlantides (note Shelley's familiar \nsymbolism of light and the water of universal life), the witch lives in \na cave amidst the Atlas mountains.  Lovely, gentle, and powerful, she \nattracts gods, men, and beasts to her marvelous storehouse of treasures.  \nThe Naiads, Hamadryads, and Oreads (spirits in natural objects such as \nstreams and trees) would be her familiars, but she is immortal and each \nof them will eventually die.  In a magic boat that can sail on air or \nwater, the witch is conducted everywhere by a hermaphroditic pilot.  She \nsends helpful or imaginative dream.  At other times she plays merry pranks.\n     Among the longer poems by Shelley, this displays perhaps the rich-\nest and most graceful of consistent imagery and lyricism.  Keats thought \nthe watch was beauty; others have interpreted her as Creative Imagination, \nVenus Genetrix, and even as the spirit of electricity.  She seems defi-\nnitely to be spiritualized love, eternal but lending beauty to mortals \nand beauty, sympathy and wisdom.  Perhaps the withch might be thought of \nas Asia before she meets Prometheus; she is the ideal of love before it \nis personified in human flesh.  Sexless herself, the witch creates in \nthe hermaphrodite the perfect union of male and female.  In this frank \nescapism to a dream world and dream symbolism, Shelley sought a respite \nfrom the real world that was increasingly pressing upon him.\n     EPIPSYCHIDION (1821, 1822) in title is a coined Greek wording to \nmean \"this soul out of my soul\" carrying on the psyche-epipsyche theme \nfrom Alastor.  With the Defence of Poetry and Adonais, both completed \nsoon thereafter, it forms a triad contemplating the process of creati-\nvity in the poet, the forces contributing to his achievement, and the \nforces corrupting and negating him.  This heroic-couplet poem arose from \nShelley's profound sympathy for Teresa (known to the Shelleys as Emilia \nor Emily) Viviani, daughter of the governor of Pisa.  Her stingy father \nhad virtually imprisoned her at the convent of St.  Anna in Pisa while \nseeking for her a husband who would ask no dowry.  A beautiful nineteen-\nyear-old brunette with some facility in writing poetry, she powerfully \nappealed to Shelley as the victim of parental tyranny.  She extravagantly \npraised Shelley and fascinated him throughout the writing of Epipsychi-\ndion, but even before the publication of the poem, she disillusioned \nShelley by meekly submitting to her father's choice of a husband.  He \nmused:  \"The person whom it celebrates was a cloud instead of a Juno.\"  \nThis section seems to start with strong autobiographical references, \nto proceed to Platonic implications and then to dwell especially upon \nastronomical conjectures.  The Platonism seems based upon a threefold \ndivision of soul enunciated in Plato's Phaedrus, Republic, and Timaeus:  \n(1) Immortal spirit to which Shelley aspires.  Emilia is the anima or \nepipsyche embodying this highest realm of the soul.  (2) Higher rational \nor mortal soul, which Shelley believes represented by Mary.  (3) Lower \nappetitive soul, apparently symboized by \"Claire.\"\n     Characteristic of a yet unformed genre, its practitioners frequ-\nently tried to offer it as what it was not in essence:  Defoe tried to \nmasqueade fiction as fact, Richardson staked his achievement upon moral \npreaching, and Sterne cavorted willfully.  Part of this uncertain quality \nof 18th-century novels arose from their incredibly mixed ancestry of \nform, an examination of which is given below.  The primary impulse of \nthe novel came from the Spanish picaresque tales, apparently burgles \nof the courtly romance, substituting a low scoundrel for the questing \nknight.  Largely composed of a string of episodes, these accounts were \nmainly held together by the racy personality of the central figure, and \nthe atmosphere of actual tangible things and genuine earthy experience \nwas pervasive.  Also, as mentioned before, through much of the 17th \ncentury the fashionable English aristocrats were addicted to the French \ninto such notable collections as Boccaccio's Decameron.\n     The pastoral romance from Hellenistic Greeks such as Apuleius, with \nItalian and Spanish imitations in the Renaissance, stimulated the arti-\nfictions of Malory and Mandeville, generating a tase for recitals of \nadventurous action.  The domestic literature of roguery, in Greene and \nDekker of the Renaissance and later in the 17th century from Tom Briwn \nand Ned Ward, strengthened the picaresque influence and provided a \nbouncy, vivacious narrative of low life.  Though the sketches of \"char\" \nby Sir Thomas Overbury and John Eare belong with the essay rather than \nwith the novel, their astute appraisals of personalities provide a \nstimulus for the revelation of character in the later novel.\n     Allegories such as Pilgrim's Progress provided fiction with a \ncompelling seriousness it had previously lacked and also offered an \nimaginative ingenuity and flashes of realism.  Lesser domestic sources \nof fiction were the hest books and chapbooks of unprofessional liter-\nateurs, which perpetuated the storytelling faculties of the people which \nChaucer had employed so well.\n     According to the Digest of Educational Statistics, the total ex-\npenditures for public and nonpublic schools at all levels of edu-\ncation from kindergarten through the graduate curriculum doubled in \nseven years.  The estimate for the recent academic year 1972-73 is \n$90,200 million.  The defense budget, on the other hand, is about \n$76,000 million this year.\n     \"The Structure of 'Tssing p'ing TIIAO' is classical; the inter-\npretation is individualistic,\" explains young Chinese choreographer \nMaleina Yung, She is speaking of her Chinese dance drama which was \nstaged recently in New York's Carnegie Hall."}, {"title": "英文 T18", "content": "     Because of its enormous size, the Pacific Ocean is often considered \nthe principal physical feature of the earth.  It extends from Bering \nStrait to 60 south latitude--a northsouth distance of about 13,500 kilo-\nmeters (8,400 miles).  Its width along the equator is about 16,900 kilome-\nters (10,500 miles).  The total surface area of the Pacific is about \n180,000,000 square kilometers (70,000,000 square miles), or about one third \nof the earth's surface.  The Pacific is the world's deepest ocean.  About \n70 percent of its total bottom area is more than 3,900 meters (13,000 feet) \nbelow sea level.  This deep area-larger than the entire surface area of the \nAtlantic Ocean--is usually called the Pacific Basin.\n     Boundaries.  The coastlines of North and South America form the \neastern boundary of the Pacific.  The western boundary is more difficult to \ndefine because of the presence of many large islands separated from the \nAsian and Australian mainland's by shallow seas.\n     Temperature of the Surface Waters.  The Pacific is a warm ocean.  Most \nof the surface water between the tropics of Cancer and Capricorn has a tem-\nperature of well above 216XC (706XF).  Little cold water from the Arctic \nOcean enters the Pacific through the Bering Strait.  But in the Southern \nHemisphere, the Humboldt Current moves masses of cold water from the Ant-\narctic Ocean through the eastern Pacific toward the equator.  Most of the \nPacific islands are on the western side of the ocean between the Tropic of \nCancer and the Tropic of Capricorn.  They enjoy warm temperatures that \nchange little from season to season.\n     The Pacific Islands The Pacific islands fall into two very different \ngroups_continental islands and oceanic islands.  The continental islands \nrise from the continental shelves and are composed of the same kinds of \nrocks that are found on the nearby continents-igneous, metamorphic, and \nsedimentary rocks.  Oceanic islands rise from the deep floor of the Pacific \nBasin.  They are composed of volcanic materials, coral, and limestone.  \nThere are thousands of volcanic islands and coral and limestone reefs \nspread throughout the Pacific Basin.\n     The continental Islands of the Pacific.  These islands are really \npartially submerged mountain chains.  They belong to the long chain of \nmountains and volcanoes that border the Pacific Basin.  Because this \nmountain chain contains many volcanoes, it is often called the Circle of \nFire or Ring of Fire.  The continental islands have a greater variety of \nrocks and more complex structures than do the oceanic islands.  Therefore \nthey are more likely to contain mineral ores of value.\n     Oceanic Islands of the Pacific.  Two different processes helped to \nform the Pacific oceanic islands.  These processes are volcanism and sub-\nsidence.  Volcanism extrudes (pours out) lava from the interior of the \nearth.  Volcanism produces an island if enough lava is poured out to form \na mass that reaches above sea level.  Hawaii is the largest and highest \nisland formed by volcanism.  It has an area of 10,414 square kilometers \n(4,021 square miles).  Its summit, Mauna Kea, rises 4,208 meters (13,796 \nfeet) above sea level.\n     The great weight of the lava forces the crust of the Pacific Basin to \nsink.  This process is called subsidence.  The older an island, the more \nit has sunk and the smaller it has become.  But while the volcanic island \nsinks, coral colonies living just below the surface sometimes build masses \nof coral on the sinking volcano.  In time the volcanic material may disa-\nppear beneath the ocean.  But the corals go on building upward, forming \nreefs just below the ocean surface.  If sand, formed by breakup of the \ncoral rock, collects on top of the rock, a low sandy island results.  \nIf this sandy island is shaped like a ring, it is called an atoll.  In \nsome cases masses of coral rock have been lifted above the surface by \nupward movement of the earth's crust.  Many islands of the Tonga group \nwere formed in this way.\n     It is easy to see a pattern in the distribution of these oceanic \nislands.  Volcanic activity has occurred along parallel cracks on the floor \nof the ocean.  In the Northern Hemisphere the most noticeable ridge forms \nthe base of the Hawaiian Islands.  These islands spread along the ridge for \nabout 2,400 kilometers (1,500 miles).  At the southeastern end of the is-\nland chain is the highest island (Hawaii), which still contains several \nactive volcanoes.  At the northwestern end of the chain are coral reefs \nand isolated rocks.\n     2. PACIFIC OCEAN AND ISLANDS Oceania The islands of the Pacific are \noften called Oceania.  New Zealand, Australia, and the islands and island \ngroups in the shallow waters off the Asian mainland (such as the Philippine \nIslands) are generally not considered part of Oceania.  The islands of Oce-\nania are divided into three main groups-Polynesia (\"many islands\"), Melan-\nesia (\"black islands\"), and Micronesia (\"small islands\").\n     Polynesia, the largest division, is made up of a large triangle of \nislands in the central and southeastern Pacific.  It includes French Pol-\nynesia; the Hawaiian Islands; Tonga; Tuvalu; American Samoa and Western \nSamoa; part of Kiribati; Easter, Midway, and Niue islands; and the Tokelau, \nCook, Pitcairn, and Wallis and Futuna island groups.  New Zealand is some-\ntimes included because the original Maori population is Polynesian.  Many \nPolynesian islands are the tops of volcanic mountains.  Others are atolls \nor coral islands.\n     Melanesia, in the southwestern Pacific, includes Irian Jaya, in Indo-\nnesia; New Caledonia; and the nations of Papua New Guinea, Vanuatu, the \nSolomon Islands, and Fiji.  The larger Melanesian islands are mountainous, \nand many of them are densely forested.\n     The many small islands in northwestern Oceania are known as Micro-\nnesia.  This region includes Naurn; Wake Island; part of Kiribati; and the \nMarshall, Caroline, and Marianas island groups.  Most of the islands of Mi-\ncronesia are low-lying coral atolls.\n     The People.  The peopleof Oceania are often divided into three ethnic \ngroups that parallel the geographic division of the Polynesians form a \ndistinct ethnic group.  Melanesians and Micronesians include peoples of \nmany different origins.  The largestther groups on the islands are the \nAmericans and the Japanese on the Hawaiian Islands and people of Indian \ndescent, who outnumber the native Melanesians on Fiji.\n     Polynesians share the same basic language and have common physical and \ncultural characteristics.  Before Europeans came to Oceania, Polynesians \noccupied almost all the islands of the South Pacific, as well as Hawaii and \nNew Zealand."}, {"title": "英文 T19", "content": "     \"Experimental Theater Enters New Era\" Often holding up a mirror to \nhuman development, experimental theater has played a creative role in \nTaiwan society over the past decade.  As it takes on the challenges of the \n1990's, it is poised to enter a new phase of progress.\n     Experimental theater was introduced to Taiwan, about 11 years ago by a \ngroup of enthusiastic young people.  Dr. Wu Ching-chi, who had previously \njoined the \"Ramama Theater\" while he was studying in the U.S.A. brought \nthis new form of performing art back to Taiwan, and started the first \nexperimental theater.\n     The most important feature of \"experimental theater\" is that it can \nbe performed without a stage and without traditional audience seats.  It \ncan be performed in the streets, in train stations, parks and public \nsquares and other makeshift venues.\n     It first started in New York, as a movement against the commercialized \nbroadway plays, and it was first known as the \"Out of Broadway\" movement.\n     Since it is not restricted by stage or venue, its style tends to be \nimpromptu and with free association, which is quite popular with young \npeople.  Not much attention was paid to experimental theater when it was \nfirst introduced into Taiwan, but when the play \"Ho Chu Shin Pei\" \nperformed by Lan Ling Theater Workshop won widespread acclaim in 1980, the \nformat began to boom in university campuses.\n     The success of \"Ho Chu Shin Pei\" encouraged young performers, and \naroused the curiosity of the general public.\n     In 1981, at the close of the Second Annual Experimental Theater Festi-\nval, it was suggested that a small experimental theater should be set up \nfor use by all people interested in the field, to train performers and \nstage workers through practical experience.\n     In the spring of 1982, the Hong Kong theater group \"Twenty-sided Idea\" \nperformed two experimental theater acts when it attended the First Asian \nTheater Festival held in Taiwan.  This success further interested enthusi-\nasts in Taiwan, and boosted their confidence and inspiration.\n     In Taiwan, the development of experimental theater coincided with the \neasing of political and social restrictions and taboos, and the venting of \ndissent and grievances.  Thus, experimental theater, with its \nanti-institutional nature and contents, became a new force in the litera-\nture and art circles.  Although the performances were crude, a lively \natmosphere was created by enthusiastic young people, and thus a theater \nwith an esthetic content was firmly rooted between 1980 and 1986.\n     As society has become more liberal since 1986, people began to care \nmore about politics and society as a whole, and thus many small experi-\nmental theater groups joined the social movements, and provided performers \nwith another method to show their conern for the environment.\n     However, during that period, most experimental theater group faced the \nproblem of not having enough talented performers, script-writers, directors \nand other stage workers, and thus the experimental theater of that era had \nto be attached to the numerous social or political movements to survive.\n     In consequence, the tough question finally came; \"What is the ultimate \ngoal?  Is it the social movements themselves or the experimental theater \nper se?\"\n     Theatrical scholar Chung Ming-teh pointed out that members of small \nexperimental theater groups lack professional training in the first place.  \nIf they participate in social movements before they achieve professional \nfinesse, they may obtain some sense of achievement, but they are only par-\nticipating in the movements in an indirect way, and subsequently will have \nto give up this method of participation.\n     This is quite true, because during the past year the number of exper-\nimental theater groups in Taiwan reduced sharply and remaining ones have \nbeen forced to rethink their positions and directions.\n     Despite the expansion and development of the 1970s, which earned small \nexperimental theater the epithet \"star of tomorrow,\" when it concerns the \nquality of perfomers and the performing environment, it is still the most \narid field in the art world of Taiwan.  Thus after a glorious decade, the \nexperimental theater became dormant last year.\n     Nevertheless what is heart-warming is that during the decade a few \ntheater groups became mature, experienced and even found a sense of direc-\ntion.\n     For instance some of the performances introduced by \"Mew Image\" in-\ncluded top perfomers and artists who showed considerable professionalism.\n     \"Performer's Workshop\" and \"The Screen\" employ permanent performers, \nprofessional directors and uses scripts written by art college students, \nto create continuous change.\n     Mr. Lee' director of the \"Screen\" lamented that even though he had \nbeen performing in plays for more than 10 years, not even one professional \ntheater group was established in Taiwan.  He said that this was because \nnobody had thought of running a theater group as a serious business enter-\nprise.\n     Mr. Lee has since quit his lilustrious performance career in the movie \nand TV, to dedicate his efforts solely to the theater.\n     Since the beginning of this year, theater enthusiasts such as Lee Kuo-\nShiu, Lee Li-chun and Lai Shen-chuan have been working hard to inspire the \ntheater in Taiwan.  Moreover, with Lin Hwai-ming determined to revive past \nglories, his dance troupe \"Cloud Gate\" is now performing again after years \nof inaction.  All this indicates that the theater in Taiwan is about to \nenter a new era.\n     Most of the experimental theater groups are facing financial hardship, \nand according to Mr. Chung Ming-teh, who is concerned about the growth and \ndevelopment of theater, the financial hardships of the groups will not be \neased within the next five to 10 years.  He believes that only those \ngroups in the forming production companies can survive.  Professional \ntheater groups, with permanent performing venues and proper administration \norganization, can only be sustained by government subsidies or corporate \nsponsorships.\n     \"The Screen\" avows to become the first professional theater company in \nTaiwan, and it has now obtained a permanent \"home.\"  It is also gradually \nbuilding up its own data archives, and trying to rehearse and perform \nsystematically and on a long term basis.\n     At the moment, is reinvesting all its box-office earnings to pur-\nchase lighting, sound systems and stage equipment.  It plans to do an is-\nlandwide tour, and if need be, will set up its own temporary stages."}, {"title": "英文 T20", "content": "     \"The run-up to my trip (to Beijing) leads me to be concerned that \nthey're going in the wrong direction with these detentions of dissidents,\" \nhe said on Wednesday in Australia.\n     Christopher voiced his \"strong distaste\" for the roundups and said \nthey may end up quashing plans to broaden Washington's security dialogue \nwith Beijing.\n     The lates known detention was on Tuesday when police accused ex-\nstudent leader Wang Dan of rumormongering and warned he would \"pay the \nprice\" if his outspokenness hurt mainland China.\n     Foreign Minister Qian Qichien flatly rejects the formula of MFN in \nexchange for human rights progress.\n     \"Human rights and MFN are completely separate issues,\" Qian reiterat-\ned on Sunday.  \"We strongly oppose linking them.\"\n     The Foreign Ministry on Tuesday rebuked Clinton for voicing sympathy \nfor mainland China's dissidents.\n     \"No foreign country, organization or individual has the right to make \nirresponsible remarks or interfere,\" a ministry statement said.\n     Ask virtually any businessman, Chinese or American, whether mainland \nChina should enjoy most favored nation tariffs and the answer is emphatic \n--yes.\n     Sino-U.S. trade is blooming at a world-beating clip and U.S. busines-\nses, whose exports to mainland China reached US$8 billion last year, are \nfinally piercing formidable barriers to reach mainland China's 1.2 billion \nincreasingly wealthy consumers.\n     America is even more crucial to mainland China, sucking up 34 percent \nof all Chinese exports in 1993--about U.S. worries about a 25 percent leap \nin its bilateral trade deficit to US$23 billion.\n     Hong Kong would also be throttled by a trade war.  Officials in the \nBritish colony that finance and manage much of mainland China's trade said \nof mainland China's MFN status would cost 75,000 local jobs, swipe up to \nnine percent from the territory's overall trade and slash 3.1 percentage \npoints from its economic growth rate.\n     Editorials Reunifying the Chinese language.  At a recent symposium on \nthe Chinese language held in Taiwan, participat scholar urged that people \non the two sides of the Taiwan Strait should strive to reunify the written \nform of the Chinese language.  The call deserves a hearty response.\n     Until 221 B.C., when the first emperor of the Chin-dynasty unified \nChina, many different forms of the Chinese language existed in China.  Uni-\nfication of the country created a need to unify the language.  The emperor \nordered a common written form be used throughout the land.\n     The move, tyrannical as it may seem from the viewpoint of modern peo-\nple, proved a gread success and a blessing for the Chinese.\n     The use of a common written language has enabled people in various \nareas of the country to communicate with one another with little difficulty \ndespite the different ways they talk.\n     The people in Shandong, for example, talk as differently from the \npeople in Guangdong as the English do from the French.  The people from the \nFrench.  The people from the two provinces can make themselves understood \neasily by writing.\n     The unification of written Chinese, therefore, has been tremendously \nhelpful in keeping the nation united throughout the centuries.\n     However, the Chinese Communists' attempt to revolutionize the Chinese \nlanguage by adopting a simplified form of the language has created a lin-\nguistic split.\n     Used on the mainland now is a simplified system much different from \nthe traditional characters, which are used in Taiwan.  The difference is \nnot so great as to make either system entirely unrecognizable to users \nof the other system.  Yet those who habitually use one do have considerable \ndifficulty understanding passages written in the other form.  This, without \na doubt, is one of the obstacles to Chinese reunification.\n     The reasons given by Peking for adopting the simplified characters \nare that the simplification facilitates the writing and learning of the \nlanguage and learning of the language and helps reduce illiteracy.\n     Illiteracy is as widespread as ever on the mainland since the sim-\nplification of the characters.  In Taiwan, where the traditional chara-\ncters are used, fewer than 6 percent of the people are illiterate, and the \nvast majority of these individuals grew up before the island reverted from \nJapanese rule to China in 1945.\n     The simplified characters, besides being deprived of the artistic \nvalue of the traditional characters, are actually harder to read.  The \nreason is simple.  Simplification of the strokes has made the characters \nlook more similar, so distinguishing one from the other has become more \ndifficult.\n     And in this era of the computer, when the need to write in long-\nhand is decreasing rapidly, simplification of the characters hardly makes \nwriting easier and faster.\n     We hope that authorities on the mainland will abandon the use of the \nsimplified characters and re-adopt the much more beautiful traditional \ntraditional form of the language.  Persisting in using the simplified \ncharacters is hampering the reunification of the nation.\n     Intel introduces new Pentium processors.  Intel Corp. introduced new \nPentium processors running at frequencies of 90 and 100 MHz.  The 100-MHz \nversion of the Pentium processor runs todays'PC software faster than any \nother processor available.  It is 50 percent faster than the original \nversion announced one year ago and has an iCOMP index rating of 815 with \na SPECint92 rating of 100.0.\n     These new Pentium processors will be manufactured in volume at Intel's \nmost advanced microprocessor production factories in Ireland and Santa \nClara.  With the 3.3 volt, 0.6 micron, 4-layer metal advanced process tech-\nnology, the size of other members of the Pentium processor family.  This \nannouncement comes on the heels of demonstrating a 150-MHz Pentium proces-\nsor at the IEEE International Solid State Circuits Conference (ISSCC) in \nSan Francisco approximately two weeks ago."}, {"title": "英文 T21", "content": "     Finally, of course, just as it is possible to have any number of \nindexes over a given stored file, so it is equally possible to have any \nnumber of pointer chains running through a given stored file.  A common \ncompression technique is thus to replace each individual data value by some \nrepresentation of the difference between it and the value that immediately \nprecedes it.  Another possible compression technique for this set of data \nis simply to eliminate all trailing blanks.  Further rear compression can \nbe achieved by dropping all characters to the right of the one required to \ndistinguish the entry in question from its two immediate neighbors.\n     We were very pleased to receive your inquiry or January 15 and \nenclose our illustrated catalogue and print-list giving the details you ask \nfor.  Also by separate post we are sending you a full range of samples and, \nwhen you have had an opportunity to examine them, feel confident you will \nagree that the goods are both excellent in quality and very reasonable in \nprice.  We invite your attention to our other nylon products, details of \nwhich you will find in the catalog, and look forward to receiving your \nfirst order.\n     Will you please send me a copy of your current typewriter catalog \nand pricelist?  I am particularly interested in portables, but require a \nmachine suitable for fairly heavy duty including stencil-cutting.  Enclose \nthe catalog of typewriters for which you ask in your letter of February 8.  \nIt includes particulars of portables by a number of makes.  We think the \nOlivetti \"Studio 44\" is a machine that would suit your purpose very well.  \nThis machine weighs 15 lbs. and is heavier than most portables, but even so \nit is conveniently portable when carried in its case.  A type-face suitable \nfor stencil-cutting could be supplied.  We have one of these machines in \nstock and, if it is convenient for you to call, we shall be pleased to let \nyou try it.\n     The \"China Post\" has recommended your form as being interested in \nestablishing business relations with a Chinese firm for the purpose of \nselling various products of your country and importing Chinese manufactured \ngoods.  We specialize in central Europe trade, but we have had no contact \nwit your country.  We address this letter to you in order to ascertain \nwhether cooperation to the advantage of both our firms could be \nestablished.  We invite you to send us details and prices, possibly also \nsamples, of such goods as you would be principally interested in selling, \nand we shall gladly study the sales possibilities in our market.\n     On the other hand, please favor us with a list of those articles you \nare interested in obtaining from here, so that we might be in a position \nto quote on same and give you all the necessary information regarding su-\npply possibilities.  We enclose herewith a list of goods for which we are \nrepresenting the leading Chinese manufacturers for their exports or which \nwe are now in a position to supply you from prime sources at the lowest \npossible prices.  This list consists of only a limited selection of the \ngoods which our various departments are regularly supplying, but we are \nfully equipped to handle your needs for any merchandise whatsoever.\n     Having already had experience in marketing products similar to your \nown we are familiar with customers' needs and are confident we could de-\nvelop a good market for you in Scotland, we have spacious and well-equipped \nshowrooms, not only at our Glasgow headquarters, but also in Edinburgh and \nPerth, and an experienced staff of sales representatives who would push \nyour business.  We should be glad to learn that you are interested in our \nproposal and on what terms you would be willing to conclude an agreement.  \nOur Mr. Wormald would then call on you to discuss details, he will be \nvisiting your town in two weeks' time.\n     After studying the catalog and price list received with your letter \nof March 31, we have no doubt we could obtain good orders for many of the \nitems, but feel you are placing both yourselves and us at a disadvantage by \nadopting a cash-settlement basis.  Nearly all business here is done on \ncredit, the period varying from three to six months.  Your prices on the \nother hand are reasonable and your manufactures sound in both design and \nquality.  We therefore suggest that you could afford to raise your prices \nsufficiently to cover the cost and fall into line with your competitors in \nthe matter of credit.  In our experience, to do so would be sound policy \nand greatly strengthen your hold on the market.  With the best will in the \nworld to serve you, we are afraid it would be neither worth your while, nor \nours, to continue business on a cash basis.\n     Would you please estimate for installing central heating in the \nbungalow at I Margggate Road, St. Annes-on-Sea.  I enclose a specification \nand a plan showing required positions of radiators.  Cost is of course a \nmatter of some importance but, as you will gather from the specification.  \nI am interested only in first-class workmanship and in the use of best qua-\nlity materials.  Completion of the work is required by August 31 at the la-\ntest, and I shall be glad if in your reply you will give me a firm comple-\ntion date so that I can make arrangements for our removal from London.\n     An estimated 25 other passengers on the British-chartered, Belgian-\nregistered bus were injured, some seriously, police said.  A police \nofficial in Neumarkt, 220 kilometers southwest of Vienna, said the bus \nsideswiped the tanker just north of Neumarkt, along a curving, rain-slicked \nstretch of highway in Austria's southern Alps.  The left front of the bus \nwas torn off in the collision.  The tanker was damaged, but it was not \nclear if its driver was among the injured."}, {"title": "英文 T22", "content": "     Thank you for your letter of July 5 enclosing specification and plan \nfor a solid fuel central heating system at the above address.  We should be \nglad to carry out the work for a total of 360, with a 2 discount allowance \nfor settlement within one month of the date of our account.  We can promise \nto complete all work by the end of August if we receive your instructions \nby the end of this month.  We would stress that the price quoted is based \non present costs of materials and labour.  Should these costs rise we would \nhave to add the increased costs to our price.\n     We have installed many similar heating systems in your area.  Our \nreputation for highclass work is well known, but if you would like to \ninspect one of our recent installations before deciding to accept our \nestimate, we would try to arrange this for you.  If, as we hope, you are \nsatisfied with the price quoted, please let us have your instruction soon.  \nThis would help us in making our other arrangements.\n     Tenders are invited for the supply to the Council's power station \nat Bamford, during the year 1994 of approximately 2,000 tons of best qua-\nlity furnace coke, delivered in quantities as required.  Tenders must be \nsubmitted on the official form obtainable from County Hall to reach the \nClerk of the Council not later than 900 noon on Friday, the 30th of June.  \nThe council do not bind themselves to accept the lowest, or any, of the \ntenders submitted.  Having read the terms and conditions in the official \nform supplied by you, I enclose my tender for the supply of coke to the \nBamford power station during 1944 and shall be glad to learn that it has \nbeen accepted.\n     Referring to our sales contract, dated October 25, we are pleased to \ninform you that we have sent to you the samples of the AM/FM cassette tape \nrecorder by are freighter.  The shipment will be made ten days earlier than \nthat in the contract.  We have received a letter on October 19 fro your \nbranch office, Macao, enclosing a specimen of guarantee cards for the \nphonographs of your order No. 904.  We will have them printed without \ndelay.  We have business relationship with all the large plywood producers \nand door makers.  You may be certain that you will find their products \ncompetitive against those of other countries.  Almost all the building \nmaterials here are exported to the United States, Japan and Southeast Asian \nCountries.\n     I beg to send you by express a few little articles belong to you \nwhich my little sister Annie found in her room.  Your somewhat hurried de-\nparture made both of us unable to remind you to bring them in time.  This, \nnevertheless, furnished me plausible excuse for writing to you.  The house \nseemed quite deserted since you left.  We all miss you.  I believe my sis-\nter is writing you something more than those expressed in this letter by \nme.  Please accept my gift as you accept my respect, and that it may please \nyou and couse you think of me sometimes.  And I beg to add that in choosing \nthe trifling present I have been happy enough to understand or guess your \ntastes.\n     You made me the sweet confession of your love and your parents, to \nwhom I have communicated my sentiments, have received my vows kindly.  I \nshould be happy with all this, yet there is one thing wanting.  Although my \nthoughts, which are always on you, present me with the feature of her who \nhas my heart, yet I am desirous of possessing her portrait.  It deems a \nnecessity to have this token of love while I am contemplating the person \nwhom I love and who is to give happiness to my existence.  I am sure you \nwill not refuse my urgent request.  It's wonderful to get a nice long \nletter from you, starting my day right.  A picture of mine for which you \nasked will be found in this letter.\n     There I go talking in song title again!  \"As long as I'm with \nyou.\"  Remember?  It was on the air the other night, and we listened to it \ntogether.  I think it's a lovely song and the words sound almost as though \nthey were written for us.  Are you still so busy?  You didn't say in your \nletter whether you are still working late, like last week.  Now, with my \npicture in front of you, you will keep my advice in mind, \"health is \nwealth.\"  It is always so nice to find a letter from you when I come home \nfrom work, especially today, the one enclosing a nice picture of yours.  I \nread your letter over and over again and each time I did so, I heard your \nvoice calling me within my heart.\n     To speak frankly, I am not rich.  I have no car, no house and no \ntreasure to give you, but I have a great love for you.  The only thing I \nwant is to have you with me.  All I want is to make you happy, and I will \ndo my best to make you happy, and I will do my best to make a happy home \nfor us I am waiting for your acceptance!  I shall see you tomorrow evening, \nbut let me have one line if possible before then.  I am writing this letter \nto reach you the first thing on your birthday.  Many many happy returns of \nthe day, dearest Ellen.  When your next birthday comes round I hope I shall \nnot have to write you my good wishes!\n     Belfast-Taiwan's Hualon Corporation and a British government \nagency formally launched a controversial textile factory project outside \nBelfast yesterday, the biggest investment in Northern Ireland for 12 \nyears.\n     Under the scheme, a 160 million pound sterling (US$240.8 million) \nfactory will be built with 60 million pounds (US$90.32 million) from the \ngovernment's Industrial Development Board (IDB), its biggest investment \nsince its foundation in 1982.\n     \"This is excellent industrial news,\" Northern Ireland's Economy \nMinister Tim Smith said at a ground-breaking ceremony at Mallusk, on the \noutskirts of Belfast."}, {"title": "英文 T23", "content": "     It is my pleasure to learn from Mr. J. R. Miller, our business friend, \nof Hudson / Company of your city, that you have a well-established company \nand represent a good number of manufacturers of other countries and you \nhave had very good business connections with all the large African import-\ners of hand tools and hardware.  Our company is the sales agent for the \nlargest hand tool company of Taiwan.  I am pleased to enclose for your re-\nference a list of the hand tools that our principal procures.  Our catalog, \nfree samples and the price list including your commission, will be sent to \nyou as soon as we hear from you.\n     The industries of Taiwan have produced a good variety of products for \nthe world market in recent years, and the total exports amounted to us$ \n5,600 million in 1974.  According to a reliable source, the total will be \nabout 6,500 million dollars this year.  You will see that Taiwan's products \nare competitive against those produced in other countries.  Recently two \nJapanese department stores have sent their buyers to Taiwan for competitive \ncommodities.  Many foreign buyers are here booking their requirements every \nday.  And, buying offices and agents of foreign importers are increasing in \nTaipei City.  You will see that many Japanese products are not as \ncompetitive as they have been and that it may be the time for you to start \nthe push of the sales of Taiwan's products in your market.\n     So far as I know, electrical and electronic products find a very good \nmarket in your country.  Should you find the two lines of business inter-\nesting, just let me know.  You will be interested to hear that we have been \nable to obtain a further supply of Bragilian Coffee of the same quality as \nthat supplied to you last year.  The total consignment is only 1,000 lb., \nand we are pleased to offer it to you at 300 per lb.  With the increase in \nfreight costs which become effective this week, the next consignment will \nbe dearer, so we recommend you to take prompt advantage of this offer, \nwhich is firm for three days only.\n     Many auto manufacturers everywhere are scurrying to fill the demand \nfor diesel powered sedans in this day of energy shortage.  Mercedes-Benz \nhas pioneered in this field since 1936 with the introduction of the 260D \ndiesel sedan.  And today Mercedes-Benz diesel sedans are the most admired \nand sought after cars.  Other manufacturers are also rushing to meet the \ndemand.  Taiwan presently ( as far as I know ) only manufactures one model \ndiesel sedan, the Yue Loong 2200.  I'm sure it would constitute a blessing \nto the consumers and the energy conscious Industrial Development Bureau to \ndevelop and manufacture more fuel-efficient diesel cars in Taiwan, or at \nleast, to encourage their importation over the importation of gasoline \npowered models.  I can suggest some excellent choices:  Mercedes-Benz die-\nsels.\n     My office is situated at Chung Shan N. Rd. Sec. 2 and my house is at \nChung Shan N. Rd. Sec. 6, Tein Mou.  Everyday except holidays I open my \noffice at 9:30 a.m. and close at 7:00 p.m.  There is only one bus route \nNo.220 operating between my house and office.  In the morning the said bus \nis very crowded, but one manages to get into it, but in the evening it is \nvery very crowded and one cannot get into it.  It is very surprising that \nthere are many bus routes to Shih Lin and many of them are empty in the \nevening hours, for example route No.269,301,310 etc.  I hope you will \npublish my letter in your newspaper to attract the attention of the \ntransport department.\n     As foreign residents in Taipei we have been happy to see the great \nprogress made by this island nation.  Progress under President Chiang \ntowards democratization has been noteworthy, while economic and social \nprogress has been especially impressive.  Naturally we were happy to see \nsimilar cultural progress then the organization of the Taipei Philharmonic \nOrchestra was announced.  It seemed that Taipei was about to make another \nstep towards becoming the world city it should be, towards growing up \nmusically.  The several concerts given last year by the orchestra were full \nof promise; excellent progress in the difficult task of building an \norchestra was being made.  We were consequently very surprised and saddened \nwhen the Taipei Philharmonic Foundation precipitously decided to dissolve \nthe orchestra.\n     In spite of many rumors that your newspaper reported, the directors of \nthe foundation have only claimed that their funds were insufficient.  Why \nthen did they refuse a substantial grant offered by the Ministry of \nEducation, and why did they allow the orchestra to perform charity concerts \nfor the Police Radio Station and the Taipei City Music Season?  Why were \nfunds from other sources not sought, thus making it a real community \norchestra?  As each concert was sold out, why were more concerts no \nscheduled to increase ticket income?  And finally, if funds were not \nsufficient to support the orchestra, why are the foundation's directors now \ntrying to expand the range of activities they wish to support?  These are \nquestions we find difficult to answer.\n     \"It demonstrates clearly that Northern Ireland is a powerful \ncontender for inward investment from the Far East... The province is now \ninternationally recognized as an ideal foothold for servicing the \nEuropean and other markets.\"\n     The launch capped four years of negotiations to bring a major job-\ncreating project to the province but it has been dogged by allegations \nthat the project is uneconomic and resulted from political pressure from \nthe British government.\n     It also evoked memories of an ill-starred project by John De Lorean \nto build futuristic cars in Northern Ireland.  The plan collapsed because \nof irregularities and a lack of cash in 1978."}, {"title": "英文 T24", "content": "     Instead of changing the purpose of the Taipei Philharmonic Foundation \nand thus forcing others to duplicate their already well-accomplished work, \nwould it not be easier for some of the foundation directors to retire honor-\nably and to let others interested in Taipei's musical progress continuer \nthe foundation's excellent beginnings?  We are sure that the many problems \nthe orchestra has faced can be overcome with sufficient diligence and pa-\ntience.  I am sure you will be interested to hear that I am going to marry \nagain.  Since Louise's death I have been very lonely, and although I shall \nnever forget her I feel she would not wish me to be without companionship \nfor the rest of my life.\n     My fiancee, Mrs. Helen Tseng, was widowed two years ago, and now wants \nto make a new life.  I am sure we are going to be very happy.  We plan to \nbe married quietly on July 2, and of course I hope very much that you will \nbe able to come.  I beg to send you by express a few little articles belong \nto you which my little sister Annie found in her room.  Your somewhat hur-\nried departure made both of us unable to remind you to bring them in time.  \nThis, nevertheless, furnished me a plausible excuse for writing to you.  \nThe house seemed quite deserted since you left.  We all miss you.  I be-\nlieve my sister is writing you something more than those expressed in this \nletter by me.\n     What a delightful surprise to receive your Christmas card!  It was \nso very, very kind of you to remember me.  Our casual acquaintance on the \nbus was so short.  I shouldn't have thought for a moment that you would \nremember.  Each time I look at the lovely card, I am warmed by thoughts of \nthat night.  It was so good to have some one to talk to, someone who was \ninteresting and gracious.  I regret deeply that we did not have more time \nto talk.  I feel I have been enriched, both culturally and personally.  I \ntold my family of our visit-that night on bus, and they were happy too; \nhappy that I had met and spoken with so fine a man.  To my students in \nschool I mentioned only that I had met and talked with a man from Taiwan.  \nBeing a teacher of Geography, I made very sure that each child learned \nsomething about Taiwan, where it is, its population, products, and what \nlittle I know of its beauties.  I wish that you could speak to my class!  \nWhat a treat that world be!\n     I take genuine pleasure in recommending Mr. Joseph Chen as office \nmanager in your organization.  For many years I have known him personally, \nas well as his family, and during his five years in business I have follow-\ned his progress with much interest.  He is a young man of culture and \neducation.  I honestly believe that your firm would be fortunate in \nobtaining his service.  I am giving this letter to Howard Wu a very good \nfriend of mine.  He is going to be in Cincinnati to live with his uncle for \nsome time, and I urge him to pay a visit to you.  Howard is a handsome and \ntalented young man and I feel certain you will enjoy to meet him.  He has \nnever been in the United States; and I'll be very grateful for any help and \nadvice you can give him.  I know he, too, will deeply appreciate any \ncourtesy.\n     My sister-in-law, Jane Liu, will be in Chicago all next week at the \nPlaza Hotel.  I've often wanted you two to meet, and this seems like the \nideal opportunity.  Jane is at the research board of Kuo Hwa Advertising \nAgency.  I told her about the book Mr. Chen is writing on the Market Survey \nand she was completely fascinated-as I know she would be!  She said she'd \nlike to know more about the American marketing situations; and I think your \nhusband and you might find it interesting to talk with her.  So call her if \nyou can-but not if it's an inconvenience.  If she doesn't hear from you, \nshe'll understand that it's because you both are tied up.  I suggest this \nmeeting only because I know you and she are bound to enjly each other's \ncompany.  Please forward my kindest regards to Mr. Chen.\n     I take liberty of asking you to interest yourself in my son.  I am \nvery desirous of obtaining a clerkship for him and entertain the hope that \nyou would perhaps kindly remember if a vacancy should occur in your bank.  \nAlthough I am aware you must have many applications of this nature, I can-\nnot resist taking the chance of its being in your power to do something for \nus in this way.  Attached to this letter is a brief statement of his exper-\niences and I hope you will like to have it.  How grateful I should be if \nyou would consider this request.\n     I beg to certify that Miss Annie Lu for three years a teacher English \nin this institution, that she has shown diligence and ability in the dis-\ncharge of her duties, and that she has enjoyed the esteem alike of her \ncolleagues and pupils.  Miss Lu was a student in several of my courses \nduring her undergraduate career at National Cheng Kung University between \n1974 and 1978, and in those courses she distinguished herself by the qua-\nlity of her analytical ability.  In extra-curricular activities, her per-\nformance we equally impressive.\n     Taiwan-based Hualon says the factory will employ 1,800 people when \ncompleted in nine years' time and produce both dye and fabrics for the \nEU and American markets, substitution imports from the Far East which are \nsubject to trade quotas.\n     But the prospect of more jobs in an economy with an unemployment rate \nof more than 13 percent has been tempered by misgivings over the factory's \neconomic justification raised in articles in the respected Financial Times \nof London.\n     It argued that the market for synthetic fabrics in Europe was shrink-\ning and said the European Commission, which vets state-aided projects, had \nonly reluctantly approved it because of heavy political pressure from Lon-\ndon."}, {"title": "英文 T25", "content": "     Taking exercise is good for our health.  By taking exercise, we can \nrelax our body and mind.  We'll be in good spirits afterwards and then can \nwork better.  At the same time, we can strengthen our muscles and trim our \nfigures.  If we don't take exercise for long, we may easily fall ill or get \nfat.\n     I enjoy several outdoor sports.  To begin with, I like swimming in the \nsea.  Swimming itself is not only a survival skill but a good way to put \nall the muscles at play.  Swimming in the sea is especially enjoyable, \nsince you're close to nature and away from the crowds in a swimming pool.  \nPlaying badminton is also fun.  It is a sport for my whole family.  Early \nin the morning, my parents and I like to seek a quiet place in the nearby \npark for playing badminton.  Besides, I enjoy dancing.  Social dance is \ngraceful but what really appeals to me is aerobics.  I always sweat a lot \nand feel good about myself after a rigorous workout.\n     I've always been a basketball fan since my childhoods.  I've been \ncrazy about such NBA superstars as Michael Jordan, Magic Johnson, Chamber-\nlain, and so on.  Even though I am only medium in height and build, I prac-\ntice playing basketball a lot and luckily I was picked as a member on my \nschool team.  We have a very strong basketball team in the northern school \ndistrict and received several championships in the basketball tournaments.\n     It takes a great deal of strength and skill to play basketball well.  \nCertainly, the more you practice, the better skill you will have.  Because \nof my height, I play front.  As a front, I have to cover my teammates, or \nblock or opponents.  I am good at dribbling and I am also a nice three-\npoint shooter.  Basketball is my favorite sport, and most importantly, I \nmake some friends by playing games on the court.\n     The traffic problems in Taiwan have become more and more serious be-\ncause some people don't abide by traffic rules.  Many terrible accidents \nresult from reckless driving.  Let's take a look at what's happening on the \nstreets.  Drunk drivers weave in and out, showing no respect for (to) other \npeople's lives.  Speeding is forbidden but is (a) common practice in Tai-\nwan.  Pedestrians are at risk while jaywalking.  The pervasive disregard of \ntraffic regulations makes people killers or victims.\n     Ensuring the safety of every citizen's life should become our top pri-\nority.  In my opinion, we should, first, severely punish those drivers or \nriders who don't obey traffic rules.  Traffic violators are responsible for \nthe confusion of the road.  Next our law should be strictly enforced; thus, \nthose drivers or motorcyclists will think twice before running (through) a \nred light or parking their cars at random.  As for pedestrians, they should \nnot jaywalk at will, or they should be punished too.  In all, only through \nthe cooperation of both the driver and the pedestrian can safer traffic \nconditions be achieved.\n     Our environment is danger of serious pollution.  However, many alar-\nming reports in newspapers seem to have little effect on the offenders. As \nwe know, most rivers in Taiwan today are polluted and the marine life is \nendangered, but still many factories and families discharge chemical and \nhuman waste into the rivers.  Also, more and more people are suffering from \nrespiratory diseases but cars and factories still keep emitting smoke.  \nWhat's worse, piles of garbage have been an eyesore and a nuisance (a \nsource of epidemics (diseases)) but so far we haven't done much about it.  \n[Taiwn has been polluted to such a degree that many people cannot stand it \nand some of them bring up several methods to improve this.]\n     There are many ways for us to protect our environment.  First, every-\none should share the consensus (/have a common sense) that environmental \nprotection is everybody's business.  Besides, engineers as well as our \ngovernment should find out the pollution sources and think (figure) out \nproper ways to halt the pollution.  Finally, strict anti-pollution laws \nshould be passed and enforced as soon as possible and heavy fines should be \nimposed on those offenders.  A clean and healthy environment is good for \neveryone and it is a goal that we should work hard for.\n     Food is not cooked on the airplane.  It is cooked either in the air-\nline's own kirchens located near the airports or by special caterers in \ndifferent cities.  Private catering companies deliver the prepared meals \nto the airplanes, where they are help warm and ready to serve in the galley \nof each plane.\n     Miss Wang turns on the public address system to speak to the pass-\nengers.  \"We will be passing out special immigration and custom declaration \ncards to all of you.  Please fill these out before we land.  You must give \nthem to the immigration and customs officials at the airport.  We'll be \nhappy to help you fill them out, thank you.\"  On most commercial airlines, \nannouncements to passengers are in English.  When the plane belongs to the \nairline of a country where another language is spoken.  The declaration \ncards ask for information about the passengers that is needed by the \nimmigration and tourism officials.\n     Smith denied the charges in 1,000-work letter to the paper and said \nyesterday that he had \"every confidence in Hualon\" and added:  \"every pro-\nject IDB supports involves a degree of risk.\"\n     The factory mirrors a similar Hualon operation in Malaysia but the \ncompany believes competitive power and land costs will outweigh the fact \nthat northern Ireland labor costs are 15 percent higher than in Asia and \ngive it a European foothold.\n     We are quite certain that we can produce products here that will be \ninternationally competitive for Europe and the US, Hualon Vice-chairman \nAndrew Oung told a news conference."}, {"title": "英文 T26", "content": "     If I were a millionair (billionaire), I would divide my wealth into \ntwo parts so that I can do what I desire to do.  With the first part of \nmoney, I would try to make my life better.  First, I would buy a personal \ncomputer.  In this modern world, a PC is necessary for the people who want \nto learn the latest information.  Besides, a student can educate himself/ \nherself through the help of many useful softwares.  Second, I would buy \ngood dictionaries and books worth reading, through which I can not only \nimprove my grades but also broaden my vision.  Third, I would decorate my \nstudy to have myself a comfortable and pleasant place where I could read \nefficiently.\n     With the rest part of money, I would try my best to help the weak and \nneedy people.  It is a good idea to build a hospital to take care of the \npeople who are sick and a school to educate the youth who could not afford \nto go to school.  As regards the orphans, they are help-less so that I \nwould raise a fund for them to improve their life.  Also, I would contri-\nbute some money to a charity fund to do more social work.  In addition, if \nmy friends were in need of financial aid, I would help them as much as I \ncould.  (All) in all, were I a millionaire (billionaire), I would do a lot \nof good things to make sure that I would not live my life in vain.\n     If I were given a million dollars, I would first buy my father a set \nof hi-fi stereo.  Father is every inch a classical music fan but he listens \nto it on the radio.  You can imagine how awful a magnificent piece of music \ncould turn out!  Sound distortions always ruin the joy and in-between com-\nmercials spoil the continuity of a symphony.  If I had so much money, I \nwould use a certain amount of it to buy Father a good set of stereo with \nhigh fidelity and crystal-clear sound effects.\n     As you pass by the railway station, you'll probably see some handi-\ncapped people begging for money under the sun or in the rain.  You'll see \nelder people strolling around, with emptiness in their eyes.  Also, there \nare some homeless lying around.  So if I were a millionaire, I would let \nthe handicapped be taken good care of.  I would build shelters for the \nhomeless and well-equipped elderly homes for the elders and I would build \nshelters for the homeless and well-equipped elderly homes for the elders \nand I would set up a good education program for the homeless teenagers so \nthat can start on their own someday.  With the money I have, I hope I could \nbuild a brighter future for the poor and needy people around me.\n     After five strong quakes Monday, northern Taiwan was jolted again yes-\nterday (May 24) with four more, including one that registered a 6.2 on the \nRichter scale around noon, the Central Weather Bureau said.\n     In addition to the large quakes, there have been more than 300 minor \nearthquakes that could not be felt without sensors during a 32-hour period \nstretching from early Monday until last night.\n     The first of yesterday's four strong quakes measured 6.2 on the Rich-\nter scale, shaking northern Taiwan at 12:04 p.m.  Just 9 minutes later, the \nsecond quake struck, measuring 5.3 on the scale.  The third quake struck 25 \nminutes later, measuring 5.5 on the scale.  Then at 1:48 p.m. the fourth \none hit, measuring 5.7 on the scale.\n     Almost all nine of the earthquakes felt on Monday and Tuesday were \ncentered about 100 km off the coast of Hualien, bureau officials said.  On \nMay 20, 1986, an earthquake struck 15 km northwest of Hualien.  That quake \nkilled one person and injured 11.\n     People fear this week's spate of earthquakes indicates a Big one is \ncoming.  But Yeh Yung-tien, director of the Graduate School of Seismology \nat Chungcheng University, said this continuous earthquake activity off the \ncoast of Hualien was normal.\n     There were reports of falling stones around Sanhsia area, northern \nTaiwan, but no casualties were reported.  Yesterday's first quake measured \n3 on the Richter scale in Taipei.  Many citizens were alarmed by it.  De-\npartment of Rapid Transit Systems employees who work on the 13th floor of \ntheir building were screaming.  So were city employees who work in the new \ncity hall near the World Trade Center.\n     Yeh Cheng, assistant manager of Howard Shyline Restaurant on the 44th \nand 45th floors of the 51-story Shin Kong Mitsukoshi building, Taiwan's \ntallest building, on Chunghsiao West Road, said the shaking \"felt like I \nwas in a cradle.\"\n     One of nature's most terrifying forces is a powerful earthquake.  A \nsevere tremble will cause a nearly complete sense of helplessness.  It \nwould seem there is nothing you can do but pray that you will survive.\nBut, in fact, there are several steps you can take, before, during and \nafter a quake, which will help assure your survival.\n     If you live in an area prone to earthquakes you should prepare in ad-\nvance a survival kit consisting of fresh water, first-aid supplies, and \ncanned goods.  It is also helpful to have a plan of action for an evacu-\nation procedure, then; in the event of an actual earthquake, you should \ntake shelter under a desk or table, away from windows, or alternatively, \nunder a door frame.  When the tremor has ended, if it is a major quake, you \nshould turn off your source of gas, as this is a dangerous fire threat a \ndangerous fire threat and post-earthquake fires can be as damaging as the \nquake itself.  If taken, these steps can help you live through a major \nearthquake.\n     Israel Radio said some 200 freed prisoners arrived in the autonomous \nGaza Strip by early evening.  The Mandela Institute, an East Jerusalem-\nbased Palestinian watchdog group, said Israel has freed only some 2,000 \nof the 5,000 prisoners it had agreed to release by now.  The agreements on \nprisoner releases were part of the Gaza/Jericho autonomy accord signed May \n4 in Cairo."}, {"title": "英文 T27", "content": "     I often went to the municipal library to review schoolwork on Wednes-\nday night.  Last Wed after studying, I happened to meet a friend in the \nstreet.  We had a wonderful time together until I found it would be too \nlate to catch the last bus if we kept chatting.\n     When I got on the bus, I found there were only four passengers.  All \nof them got off on the way while my destination was still far away.  It was \nthe terminal.  The bus driver began to behave strangely.  First, he drove \nat too low a speed and turned off the light in the bus.  Then he began to \ntalk to me rudely and cracked several dirty jokes.  I was frightened.  I \nsearched my mind what to do.  We were still on the road and though it was \nnear midnight, the streets were still brightened by car lights and neon-\nlights.  I pulled the bell strap and sternly wanted him to let me get off \nat the next stop.  My attitude was serious because I did not want to let \nhim sense my fear.  Perhaps my stern attitude prevented his further bother-\ning (harassment).  The fat, ugly-looking bus driver let me off.  I felt \nrelieved.  Then I called my father to pick me up.\n     I do not dare to imagine what would happen to me if the bus driver was \nas fierce and abnormal as those criminals described in the newspaper. From \nthen on, I still go to library but I know better than to take the last bus \nhome.\n     I live in Shu-Lin and I am a commuter because my school is in Taipei.  \nI have to take a train to school six days a week.  Usually there is no big \ntrouble in the early morning when I go out from Shu-Lin.  But it is hell-\nlike when I come home after school.  The train is always overcrowded like \nthe horrible scene in Tokyo Train Station where the passengers are pushed \nfrom the door by a \"push-man.\"  Taipei Train Station is no better than \nthat.  Even worse, people are ruder and the ait-conditioning is worse.  In \nwinter, I would make light of the discomfort by telling myself that it is \nwarmer to stick together.  But during the summer, all the oily, sticky and \nstinky bodies make up a horrible and \"steamy\" sight!\n     One day, I rushed to the station after school, hoping I would have a \nseat if I could arrive the platform earlier.  But many people were already \nthere like ants.  I was pushed in and kept being pushed all the way toward \nthe back.  Suddenly, someone stepped on my left shoe and it got stuck and \nleft out as I pulled back my left foot.  It was so packed that it was im-\npossible for me to turn around and bend to pick it.  Nevertheless, the \nwhole crowd kept moving forward like a river of no return.  I could not but \nyell:  \"my shoe, my shoe!\"  Somebody must hear and I heard him saying:  \"I \ngot it!  But where are you?\"  I answered, \"Here, right in the back!\"  After \na few seconds, I saw my deformed shoe being passed by the passengers, one \nby one, until it reached me.  I felt relieved and grateful and yet very em-\nbarrassed.  People grinned at me.  That was really an unforgettable exper-\nience.\n     In order to make a good impression during a job interview, you need to \nprepare yourself for the interviews.  You must arrive in plenty of time for \nthe interview, so that you give yourself a little time to relax before the \ninterview begins.\n     You have to look your best for the interview.  The impression you make \nas you walk into the room is very important.  Try to find out if the com-\npany or organization has any rules about dress.  Then try to dress accord-\ning to their regulations.  If they don't allow their staff to come to work \nin casual clothes, you may ruin your chances for a job by wearing the wrong \nsort of clothes to the interview.\n     You also need to plan what you are going to say.  In some interviews \nyou will have to do a lot of talking, and in others you will only have to \nanswer a few questions about your education and experience.  You need to \nhave answers ready about yourself, your school work, your strong points, \nyour reasons for applying for the job, and the salary you expect.\n     Air pollution has probably been with us since man discovered fird, \nbut it did not begin to be a problem until men began living in towns and \ncities, and had many fires burning at the same time within a small area.  \nToday, the air pollution problem is to found in every part of the world.  \nWe are told that policemen in Tokyo who direct cars during the busiest \ntimes of the day must stop to breathe pure oxygen from containers placed at \nstreet crossings.\n     One of the greatest problems facing the world today is the recent \nspectacular increase in the human population.  As a result the advance \nof medicine and the improvement in living standards, people are living \nlonger lives, and fewer infants are dying from disease.  This, of course, \nis a gratifying development, but it means that the world population is \nincreasing at a faster rate than are food supplies.\n     Some experts are very pessimistic and declare, that at the present \nrate of population increase, there will not be enough food for everybody \nwithin a hundred years.\n     John McGuckian, the IDB chairman, dismissed fears about the company \ncaused by charges of insider trading against two of the Oung brothers \nwhose family controls the company.\n     He said the charges were a matter of public knowledge and said neither \nman had anything to do with the project.  \"Our misgivings are allayed.\"\n     Oung Ta-ming, a former group chairman, has appealed against a three-\nand-a-half-year jail sentence for insider trading and has since been elect-\ned to Taiwan's parliament.\n     Oung Ta-ming, chairman of the textile subsidiary, went to Malaysia and \nhas not been tried.  \"Hualon Corporation is a highly-rated world class com-\npany,\" the IDB chairman said."}, {"title": "英文 T28", "content": "     It seems that drugs have become a plague in our society overnight.  \nNot only does the number of the drug users keep increasing but their aver-\nage age has been going down.  However, the government has failed to keep \npace with this growth in its efforts to treat these cases.  Drug abuse \noften leads to crimes.  Juvenile delinquency, for example, is an obvious \none.  Drugs can do alarming damage to both users  and our society.  Amphe-\ntamine, for instance, can harm cerebral nerve system.  And the illegal \npractice will threaten the supply of our future workforce.  There is a \nhardest lesson in Chin-Dynasty when popular opium consumption (use) des-\ntroyed the whole social morals.  It's time to call for a war on drugs.\n     The authorities concerned need to make more determined efforts so that \nthe illegal practice will decline eventually.  One of the solutions is the \nimposition of the harsh penalty on drug trafficking.  More important are \nefforts to help addicts kick the habit.  To establish halfway houses for \nthese addicts is necessary.  The war on drugs will require (call for ) the \nefforts of all citizens.\n     Quite a number of people had already lined up at the bus stop when I \narrived.  I walked up to the end of the waiting line, and was soon follow-\ned by many others.\n     Twenty minutes passed.  Finally, a bus with a full load of passengers \nslowed down and came to a stop in front of us.  Some of the impatient peo-\nple, breaking from the waiting line, rushed toward the bus.  A couple of \ngirl-students came out from no-where and pushed past me.  I was the last to \nget on board and was sandwiched between the door and one of that group of \ngirls.\n     That girl stood so close to me that I nearly smelled a slight pleasant \nfragrance of shampoo.  I felt my heart beat violently.  Suddenly, the dri-\nver put on the brakes at the intersection.  All the passengers moved for-\nward.  That girl fell into my arms!  My right hand got stuck between im-\nmovable bodies, so I used my left hand to help her up.  I felt the softness \nof her body.  How I wish time would stand still at the very moment!  To my \ndisappointment, the girl quickly stood on her feet again and gave me an an-\ngry stare.  What an unforgettable bus ride!\n     Tourism is a modern business, or industry.  In the days before in \n-expensive means of transportation, only the wealthy could travel for plea-\nsure.  Today, inexpensive and rapid means of travel make it possible for \nworking people to become tourists.  Leisure in the form of long weekends \nand paid vacation, or holidays, also make it possible for working people to \ntake trips.\n     Almost 80% of international tourists are from industrialized countr-\nies.  Millions of workers in northern countries take paid vacations in \nwarm, sunny places.  And workers in warm climates often travel to places \nwhere they can enjoy winter sports.  Many people also travel within their \nown country.  This is called internal tourism.  People from rural areas go \nto the cities for vacations; people from the city vacation in the country, \nat beaches, or in mountains.\n     Tourism is so important that there is a special government department \nfor tourism.  This department uses the information on the immigration cards \nto plan facilities for tourists.  Comfortable, clean, and convenient accom-\nmodations and travel facilities are necessary if tourists are to visit a \ncountry.\n     It is important that such accommodation are kept clean and in good \ncondition.  If a government dose not require licenses and inspections, \nthese places may not be kept up well.  The tourism department works with \nthe government and with tourism industry as a whole to build the country's \ntourism business.\n     When the people of a country know that tourism is necessary for their \ncountry's well-being and growth, they are happy to have tourists.  They are \npolite and eager to have the visitors enjoy themselves.  All the flight \nattendants work hard to make the passengers feel welcome aboard the plane.  \nThe passengers are the people riding in the airplane.  Most of these \npassengers ate tourists people who travel to see another places, area, or \ncountry.  Most tourists travel for pleasure.  They enjoy seeing different \nplaces and meeting other people.\n     Sue Wang is a stewardess and Sam Lee is a steward for their country's \nairline.  Their job is to look after the comfort and safety of the pass-\nengers who ride in the passenger cabin of the airplane during the flight.  \nOn the way, the cabin attendants serve food and beverages to the pass-\nengers.  This keeps the attendants busy on their feet during most of the \nflight.  Their duties also include welcoming the passengers aboard the \nairlines and helping them to find their seats.  A flight attendant must \nshow the passengers where the emergency exists are and explain how to use \nthe oxygen masks.  They must be able to answer questions about the time of \nflights and the services of the airlines.  A flight attendant has to enjoy \ntaking care of and serving people.  All flight attendant must be able to \ngive first-aid.\n     Eugene Chien, Taiwan's ambassador to Britain, said his wife had told \nhim to put on a bullet-proof jacket while visiting the province because of \na 25-year conflict which has killed 3,000.\n     \"But it is not like that.  It is a wonderful land a peaceful environ-\nment.  The quality of work is welltrained and the trade unions are ex-\ncellent.\"\n     VIENNA, Austria-Five people, including three American tourists, were \nkilled Thursday when their tour bus collided with a tank truck on a slipp-\nery Alpine mountain pass, police said."}, {"title": "英文 T29", "content": "     \"Blueprint For Prosperity\" With its burgeoning trade and high foreign \nexchange reserves, Taiwan is already known as an economic powerhouse in \nEast Asia.  Now a hung infrastructure program has been announced that will \nmake ita trend-setter in improving the quality of life as well.\n     In Taiwan's headlong rush to development over the past 30 years, in-\nfrastructure and lifestyle have been neglected.  Now, all that is going to \nchange under a mammoth US$300 billion six-year development plan designed to \nturn the island into a developed nation by the end of the century.\n     Main areas due to benefit under the plan are transportation and commu-\nnications, but provision of energy and improvement of the environment will \nalso receive large shares.\n     Because of the high-tech nature of many of the projects, a large pro-\nportion of the work needed under the plan will be done by foreign firms, \nand they are currently lining up for lucrative contracts.  In terms of \nspending, the Taiwan plan dwarfs that being taken by other countries, in-\ncluding the effort to rebuild Kuwait after the Gulf War, which only cost \nabout a quarter of the Taiwan total.\n     The 779 individual projects included in the overall plan will involve \npublic works spending of US$199 billion, covering everything from high-\nspeed railways to new universities.  And in view of Taiwan's foreign \nexchange reserves of US$75 billion, no one doubts that the island has the \nability to pay.  The government is even departing from its previous policy \nof not incurring debt by issuing bonds to pay for the plan.  In view of the \nTaiwan public's thirst for investment vehicles, there is little doubt that \nthese bonds will be popular.\n     Apart from upgrading the infrastructure as a whole in Taiwan, the plan \nwill also increase spending in the central, southern and eastern parts of \ncentral, southern and eastern parts of the island to achieve a more equita-\nble spread of wealth and prevent a migration to the overcrowded north.\n     To improve transportation, for instance, a new high-speed railway from \nTaipei in northern Taiwan to Kaohsiung in the south will be built at a cost \nof US$10 billion.  This will reduce the traveling time between the two \ncities from five hours to two hours.\n     The first stage of providing mass rapid transit systems for Taiwan's \nmajor cities is under way with a US$15 billion project in Taipei whose \nfirst stage is due to be completed next year.  The city of Kaohsiung re-\ncently followed the Taipei example of selecting a U.S. consultant for its \nsubway project, which will cost US$7 billion.  Smaller systems are being \nplanned for Taichung in central Taiwan and Tainan in the south.\n     A branch of the Taipei subway will go to the Chiang Kai-shek \nInternational airport, where another facility under the plan will be the \nconstruction of a second terminal to increase capacity from eight million \nto 20 million passengers a year at a cost of US$770 million.  A completely \nnew airport in Kaohsiung will be constructed at a cost of US$500 million,\nand facilities at Green Island, Orchid Island, and Hengchun close to the \nsouthern tip of the island will be upgraded.\n     Also being studied for feasibility is new deep-water port in Kaohsiung \nat a cost of Us$12 billion.  And also in the communications sector, the \nwhole telephone system in Taiwan will be modernized.\n     Since reducing pollution and protecting the environment is regarded as \nhaving greatest priority among most people in Taiwan, a total of US$30 \nbillion will be spent on this sector during the period of the plan to \nconstruct an islandwide network of solid waste incinerators, a pollution \nmonitoring system and improved sewerage systems.\n     Jobs created from the projects are expected to help raise per capita \nincome from the present US$8,000 to US$14,000 by the end of the decade, \nputting Taiwan well within the ranks of developed nations.\n     To reduce sky-high rents, the government will invest US$42 billion in \npublic housing.  By raising the quality of life in Taiwan, it is hoped that \nmore technicians will be persuaded to stay and help make the island \na world financial, technological and commercial center.\n     To built up better relations with its trading partners, Taiwan intends \nto award as many of the contracts as possible to countries with which it \nruns up sizable trade surpluses, notably the United States and some coun-\ntries in Europe.\n     Furthermore, it is expected that Taiwan will receive a big economic \nboost from the projects which will help to prevent a recession over the \nnext few years.\n     According to official policy objective outlined by the government, the \nfive main aims of the plan are to raise the quality of life, increase na-\ntional income, provide sufficient resources for continued industrial grow-\nth, and promote balanced regional development.\n     The government realized that efficient allocation of an ample supply \nof productive resources is the key to industrial modernization and sus-\ntained economic growth.  To ensure the development of more high-tech \nindustrial establishments, the government will carefully monitor the \ndistribution of productive resources such as land, water, energy and trans-\nportation facilities during the period of the plan.\n     Priority is also being given under the plan to accelerating the devel-\nopment of backward areas by improving schools, housing, medical, cultural \nand transportation facilities.\n     To improve national wellbeing, Taiwan will be divided into 18 \"living \nprimeters\" which will be selfcontained communities providing accommoda-\ntion, and medical and shopping facilities.  In addition, a national helath \ninsurance program will be inaugurated in 1994 to provide medical insurance \ncoverage to everyone.  As well as providing a more favorable climate for \neconomic growth, it is expected that the plan will assist social stability \nby providing more employment.\n     On the demand side, and increase in public investment will encourage \nprivate investment and consumption, thereby expanding domestic demand and \nreducing the trade surplus.  These developments will reflect a higher de \ngree of economic independence and reduce vulnerability to economic \nturbulence.\n     The government realizes that success of the program will depend not \nonly on adequate funding and effective implementation, but also on whole-\nhearted support by the private sector.  To ensure that public and private \nresources are put to the best possible use, the government will introduce \nnew fiscal incentives, encourage the relalocation of industries and update \nrelated legislation during the plan period.\n     Among the targets of the plan are that economic growth will average \nseven percent a year, that investment in the public sector will grow by \n12.5 percent a year, manufacturing will grow by 6.5 percent annually, and \nthat the service and high-tech industries will grow particularly quickly.\n     By the end of the plan, Taiwan's power capacity will be raised from 17 \nmillion kilowatts to 27.5 million kilowatts, and on the social and recrea-\ntional fronts, at least one new university and several high schools will be \nset up, as well as a number of libraries, swimming pools, parks and sports \ngrounds.  Social welfare services will be enhanced, and more creational \nareas will be established.\n     \"Gardening Reaches New Heights\" Lack of space at ground level has \ncaused many householders to look at what would otherwise be a desert to \ncreate greenery--the tops of apartment buildings.  Rooftop gardens are now \nall the rage in Taipei, allowing people to show an amazing amount of crea-\ntivity along with their \"green fingers.\"\n     Alongside rapid development in business and industry, Taipei has been \nconstantly changing in decent years.  High-rise buildings have replaced the \nold two-storied houses, and people seldom have the chance to stand on solid \nground.  The high price of land caused businesses to use every piece that \nbecomes available to make even bigger returns.  As green fields become \nscarcer and scarcer, the only greenery left is often in the road dividers \non some main boulevards and a handful of small parks in Taipei."}, {"title": "英文 T30", "content": "     William L. Seavey's crusade can be summed up with his toll-free num-\nber:  1-800-OUT-OF-LA.\n     For 10 years, he has sermonized on the downfall of \"the Los Angeleses, \nthe New Yorks and the Detroits.\"  He has devoted himself to telling people \nto move out of a city many weren't ready to leave.\n     But what with the crime, the crowds, the traffic-and of course the \nriots, fires, floods and earthquakes-Angelenos could be excuse these days \nif they wondered whether Seavey didn't have a point.\n     Southern California, Seavey said in a news release he circulated after \nthe Jan. 17 Northridge earthquake, is \"the most dangerous urban area in the \nUnited States.\"\n     In the wake of the quake, Seavey shook into action.  He dashed out 500 \npieces of mail to San Fernando Valley residents, dropped off brochures at \nemergency shelters, wrote guest editorials for local newspapers.\n     Convincing people to leave urban areas is Seavey's mission, and help-\ning them escape is his livelihood.  And he's not the only one.  Entrepre-\nneurs like Tom Evons and Constance See are players in the dugout business.\n     Last year, when 900 people packed a room in La Jolla to hear Evons \ngive his speech on \"Escape San Diego,\" Evons knew what his next move would \nbe.  He expanded his operation to include \"Escape L.A.\" and \"Escape San \nFrancisco.\"\n     See promotes leaving any risky place.  She sells a map of the United \nStates emblazoned with the question, \"Are You Safe Where You Live?\"  It \nis overlaid with statistics on rapes, murders, prison escapes, Superfund \nsites, breast cancer rates, earthquakes and other recipes for a short life-\nspan.\n     \"I'll show you where the cancer, crud and crime are across the United \nStates,\" says See, who runs City Seekers out of her home in Olympia, Wash.  \n     After broadcast appearances in Los Angeles and San Francisco last \nfall, See was barraged with phone calls:  900 from Los Angeles and 700 from \nSan Francisco.\n     About 15 percent of the callers went on to become clients.  They paid \nSee US$25 to compile information on particular cities and US$45 to be \nmatched with their own personal Eden somewhere in the country.\n     Some would say that idylls are getting harder to come by.  When Matt \nHolbert left Galveston, Texas, for Boise, Idaho, he thought he was bidding \nadieu to urban clutter, gangs and graffiti.\n     Not so, Boise is beginning to grapple with some of the name \"big city\" \nplagues.  Growth rates in Boise have been running at six percent for the \npast three years-two percent is about the norm.\n     \"If it keeps growing at this pace,\" said Holbert, \"we'll find a small-\ner city.  But it's hard to get away from this growth.\"\n     At a recent rally to stop the building of a bridge across the Boise \nRiver, protesters sported T-shirts emblazoned with the word \"Boiseangeles\" \nenclosed in a circle with a red slash through it.\n     The same sentiment is widespread on the coast.  About 65 percent of \nAllied Van Lines 1993 California relocations were outbound, up from 58 \npercent in 1991.  United Van Lines reported similar figures for 1993.  In \nLos Angeles County deportees outnumbered arrivals by more than 2-to-1.\n     The subscription list for Seavey's quarterly newsletter has grown from \n100 to 2,000 in three years with the largest growth coming in Southern \nCalifornia.\n     \"That's where the action is,\" said Seavey, a former resident of Los \nAngeles who now lives in Pahrump, Nev., (Population:  10.000.\"\n     These days, he spends most of his time on the road, living out of his \nmotor home and extolling the virtues of small town life at each stop.\n     He sells all kinds of relocation accessories; audio tapes and books on \neverything from setting up a home-based business to \"Selling your Southern \nCalifornia Property When No One's Biting.\"  He runs a \"Meet, Match and \nMove\" support group and oversee a network of \"Hinterland Host,\" rural folks \nwho serve as a welcome party to the newly relocated.\n     Seavey isn't sure how to react to others, like Evons and See, who have \nclimbed on the get-out-of-L.A. bandwagon.  On one hand, he says he's re-\nluctunt to give out any competitors' names for fear of drawing attention to \nthem.  But then he adds.\n     In some sense, the more the merrier People need to Know what the \nalternatives are to living in big, crowded, unsafe places.\n     \"And that is our mission.\"\n     Rangoon, Burma-The Ministry of Hotels and Tourism has allowed 147 pri-\nvaately owned hotels and inns to operate in the country, the official news-\npaper New Light of Myanmar reported yesterday.  There are now over 2,100 \nhotel rooms in Burma.  Foreign investors from six countries are currently \nbuilding 17 more hotels, investing over US$500 million, the newspaper re-\nported.\n     Chicago-Amoco Corp. plans to announce a major restructuring next month \nthat will sharply reduce the size of its corporate staff and replace the \ncompany's three divisions with 17 separate groups.  The company has said \npreviously that the restructuring would cost jobs, but has not been spe-\ncific.  The corporate staff would be cut to about 400 positions from more \nthan 6,000, but many of the workers would be transferred within Amoco and \nwon't result in layoffs, spokesman Jim Fair said Wednesday.  The 17 groups \nwill answer to three vice presidents, formerly the chiefs of the three main \ngroups.  The separate staffs for the three divisions will be reassigned or \ndisappear, Amoco told employees.\n     Tel Aviv-Israel released several hundred Palestinian prisoners Thurs-\nday, but a watchdog group said the releases lagged far behind what was \nagreed.  The army spokesman's office said those released were not members \nof militant Islamic groups opposed to the peace process and had not killed \nIsraelis.  The statement gave the number of released only at \"several \nhundred.\""}, {"title": "英文 T31", "content": "     Taiwan can increase its Gross National Product (GNP) five fold if it \ncan successfully use the economies of the mainland and Southeast Asian \ncountries for its future growth, a government-commissioned report said yes-\nterday.\n     The report, funded by the Ministry of Economic Affairs' Industrial De-\nvelopment and Investment Center, was undertaken by the Far Eastern Club of \nEconomic Research.\n     The research for the report was overseen by Council for Economic Plan-\nning and Development Vice Chairman Chi Schive.\n     The report, which studied ways in which Taiwan could be transformed \ninto a regional finance and operations center, said that Taiwan could \npotentially increase its GNP from US$210.5 billion to US$1 trillion if it \ncould use these countries as a hinterland for the island's economic deve-\nlopment.\n     Schive said in the report that Taiwan should expand its hinterlands \nfor economic development if it wishes to replace Hong Kong and Singapore \nand become a business operational center in the Asia-Pacific region.\n     Therefore, Taiwan should pursue its \"Go South\" and mainland policies \nsimultaneously so that it would have a large hinterland for economic deve-\nlopment, the report said.\n     In 1992, Taiwan's Gross National Product (GNP) was US$210.5 billion, \nwhich was more than twice Hong Kong's US$91.4 billion and 4.5 times Singa-\npore's US$46.6 billion.\n     Taiwan, however, was behind Hong Kong and Singapore in telecommunica-\ntions, mail and financial services, the report said.\n     The report cites an evaluation on the telecommunications infrastruc-\ntures in developing countries and said Singapore came first with a score of \n97.5 on a 100 point scale.  Hong Kong came in second with 94.8.  Taiwan was \neights on the list, with a 74.8 score.\n     Taiwan should therefore speed up its efforts to privatize the island's \ntelecommunications industry and ease the ruling over state-run corporations \nif it wanted to become a financial and operations hub in the Asia-Pacific \nregion.\n     Schive also proposed that Taiwan should ease restrictions on capital \nflow, the stock market and non-banking financial services to make the bank-\ning and financial sector more competitive.\n     Taiwan should also simplify the procedures for foreign or overseas \nChinese investments in Taiwan's stock market, banking services and other \nbusinesses, he said.\n     Currently, Hong Kong dominates the market for businesses going to the \nmainland.  Its economic strength, or GNP, could therefore jump to US$484.4 \nbillion, he said.\n     Singapore, on the other hand, was the gateway to the Association of \nSoutheast Asian Nations (Asean) markets and its economy had the potential \nof growing up to S$373.9 billion.\n     Schive said that Taiwan's GNP could be expected to grow to about \nUS$400 billion if its present investments in the mainland was taken into \naccount.\n     However, if Taiwan could successfully develop its investments in \nSoutheast Asia, its economic strength would grow by an additional US$600 \nbillion.\n     If this happened, then foreign companies would want to set up their \nbusiness headquarters in Taiwan, the report said.\n     A mainland Chinese wearing a serviceman's uniform hijacked a mainland \nSoutheast Airlines flight to Taiwan yesterday morning after he threatened \nthe pilot with a fruit knife and a fake explosive.\n     He had on tow his wife, two sons aged 11 and 9, and foster mother.\n     The hijacking was the first from mainland China to Taiwan this year \nand the 11th since April last year.\n     Police identified the hijacker as 35-year-old Lin Wenqiang of Fujian \nprovince.  Lin was taken into custody for questioning, while the plane was \nallowed to continue its flight to Fujian via Hong Kong in the afternoon.\n     Lin told police that he decided to hijack the Boeing 737 because he \nwas irritated over bureaucratic problems over his application to set up a \ncompany in Changsha in the southeastern province of Hunan.\n     Lin's wife, 32-year-old Huang Chunlien, refused to leave the airport \nupon learning that her husband was taken into custody.\n     But the prosecutor decided to hand Huang and her family over to the \nflight crew after he questioned them and determined that they were not in-\nvolved in the hijacking.\n     Huang tried to resist and had to be dragged onto the plane by police \nofficers.\n     Vice Justice Minister Lin Shyi-hwu said Lin's family members were re-\nturned to the mainland because they were not involved in the hijacking and \nwere given the same treatment as other passengers.\n     Lin said the decision may help curb hijackings.  In two previous \ncases, family members of hijackers were allowed to remain in Taiwan because \nthey were found to be involved in the hijackings, he said.\n     Kao Koong-lian, vice chairman of the Mainland Affairs Council, urged \nthe mainland authorities to sign an agreement with Taiwan for the repatria-\ntion of hijackers as soon as possible.  Before the agreement is signed, \nTaiwan has to handle hijackings according to its own law, he said.\n     Interior Minister Wu Po-hsiung said when the Straits Exchange Founda-\ntion and its mainland counterpart meet in Taipei in late March, they might \nagree to add a retroactive clause for the repatriation of mainland hijack-\ners who have come here in the past year.\n     The plane, carrying 133 passengers and crew members, was hijacked to \nTaiwan shortly after it took off from Chengdu in southwestern China on a \nregular flight to Fuzhou.\n     The plane landed at the Chiang Kaishek International Airport at 10:42 \nam and was allowed to leave Taiwan at 2:44 pm.  A spokesman for the Hong \nKong Government said the plane had entered the mainland at 3:30 pm."}, {"title": "英文 T32", "content": "     The ceiling for foreign investment in Taiwan stock market will be \nraised to US$10 billion starting tomorrow, the Central Bank of China \n(CBC) announced yesterday.\n     \"The move signifies our commitment to promote financial liberali-\nzation and internationalization,\" CBC Deputy Governor Yu Cheng told a \nnews conference.  The move is expected to attract more foreign funds to \nthe Taiwan bourse.\n     The ceiling for total investment by foreign financial institutions, \nwhich gained permission to invest directly in the Taiwan bourse in early \n1991, will be raised to US$7.5 billion from US$5 billion, Yu said.\n     The central bank also set a ceiling of US$2.5 billion on overseas \nfundraising by Taiwanese securities investment trust firms.  Previously, \nthe firms had not faced a formal ceiling.\n     Yu said the reform would allow foreign funds to account for about \nsix percent of the total stock market capitalization, roughly in line with \ncurbs imposed by South Korea on its market and near the proportion of \nJapanese stocks held by foreigners.\n     The central bank, concerned that fund inflows could destabilize finan-\ncial markets and hurt exporters by pushing up the value of the Taiwan dol-\nlar, had resisted heavy political pressure to raise the investment ceiling \nfor several months.\n     The Securities and Exchange Commission, which wants to make Taiwan a \nregional financial center as the island prepares to join the General \nAgreement on Tariffs and Trade (Gatt), had pressed for a ceiling as high \nas US$20 billion.\n     \"We welcome foreign funds for long-term investment, but not those for \nshort-term profit-taking,\" said CBC Deputy Governor Yu.\n     He said capital inflows might push the annual M2 money supply growth \nup to an estimated 16.7 percent.  \"The central bank is prepared to prevent \nthis by absorbing liquidity in the money market to keep growth in a range \nof 10 to 15 percent,\" he added.\n     Foreign financial institutions have so far received approval to bring \nUS$4.71 billion into Taiwan, and have actually remitted US$3.36 billion.  \nDomestic investment trust firms have been approved to remit US$1.53 billion \nand have brought in US$1.1 billion.  \"There is still ample room for foreign \ncapital to flow into the local bourse,\" Yu said.\n     Presently, foreign funds account for about two percent of Taiwan's \ntotal stock market capitalization.  Securities and Exchange Commission \nChairman Tai Li-ning said the ratio should be raised to 10 percent if Tai-\nwan wanted its stock index to be incorporated into the widely known Stanley \ninternational stock index.\n     Local fund managers and securities analysts said the reform was ex-\npected to trigger a new wave of foreign interest in the Taiwan stock mar-\nket, which soared 80 percent in the final quarter of last year although it \nhas fallen back since mid-February amid heavy profit-taking.\n     Some other analysts pointed out, however, that selling pressure re-\nmained likely to cut short any nearterm rally sparked by the central \nbank's announcement.  The market's weighted index closed at 5,671.46 yes-\nterday, and brokers expect the index to surge to 6,000 in the coming week.\n     With economic growth officially forecast at 6.2 percent this year and \ncorporate earnings per share expected to rise by around 20 percent, some \nanalysts said the index could hit 7,000 points by year's end.\n     The central bank's announcement sent the New Taiwan dollar advancing \n10.8 cents to close at an eight-month high of 26.32 to the US dollar from \n26.428 on Friday.\n     Foreign exchange dealers said the New Taiwan dollar could remain un-\nder upward pressure next week unless the central bank intervened in the \nmarket.\n     Premier Lien Chan, stepping up efforts to deter election fraud, called \nyesterday for revised polling procedures and a thorough investigation of \nthe bribery that marred Monday's internal elections of 21 local parlia-\nments.\n     Lein ordered the Interior Ministry to study the possibility of narrow-\ning the time between the elections of councilors and the internal voting \nfor speakers and vice speakers.\n     Having the polling for both take place on the same day, which would \nrequire election law revisions, might deny candidates the time they need to \nmanipulate the speaker and vice speaker races, Lien said.\n     The current procedures, which schedule the internal elections of \nspeakers and vice speakers about a month after the councilor polls, con-\ntribute to the election fraud, Lien maintained.\n     The Interior Ministry may have to propose further revisions to the \nElection and Recall Law, even though one set of revisions has already been \nsubmitted to the Legislative Yuan.\n     Lien gave his instructions during a Cabinet meeting following a con-\ndemnation Wednesday by ruling Kuomintang leaders of the corrupt speaker and \nvice speaker races.\n     During yesterday's meeting, Minister Without Portfolio Huang Shih-\ncheng proposed a radical cut in the terms of elected officials to one year, \ngiving them little time to profit, through their elected positions, from \ntheir campaign investments.\n     Inside the Legislature, lawmakers have also stepped up efforts to de-\nter election fraud.  Many lawmakers have proposed to revise the Election \nand Recall Law in a move to prevent vote-buyers from taking office.\n     Ruling Kuomintang lawmaker Huang Chu-wen yesterday asserted that \npolitical parties should also be punished if their members were convicted \nof election fraud.\n     He said a party may be disbanded if its members are frequently involv-\ned in polling irregularities.\n     The alleged vote-buying and violence in Monday's speaker and vice \nspeaker elections have embarrassed the ruling party because virtually all \nof the contests were between rival KMT factions, not between the KMT and \nopposition parties.\n     Shocked by the allegations, KMT leaders on Wednesday berated the party \napparatus for either turning a blind eye to the fraud or even helping KMT \ncandidates buy votes.\n     But it remained doubtful yesterday whether the government and the rul-\ning party can succeed in curbing election fraud at the grassroots level.\n     Regional KMT factions have been a major force and source of votes for \nthe ruling party in national elections.\n     KMT Secretary General Hsu Shui-teh, admitting that the factions are \nbeyond the control of the party, yesterday rebuked the criticisms that the \nparty apparatus had abetted the irregularities.\n     In a meeting of ranking KMT officials to evaluate the elections, Hsu \nsaid the criticisms were unfair and a blow to the party faithful who worked \nhard to win the elections.\n     Hsu, pinning the blame on what he said was a deterioration of social \nvalues, said winning in the elections is the first priority.\n     He said the criticisms were apparently from people who had never ac-\ntually participated in campaigns and thus had never known the difficult-\nies.\n     Although there have been numerous allegations about vote-buying sur-\nrounding Monday's elections, judicial officials said it is difficult to \nobtain evidence.\n     Justice Minister Ma Ying-jeou, saying a total of 32 cases of vote-\nbuying in Monday's elections are currently being investigated, urged wit-\nnesses to come forward."}, {"title": "英文 T33", "content": "     Despite the ruling Kuomintang's landslide victory in Monday's elec-\ntions of local council speakers and vice speakers, party leaders yester-\nday condemned the widespread use of violence and vote-buying.\n     Yu Kuo-hwa, a member of the Central Standing Committee, said the of-\nfenses, apparently committed by KMT candidates in the almost intraparty \nrace, have seriously dampened the party's reputation.\n     Vice Chairman Hau Peitsun questioned whether regional party offices \nhad turned a blind eye to the rampant bribery, or had even helped KMT \ncandidates with their illicit activities.\n     Both KMT leaders called for an investigation into the matter, to \npunish those who were involved in the illegalities.\n     Their criticisms followed an evaluation report on Monday's elections \npresented at the meeting of the decisionmaking Central Standing Committee.\n     Responding to the censure and the report, KMT Chairman Lee Teng-hui \nordered an internal investigation into the irregularities and factional \nclashes that were at the core of the matter.\n     According to the report, prepared by the party's Department of Organi-\nzation Affairs, the elections were seriously marred by violence, vote-buy-\ning and factional competitions.\n     The report points out that the department had attempted to select the \nbest candidates through coordination and negotiations between KMT fac-\ntions.\n     But the factional competition was further worsened by the \"overwhelm-\ning interference\" of money, causing the race to get out of control by the \nparty.\n     The factional conflicts will continue to hamper the normal operation \nof local parliaments, and by extension, the development of the cities and \ncounties, stated the report.\n     It is imperative for the party to seek out ways to settle factional \ndifferences.\n     The report calls on the ruling party and the government to step up \nefforts to \"make evaluations and improvements\" to curb election offenses.\n     Local media have reported that some of the candidates spent close to \nNT$100 million to win their campaigns.  Death threats and shootings have \nalso been reported.  In one case, the brother of the speaker at the Tai-\nchung County Council was shot and seriously injured.\n     Tough competitions between KMT's local factions were behind the de-\nviant break in procedure.\n     The KMT, as expected, swept almost all the speaker and vice-speaker \nseats in the 21 county and city councils of Taiwan, in races that were \nactually competitions between the ruling party's local factions.\n     The local factions, with their political ambitions and alleged con-\nnections with gangsters, have always been a headache for the ruling \nparty.\n     Factional control of the councils brings not only political fame for \nthe winning faction, but may also help the group manipulate government \nresources.\n     At the Central Standing Committee meeting yesterday, Chairman Lee \nhed to acknowledge this fact when instructing party aides to study and \nseek out ways to solve factional conflicts.\n     Yu Kuo-hwa, saying that the party's reputation had been seriously \ndamaged by the corrupt election practices, demanded the party to do more \nthan \"make evaluations and improvements,\" as suggested by the Department \nof Organization Affairs.\n     Vice Chairman Hau Peitsun, despite lauding the department for its \nsuccess in the election, called for an investigation.\n     Hau said he could not understand why the KMT candidates still had to \nbuy votes in an almost intra-party race.\n     Meanwhile, Justice Minister Ma Ying-jeou vowed to continue investi-\ngations into vote-buying allegations.\n     \"These cases... have not yet been closed despite the fact that the \nvoting is already over.  We will continue with the investigations,\" said \nMa.  But he did not specify which cases.\n     A woman who killed her husband after he allegedly and repeatedly beat \nand raped her was sentenced yesterday to five and a half years in jail, \ndrawing an immediate outcry from women's rights advocates who called the \nsentence too harsh.\n     A judge announced the five year, six month prison sentence against \nTeng Ju-wen, 22, in Panchiao yesterday afternoon in what had been consid-\nered a judicial test case for women abused by their husbands in Taiwan's \nmale-dominated society.\n     Holding a string of Buddhist beads in her hands, Teng, remained silent \nas the judge read the verdict.\n     Teng had testified at her trial that she was first raped by her hus-\nband when she was 15 years old, before they were married.  She also testi-\nfied that her husband, 20 years her senior, had also raped and abused her \nmother and sister.\n     Teng's attorney yesterday immediately announced he would appeal.  Ear-\nlier, another defense attorney had promised to launch an appeal if Teng re-\nceived a sentence of more than five years.\n     Women's groups, who had brought the case under the public's attention, \nexpressed disappointment and anger.\n     \"We have lost hope in the judges,\" Wang Ping of the Awakening Foun-\ndation told The China Post.  Wang criticized the court for not considering \nthe years of brutality Teng suffered from her husband before she killed \nhim.\n     In his explanation of the verdict, the judge cited the fact against \nTeng that she had killed her husband, Lin Ah-chi, two hours after he had \ngone to sleep, and that she had remembered all the details afterward.\n     In her favor, the judge accepted that she had turned herself in to the \npolice and commended Teng for her good behavior after she was arrested.  \nThese two factors had moved the court to use Article 62 of the Criminal \nCode in order to reduce the verdict, the judge commented.\n     Wang said she was especially upset because Teng's two sons would re-\nmainin the care of Lin's brother.  Before the killing on Oct. 27, the two \nboys had often been cared for by Teng's parents, she said.\n     In an interview with the press before the verdict was announced, Teng \nhad expressed the hope that her sentence would be delayed for a while so \nshe could arrange care for her children, yet in the end that request was \nnot considered.\n     The cost of the trial will have to be borne by Lin's relatives, the \njudge ruled.\n     As to the appeal, Cheng Huei-chi, the secretary-general of the Taipei \nWomen's Rescue Foundation said she was not very optimistic while Wang de-\nclared she was not overly pessimistic.\n     Cheng said a lawyer had told her that, compared to previous cases, a \nminimum sentence of 10 years would have been normal.\n     Seen in this light, she said there has been some progress, though she \nthought the sentence should have been less than three years.\n     Wen Yueh-mei, who had come all the way from Hsinchu to express her \nsupport for Teng, said she had not expected a not-guilty verdict, since \nthe laws of the Republic of China are still not effective enough to pro-\ntect the victims of wife abuse.\n     The women's groups will continue their efforts to increase awareness \nof this serious social problem, Wang promised, though no plans for any \nconcrete actions were currently ready.\n     Activists have repeatedly called for a law against violence in the \nhome and for a network of shelters for battered women.  The Teng case is \nnow seen by some commentators as a possible catalyst for action to help \nothers in a similar situation.\n     The Social Affairs Department of the Ministry of the Interior is in-\nvestigating the possibility of expanding a 24-hour shelter network for \nabused children to accept women as well."}, {"title": "英文 T34", "content": "     Many people in the world are concerned about the dwindling size of \nthe whale population in the oceans and seas.  They are worried because \nthe number of whales is getting so small.  Whales are very large aquatic \nanimals which have been hunted since about the seventh century.  However, \ncertain types of whale have been hunted too much and there are so few now \nthat they are in danger of becoming extinct.  Fortunately, these concerned \npeople are working to save the whales from such a fate.\n     Why should we save the whales?  There are two important reasons.  The \nfirst is that whales help to keep a balance between plants and animals.  \nPeople have disturbed this balance by throwing waste into the sea.  This \nsewage and garbage has increased the amount of salt in the sea water which \nin turn, helps the tiny plants and animals, called plankton, to grow.  Al-\nthough plankton can be harmful to fish, they are necessary for whales to \nsurvive.  Whale eat enormous amounts of plankton and therefore keep the \nocean environment clean enough for fish.  In addition, because fish are an \nimportant source of food for many people, people need whales, and so they \ntry to save them.\n     How are people working to save whales?  Some worried people are attem-\npting to save whales through legal means, by using the law.  These people \nattend meetings of the whaling commissions to ask whalers to reduce the \nnumber of whales that can be killed in a year.  They also work within coun-\ntries to persuade law-makers to make whaling and the use of whale products \nillegal.\n     Other concerned people are working to save whales from becoming ex-\ntinct in another way.  These people believe that the best means to save the \nwhales is by making whale products expensive.  They think that if whale \nproducts become less economical than similar products, then whalers will \nkill fewer whales.  Consequently, the supply of whale products will de-\ncrease because of the low demand for them.  One of the most valuable whale \nproducts is whale oil which is processed from the fat, meat and bones of \nwhales.  This oil has many uses, for example, in the manufacture of mar-\ngarine, soap and certain cosmetics.  One large whale may yield 150 barrels \nof whale oil.  However, scientists have found an alternative yet similar \noil from a desert plant, jojoba oil.  People are trying to produce jojoba \noil more economically than whale oil and hope that is this way they can \nhelp to save the whales.\n     Concern for whales is world-wide.  Some governments will not let peo-\nple sell whale products in their countries.  Others have changed the laws \non whaling to prevent whalers from killing as many whales as before.  Peo-\nple who want to save the whales are very hopeful that the gentle giants of \nthe ocean can be saved.\n     Why should we save the whales?  There are two important reasons.  The \nfirst is the whales help to keep a balance between plants and animals.  \nPeople have disturbed this balance by throwing waste into the sea.  This \nsewage and garbage has increased the amount of salt in the sea water which \nin turn, helps the tiny plants and animals, called plankton, to grow.  Al-\nthough plankton can be harmful to fish, they are necessary for whales to \nsurvive.  Whales eat enormous amounts of plankton and therefore keep the \nocean environment clean enough for fish.  In addition, because fish are an \nimportant source of food for many people, people need whales, and so they \ntry to save them.\n     How are people working to save whales?  Some worried people are at-\ntempting to save whales through legal means, by using the law.  These peo-\nple attend meetings of the whaling commissions to ask whalers to reduce the \nnumber of whales that can be killed in a year.  They also work within coun-\ntries to persuade law-makers to make whaling and the use of whale products \nillegal.\n     Other concerned people are working to save whales from becoming extin-\nct in another way.  These people believe that the best means to save the \nwhales is by making whale products expensive.  They think that if whale \nproducts become less economical than similar products, then whalers will \nkill fewer whales.  Consequently, the supply of whale products will de-\ncrease because of the low demand for them.  One of the most valuable whale \nproducts is whale oil which is processed from the fat, meat and bones of \nwhales.  This oil has many uses, for example, in the manufacture of mar-\ngarine, soap and certain cosmetics.  One large whale may yield 150 barrels \nof whale oil.  However, scientists have found an alternative yet similar \noil from a desert plant, jojoba oil.  People are trying to produce jojoba \noil more economically than whale oil and hope that in this way they can \nhelp to save the whales.\n     Concern for whales is world-wide.  Some governments will not let peo-\nple sell whale products in their countries.  Others have changed the laws \non whaling to prevent whalers from killing as many whales as before.  Peo-\nple who want to save the whales are very hopeful that the gentle giants of \nthe ocean can be saved.\n     One of the famous tourist attractions situated in Kuala Lumpur is the \nMuzium Negara which is also one of the finest institutions of its kind in \nSoutheast Asia.\n     The Muzium Negara, barely three decades old, was charged by our first \nPrime Minister, Tunku Abdul Rahman with the task of helping to forge a \nspirit of national identity in Malaysia's multi-racial society.  This mu-\nseum has become the focus of this culturally rich country, a preserver of \nthe nation's heritage.\n     Seriously damaged during World War II, a publicity campaign was mount-\ned in 1962 to rebuild the museum.  It was also necessary to replace the ex-\nhibits lost during the war.  The campaign included, among other things, co-\nllecting artifacts, especially Malay crafts.  Help came from unexpected \nquarters.  A Malay wood carver donated 40 lengths of magnificent hand woven \nsilk, all of them more than 60 years old.  Intricately carved doors and \nscreens and antique furniture of an entire Chinese household came from Ma-\nlacca.  A woman donated a full bridal outfit from the golden and jewelled \nheadband down to the beaded slippers sewn by the bride.  The museum was \nopened in August 1963.\n     To entice people into the museum again and again, exhibitions are held \nevery now and then.  These exhibitions range from Malaysian arts and crafts \nand natural history to information on public interest campaigns.  In addi-\ntion, foreign embassies are invited to use the museum for special travell-\ning exhibits from their own countries.  Live demonstrations, singers and \nmulti-media presentations spice up the shows.\n     One of the most successful exhibitions was one that featured the per-\nsonal effects of a popular Malaysian actor, director, singer and composer, \nP. Ramlee, soon after he died.  His films were screened daily during the \nexhibition.  Popular singers and film stars were invited to perform and the \nmuseum was kept open until midnight.  \"A museum should not only show the \npast, but also the changes of the present,\" said the then director-general \nof the museum.  Despite many raised eyebrows, a million people visited the \nmuseum during the three weeks of the exhibition.\n     As protector of the nation's heritage, the government-funded Muzium \nNegara stands to safeguard national monuments and to prevent important ob-\njects from being exported.  What it is building, more than a collection of \nartifacts, is a sense of nationhood, and it serves as a model of what can \nbe done with an unlimited supply of imagination, energy, flexibility and \noptimism."}, {"title": "英文 T35", "content": "     Musical mystery tour, from a to z, inspired by the 200th anniversary \nof Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart's discordant death.  Amadeus--Mozart's middle \nname is also the name of Peter Shaffer's acclaimed play on the life of \nMozart, which was subsequently made unto the Oscar-winning film of the \nsame name.  Amadeus means just \"beloved of God,\" and Peter Shaffer used \nthe name to emphasize that the greatest inspiration cannot be simply \nreasoned, that something above ourselves is the only true source of \ncreation.  The dramatic metaphor, both on stage and in film, is an \nimpressive one.  But Shaffer's poetic license offers an often false \nhistorical picture of the composer.  Mozart was playful, yes; he was \nhardly a teetotaler, yes.  But he was not simply a drunken yokel; after \na childhood in the finest courts of Europe, he could put on the social \ngraces when necessary.\n     Beethoven and Bach--History conveniently inserts Mozart the \nclassicist between Bach the baroque and Beethoven the romantic.  \nBach was something of an old fogy, but Mozart loved imitating Bach \nfugues for both piano and symphony music.  Bach's son was one of \nMozart's best friends, and they must have discussed the more serious \ncomposer with a mixture of awe and humor.  On the other hand, Beethoven \nplayed Mozart's music constantly, although he thought Mozart's own \npiano playing \"antiquated.\"  Beethoven wrote, \"to my last breath I \nshall be one of Mozart's greatest admirers.\"  And in a benefit for \nMozart's wife, he played one of Mozart's piano concertos as a tribute.\n     Constanze--Mozatrt's wife has been departed as unfaithful and the \nfoolish.  The reality is that Mozart could never have written music \nwithout his wife by his side.  After Mozart's death she needed money, \nand in her exhausting rounds to get this money, she made many an \nenemy.  But contemporaries tell about Mozart's dependence on his wife, \nher understanding, the fact that never once in their nine years \ntogether did they ever separate.\n     Debts--Mozart was never as poor as later generations made him out to \nbe.  His home was comfortable, his assignments were regular, if not \nterribly well paid.  But money was constantly on his mind.  No, he \nwasn't slavish to one patron, as Haydn was.  But he was never satisfied \nwith his payments--which were invariably late.\n     Emperor Joseph II--Did he exploit Mozart or aid him?  One of Mozart's \ngreatest patrons, Emperor Joseph refused to offer Mozart a \"stead \njob.\"  Mozart was of two minds about this.  He knew that having a \nreliable income would help, but he also knew that he could not \nrespond like Haydn, simply offering notes on the spot.  Then, too, \nMozart was too audacious to be simply an imperial courtier.\n     Flute--Did Mozart actually despise the flute?  He did write to his \nfather, \"I become quite powerless whenever I am obliged to write for \nthis instrument, which I cannot bear.\"  One of the world's great \nflautists, Jean-Pierre Rampal, has said that Mozart actually loved \nthe flute, but was simply in a bad mood when he wrote of his displeasure \nand that his solos prove how much he felt for the instrument.\n     Gourmandism--Did Mozart actually enjoy the famed kugel (marzipan \ncake), which was names in his honor?  Nobody knows.  In his hundreds of \nletters, he never comments on the great dinners given in his honor.  \nAnd it seems that food was of no importance to the composer.  \nOne food historian has written that he probably dined on pickled \ncabbage regularly.  It was cheap, popular and probably contributed to \nMozart's stomach problems.  On the other hand, one only need consider \nDon Giovanni to know that Mozart was hardly oblivious to food.  The \nDon's last aria is nothing less than a description of a huge banquet.  \nSo magnificent is this banquet that he keeps describing the menu \neven as he is dragged to hell!\n     Haydn--What good friends they were, how amiable and respectful they \nwere of each other, yet how different in their callings.  Haydn was, \nfirst and last, paid to provide entertainment for courts and aristocrats \nof his age.  One somehow cannot imagine him in any other century.  \nMozart, though, could transmute his music into any age.\n     Infant prodigy--Was Mozart the youngest composer?  While he began \ncomposing at the age of four, he was outdone by Camille Saint-Saens, \nwho was writing a the age of three.  Nobody remembers Saint-Saens' \nworks, but Mozart's were lovingly preserved by his father in little \nnotebooks that can still be seen in Vienna today.\n     Jocularity--Was Mozart as stupidly funny as Peter Shaffer made him \nto be?  He was a notorious practical joker.  Yet there was often \nreason in his madness.  In 1787, a singer playing Zerlina in the first \nperformance of Don Giovanni confessed that she was unable to scream \nin the right manner and the right manner and the right place for the \ncomposer.  Finally, Mozart asked the orchestra to repeat the work while \nhe sneaked on stage.  When she was about to sing, he pinched her bottom \nand she shrieked!  \"Now that,\" said Mozart, \"is the way you should do \nit on stage!\"\n     Kingdom--How great was the imagination of the prodigy Mozart?  \nConsider the kingdom of Rucken.  Rucken means \"back,\" and in his childhood \nMozart created this kingdom with its own laws and subjects.  Everybody \nwas good, people smiled, the kings were wonderful.  Mozart even drew \nmaps of the kingdom of Rucken, though, alas, they are gone today.  What \nis the significance of Rucken?  At no time was Rucken simply a hiding \nplace, a respite from his years as a prodigy.  It was an artistic \ncreation, a world of the imagination, as real as the creation of the \nmusic itself.\n     Lachrimosa--When composing the Requiem Mass, Mozart did not compose \nit in order.  In fact, he composed only a single movement in total, \nthis the Introitus, the first section.  Other parts were sketched \nout in his usual style.  The Lachrimosa consists of only eight bars in \nMozart's hand; these are almost certainly the last eight bars the \ncomposer ever wrote.\n     Masonic connections--How could Mozart, so filled with creativity, \nebullience, joy and sadness together, join an organization like the \nFreemasons?  Mozart belonged for many reasons.  In 1784, he almost died \nfrom tuberculosis, and in this bush with death ordinary Catholicism \nseemed, to the composer, simply insufficient.  For succor, he turned \nto the religion of the elect, with its profusion of symbols and magic.\n     Nobility--While The Marriage of Figaro was described as Mozart's \nparody of the nobility, Mozart's own feelings were more complex.  \nFrom his first journeys to the great courts of Europe, he loved the \naristocracy, aped their fashions and felt that such beauty and splendor \nwere part of the natural order of things.\n     Opera--Mozart died at 35 and composed no less than 25 works for the \nstage:  intermezzos and serenades, comic operas, operettas, comedies.  \nHistorically, he was the first important composer to write opera to \nGerman tents.  But even in Italian he was revolutionary.  One could \nsay that along with Monteverdi and Wagner, only Mozart had the sense \nof characterization, orchestral color and musical ideas that gave \ninsight into the idiosyncrasies of his characters.\n     Pantheon--Where is Mozart in the musical pantheon?  Bioacchino \nRossini was once asked who the greatest composer was.  \"Beethoven,\" \nhe was asked.  \"Oh, Mozart,\" he shrugged.  \"Mozart wasn't among the \ngreat composers.  He was the only composer.\"  \nRichard Strauss, in a rare moment of humility, said that he would \nexchange every one of his own operas if he could have written merely \ntwo bars from a scene of Don Giovanni.\n     Quickness--How fast did Mozart actually write his work?  In his last \nyear, La Clemenza Di Tito, which only recently has been considered a \nmasterpiece, was written and orchestrated in 18 days.  In the same year, \nhis three last symphonies were written in a total of five weeks.  And \nin those same five weeks Mozart composed several songs and sonatas well.  \nThe Don Giovanni overture was written in a single sitting.  And, yes, he \ndid write on tablecloths, envelopes and other scraps of paper.\n     Radical--When a publisher told him to be more accessible, more like \nHaydn, less dramatic, less self-absorbed, he asked why.  \"If you do not \nwrite more what people want,\" said the publisher, \"you will have no \nmarket for your music.\"  \"Then,\" said Mozart, \"I will write nothing more.  \nAnd I will go hungry.  Don Giovanni was not written for the public,\" he \nsaid.  \"It was written for myself and few friends.\"\n     Salieri--Did Antonio Salieri Kill Mozart?  Mozart, ever the \nhypochondriac, felt that he was being poisoned.  Salieri was certainly \njealous of his genius.  Chances are that Salieri didn't poison him, and \nthe latest medical analysis puts Mozart's death sown to streptococcal \ninfections, allergic hypersensitivity and a decline in kidney functions.\n     Travels/travails--Both confused Mozart's personality.  He had been \ntraveling through Europe from the age of four.  When a border guard once \nstopped him, asking for identification, the six-year-old Mozart simply \ntook out his violin and played it for the guard, who let him move on.\n     As for Mozart's travails, they were endless.  Money problems, work \nproblems, lack of appreciation, the list is endless.  And yet, on the \nsame day that Mozart would write a letter lamenting his personal \nhardships, he would compose a concerto, a sonata or a symphony of charm \nor ebullience.  The dividing line between catastrophe and creation is \nnot for mortal man to comprehend.\n     Understanding--Mozart often said that he wrote music mainly for \nhimself, but he needed adulation, he needed appreciation.  In a moment \nof rare confession, he wrote to his father:  \"Give me the best instrument \nin Europe, but listeners who understand nothing or do not wish to \nunderstand and who do not feel with me in what I am playing, and all my \npleasure is spoiled.\"\n     Vienna--Was Vienna really the one true inspiration for Mozart?  \nwhile virtually every 18th-and 19th-century Central European musician \nsettled here for long periods, during Mozart's time Vienna was \ndominated by politics.  The emperor, while admiring Mozart, was miserly \nand loved his own intrigues.  Mozart did not die a pauper here, but his \nhappiness in creation was diminished by Vienna itself.\n     Working habits--Mozart never had working habits.  He had no days \nor nights when inspiration came.  Inspiration was unstoppable!  When he \nwas four, his father, Leopold Mozart, found him writing notes for a \nclavier concerto.  When asked to see the work in progress, Mozart \ngave it to his father for a few minutes, then took it back and, even \nwhile talking, finished it.  When Mozart was 10, his father became \ndangerously ill, and the family was not allowed to touch the barber \ncomplained that he couldn't give Mozart a haircut, because at any \nmoment the composer might run to the clavier and jot down a new piece \nof inspiration.\n     X--Who was that masked man who appeared at Mozart's door and \ncommissioned him to write the Requiem in February 1791?  It was Count \nvon Walsegg, who often commissioned that he would put under his \nown name.\n     Youth--Was father Leopold Mozart exploiting his precocious son?  \nTwentieth-century sociologists and psychologists may analyze all they \nwant to, but not a word of anger or doubt was ever written by Mozart \nagainst his father.\n     Zaide--Permit me to end on a personal note.  If Richard Strauss \ncould choose two of Mozart's bar s to be the greatest ever written, I can \nchoose three movements that seem archetypal Mozart.  For simplicity \nthat somehow transforms itself into a depth of tragedy and compassion, \nthere is the almost naive theme from the A Minor Rondo, K.511.  Second, \nI nominate a rare recording of the 40th symphony performed by Bruno \nWalter and the Columbia Symphony Orchestra.  At the climax of the \nfirst movement, Walter takes a most forgivable liberty, by actually \nstopping the orchestra for half a beat.  It is as if he had ascended \nMount Everest and, instead of moving down, says, \"Look about us.  Look \nat the universe we have conquered.\"  That silence is as beautiful as the \nnotes that encompass it.  My third choice is a relatively unknown aria \nthat seems to rise from the unfinished opera, Zaide.  Dame Kiri Te Kanawa \nhas recorded Ruhe Sanft, mein holdes Leben, an aria that seems to rise \nfrom the depths of the earth.  Serious and lyrical, soaring and \ndignified, its personality comes from the penetration of Mozart's own \nimmortal soul."}, {"title": "英文 T36", "content": "     My name is Lemuel Gulliver and my father was a land-owner in England.  \nHis estate was not a large one and as he had five sons there was little \nmoney for our education.  My own ambition was to travel and in order to \nmake this possible I persuaded my father to have me trained as a doctor.  \nWhen I was young doctors were not thought very important, but the work \nsuited me because at the end of my training I was able to become a ship's \ndoctor and to travel all over the world.  Even when I married and had a \nfamily I did not stay on land for long and there are many tales I could \ntell you of the strange things I saw abroad.  But the strangest story of \nall began in May, 1699, when the voyage began which I shall now describe \nto you.\n     The journey started well and our sailing ship had traveled many \nmonths and half way round the world before anything unusual happened.  \nIt was near the coast of Sumatra that our luck changed.  A terrible storm \narose and for many days the ship was driven through seas which were unknown \nto our captain.  Many of the crew died.  At last the strong wind drove the \nship on to a rock where it was broken to pieces by the waves.  Only six of \nthe crew were able to escape in a small boat.  I was one of them.  We \nmanaged to row three miles but our efforts were in vain.  The boat was \nturned over by a mountainous wave and I was the only man who was able to \nswim to land.  It was a strange and lonely place in which I found myself.  \nThere was no sign of any people and, after looking in vain for a house \nwhere I could get help, I lay down to rest.  My struggles had made me so \ntired that very soon I was fast asleep on the grass.\n     It was morning when I woke and tried to get up.  But some thing \nstrange had happened while I was asleep.  I could not move an inch.  \nMy arms and legs were now tied down to the ground and even my hair, \nwhich was long and thick, had been tied down with string so that I could \nnot move my head to see what had happened to me.  All around me was the \nhum of little voices--but I could see nothing except the empty sky.  I \nknew there were people around me, but as far as my eyes could reach \nthere was no one to see.\n     As I lay there, frightened and puzzled, there was a movement on my \nleft leg.  Something alive was moving up my body towards my chin.  It \nfelt like a large insect and I looked anxiously down to see what was \ncoming.  It came into sight and there I could see a tiny man, less than \nsix inches high, marching up my chest with a bow and arrow in his hand.  \nI could hardly believe my eyes.  But after him came forty more little \nmen who marched up my body as though it was a battlefield.\n     In my fright at seeing these astonishing people I cried out so \nloudly that all the little men ran away.  Many of them were hurt jumping \nfrom my side down to the ground; but soon the others came back and climbed \nover me again.  One of the bravest came far enough to see my face and \ncalled out to his friends with admiration.  I could not understand his \nlanguage, however, and my fright increased.  Struggling hard, I was able \nto free my left arm and to loosen my hair a little so that I could turn \nmy head.  Before I could seize them, all the little people ran away and a \nhundred arrows hit my hand like needles.  Others fell on my face so that I \ncried out with pain and fought to get free.  More arrows came, however, \nand at last I decided it was safer to lie still.\n     As soon as I was quiet the soldiers stopped shooting arrows at me, \nbut more and more little people were joining the crowded around me.  \nNow that I could move my head a little I could see hundreds of workmen \nbuilding a stage about eighteen inches high, near my right ear.  I watched \nthem working and was amazed by their skill and speed.  Though they were no \nbigger than my thumb these people seemed able to do everything that men of \nour own size can do.\n     When the stage was finished a well dressed man, who seemed to be an \nimportant officer, climbed to the top with three servants.  From there he \nwas able to speak directly into my ear and leaning towards me he made a \nlong speech.  I could not understand one word, of course, but from the \nrepetition of the name \"Lilliput\" I guessed that this must be the name of \nthe country to which I had come.  Later I learned that this was correct.\n     The officer's manner seemed friendly and when he had finished I felt \nless frightened.  I was very hungry, however, and I put my finger in my \nmouth to show that I needed food.  At once the officer understood me and \nshouted orders to his men.  Soon ladders were placed at my side and a \nhundred little men climbed up to my mouth with baskets of food.  The \nmeat and the vegetables looked much like our own food, but everything \nwas very small.  I took two or three baskets at a mouthful and then ate \nsome loaves of bread which were smaller than marbles.  They brought me \nbarrels of water which were no larger than nuts and the little people \nwere so amazed at the number of barrels that I drank that they danced \non my chest with excitement.\n     It would have been easy now to seize forty or fifty men and throw \nthem to the ground, but as I had tasted their food and drink I felt I \nwas now their guest.  More and more I admire bravery in coming so close \nto me.  Now another official came up my chest and showed me a piece of \npaper smaller than my thumb nail.  It seemed to be important, but I did \nnot understand then that it was an order from the King of Lilliput that \nI should be taken to his capital city, which was called Mildendo.  As I \ndid not know of this plan I did not worry about it.  Instead I began to \nfeel very sleepy because the little people had put in my water a medicine \nto make me sleep.  Now they loosened a little the ropes that me and they \nput more medicine on the places where their poisoned arrows had hurt me.  \nI felt more comfortable when this had been done and soon I fell asleep \nagain.\n     While I was asleep five hundred carpenters and engineers set to work \nbuilding a car big enough to carry me to Mildendo.  Can you imagine a \nwooden cart strong enough to carry two elephants?  We would find this \ndifficult to build, but the Lilliputians found it just as difficult to \nbuild a cart to carry me.  Some of them were skilled engineers, however, \nand even before I had fallen asleep they had almost finished building \nthe biggest cart ever known in Lilliput.  It was seven feet long and four \nfeet wide and had twenty-two wheels.\n     The cart was brought by my side and then, while I was fast asleep, \nnine hundred of the strongest men in the country lifted me on to the \ncart with ropes.  Fifteen hundred horses, each about four inches high, \nwere fastened to the cart and we started on our way to Mildendo.\n     I slept while all this noise and activity went on around me.  It \nwas not until after we started that I was woken in the following way.  \nOne or two young men were anxious to see what I looked like when I was \nasleep and they climbed up on to the car to look at me.  They were very \nbold and one of them put the sharp end of his spear up my nose to see \nwhat would happen.  This made me sneeze violently and I woke up at once \n-- while the young men crept away feeling very brave and successful.\n     We traveled for two days.  At night when we rested, five hundred \nguards stood on either side of me ready to shoot if I tried to escape.  \nBut I saw no reason to be violent and lay quietly on my cart until we \nreached the gates of Mildendo.\n     The news of my arrival had spread quickly and everyone had come \nout of the city to see the marvelous \"Man-Mountain\" as they called me.  \nFirst of all came the King himself, with all his family and servants.  \nBut until I was securely fastened the King's advisers would not let him \ncome near me.\n     A house was ready for me.  It was one of the biggest buildings in \nthe city--an old temple into which I was just able to creep and to lie \ndown on the floor.  The walls were very thick and there were two windows \nthrough which the King's workmen had tied more than ninety chains.  These \nwere fastened to my leg with thirty-six locks.  When everything had been \ntested then, at last, the ropes which tied me to my cart were out.  While \nall the people shouted with astonishment I was able to get slowly to my \nfeet and to look around me.\n     I never saw a prettier sight.  The country was like a garden, with \ngreen fields of grass surrounding fields of wheat and vegetables which \nlooked like flower beds.  The roads were no wider than garden paths and \nthe tallest trees were just seven feet high.  At my feet lay the great \ncity of Mildendo, like the painted scene in a doll's theatre.  Thousands \nof handsome little people crowded round me.  They were all less than six \ninches high and the animals were even smaller.  Horses and cows were four \ninches high, sheep only an inch high and the hens and geese looked like \nmany-coloured flies.\n     The King and his family had been watching me from a tower nearby.  \nNow the King came down by himself to look at me more closely.  At first \nhe approached on horseback, but the horse was so frightened that the royal \nrider was almost thrown to the ground.  At last the King got down from his \nhorse and walked round me at a safe distance to examine me on all sides.  \nI was as interested in him as he was in me and lay down in order to see \nhim better.  Putting my face three yards from him I was able to get a good \nview.  The King was half an inch taller than any of his people and his \nface was strong and manly.  His complexion was fair and he held himself \nvery straight while walking with graceful and kingly movements.\n     The uniform he wore was very plain, but his head-dress was made of \ngold and ornamented with jewels and feathers.  The sword, which he held \nready to defend himself if I should escape, was almost three inches long \nand rich with gold and jewels.\n     The King spoke to me several times while we examined each other--and \nI replied--but neither of us could understand the other.  Several of his \npriests and lawyers then tried to speak to me, but although I answered in \nall the many languages I knew, they did not know one of them.\n     After several hours the King and his servants went home, leaving me \nwith a strong guard.  Some of the great crowd of citizens which remained \nwere foolish enough to shoot arrows at me, when they thought I could not \ndefend myself because of my chains.  The Captain of the Guard, however, \nwas quick to prevent this and he seized six of the leaders.  As a punish-\nment he bound the prisoners and gave them to me.  While the crowded \nwatched fearfully I took them all in my right hand and put five of them \nin my coat pocket.  Then I made a terrible face at the sixth as if I meant \nto eat him alive.  The poor man shouted with fear and even the guard \npitied him--especially when I took out my penknife.  But I soon relieved \nthem when, cutting the ropes which bound him, I put him gently on the \nground and let him run away.  One by one I treated the other five \nprisoners in the same way and I could see that the soldiers and people \nwere all pleased by my kindness.  After that no one else tried to hurt me.\n     When the news of my arrival spread through the kingdom of Lilliput \ngreat crowds of people came to see me from every corner of the island.  \nVillages were almost emptied and the farms would have been completely \nneglected if the King had not made a law that no one could visit me more \nthan once.  Meanwhile the King himself could not decide what to do with \nme.  Many of his advisers warned him of the danger of keeping me alive.  \nThey said I might escape and destroy the kingdom with my great strength.  \nEven if I did not escape I would ruin Lilliput if the people had to \nprovide the amount of food I needed.  These advisers wanted to starve me \nor to kill me at once with poisoned arrows."}, {"title": "英文 T37", "content": "     Change on a large scale is often difficult to manage.  The \neconomic development of industrial society caused hard ship and \nsuffering for some groups and individuals whose jobs were threatened.  \nThe storm of rapid change that swept over these people at the \nbeginning of the industrial era uprooted their way of life.  The \nLuddites, for example, were such a group of discontented British \nworkers in the early nineteenth century.  The introduction of labor-\nsaving technology in the textile industry had affected their jobs.  \nThey protested against the lowered wages and unemployment that \nresulted by smashing the new machinery.  Similarly, in the last \nquarter of the twentieth century, electronicing world.  As a result, \nan economic storm is again threatening industrial society.\n     In North America, technological change has been an accepted part \nof economic development.  People invent new machines to replace \nworkers, and therefore jobs are lost.  However, so far the new \ntechnology has created enough new jobs for almost everyone.  Like the \nLuddites today suffer, both are forgotten in the general prosperity.  \nSince the onset of the Industrial Revolution, the economies New but \ndifferent jobs have replaced those jobs that machines have taken over.\n     According to G. G. Schwartz and W. Neikirk, the fear that factory \nworkers would be displaced by machines caused an automation scare in \nthe United States in the 1950s and 1960s.  However, those workers who \nwere displaced found jobs in the service sector, for example in \noffices, banks or government service.  In Schwartz and Neikirk's view, \nthe electronics revolution has now reached sector as well as the \nfactory.  Offices using microcomputers, word processors, and \nphotocopiers can produce much more with other same number displaced \nfactory workers can no longer turn to the service sector for new \njobs.  Both industries and services can now maintain and even increase \nproduction without hiring new workers.\n     Many observers dispute this pessimistic conclusion.  In their \nopinion, increased production will bring lower prices, which will \nallow people to buy additional items.  This increased buying will then \nstimulate more production, and this in turn will create more jobs.  \nHowever, Schwartz and Neikirk argue that people today might instead \nspend their extra money on services that do not add new jobs, or they \nmight buy goods that are made in automated factories.  They conclude \nthat the scenario of the Industrial Revolution may no longer be valid \nfor today's conditions.\n     Let us look more closely at how technological change in indus-\ntrialized societies can involve both a loss of jobs in some sectors \nand a change in the kinds of jobs available to workers.\n     We have already discussed the broad reasons for the loss of jobs.  \nIt is worthwhile, however, to look at specific examples of the \nprocess.  Cordell points to two kinds of change in manufacturing that \naffect employment.  The product itself is being simplified, and the \nmethods of production are becoming automated.  In either case, fewer \npeople are needed to make a product.  As Cordell points out, \"the type \nof production that simplified the product makes it less expensive to \nproduce and sell.  Industries that fail to adopt the new \ntechnologies will be greatly affected.\"  His examples are the Swiss \nwatch industry, where employment fell by 46,000 in the mid-1970s, and \nthe West German watch industry, where jobs decreased by 40 percent at \nthe same time.  An electronic watch requires the assembly of five \ncomponents; a mechanical watch requires one thousand assembly \noperations.  Another example of a simplified product that Cordell \ncites is the staffs by 50 percent.  A second reason for the loss of \njobs is the use of microelectronics in all phased of industry.  New \nplants are being built that use robots instead of people.  \nCordell's example is the Yamazaki factory at Nagoya, Japan, which would \nhave needed 2,500 workers without microelectronics and robots.  With \nthis technology, however, it will need only 215 workers.\n     While some jobs will disappear as a result of these changes, \nothers will change, and still others will be created.  The new jobs, \nhowever, will not be of the same types as the old ones.  As Cordell \nsuggests, machines are becoming \"smarter,\" so workers will not need to \nknow as much to do their work.  This is known as de-skilling.  Because \nof automation, more and more jobs will require unskilled labor, and \njobs for semiskilled workers will disappear.  On the other hand, the \nnew technologies may create employment for people who design and \nprogram machines, or those who perform professional services.  These \njobs will require a high degree of knowledge and training.  Consequently \nthe job market is becoming polarized, for unskilled workers will no \nlonger be able to become semiskilled workers by acquiring experience.  \nOnly training and knowledge will enable a person to perform a skilled \njob.  The will create an unbridgeable gap between the skilled and \nunskilled members of the work force.\n     Cordell notes that these changes in the conditions of work in \nindustrial societies can lead to important changes in the social \nstructures and attitudes in these societies.  He concludes that \"the \nfuture of work and working in the evolving information society is \nsubject to much debate.  The new technologies will change the nature \nof work, the place of work, and the importance of work to society.\"  \nIn his view, workers will have to share jobs and income, for there \nwill not be enough jobs for everyone.  To maintain an economy, \nmembers of an society must have the income to be consumers.  However, \nCordell points out that economic changes might be easier to achieve \nthan other changes.  Instead of learning how to make a living, people \nwill need education in how to live.  People will have to change their \nattitudes about work.  They will have to learn not to see value in the \nidea of work itself.  And they will have to learn not to regard paid \nwork as more important than voluntary work.\n     Industrial nations seem to be in transition from societies based \non work to societies in which the meaning of with a minimum of \ndisruption to the individuals who need society's protection.\n     Genetic engineering is such a new field that its potential is \nonly beginning to be understood.  Ever since the first product of \ngenetic engineering-human insulin-was tested in 1988, the new \ntechnology has reached into many diverse fields.  Many companies that \nhave used genetic engineering have begun to make significant profits.  \nIn fact, genetic engineering is rapidly becoming the most important \nform of engineering.  Great advances are already being made in such \nfields as food production, industry, and medicine.\n     The use of biogenetic technology in food production can be \ndivided roughly into two main categories:  genetic agriculture and \ngenetic food processing.  The application of genetic engineering to \nagriculture can be further subdivided into land-based techniques and \ntechniques of aquaculture and mariculture.  Many of the most recent \ntechniques of land-based agriculture are important in animal farming, \nbut some apply mainly to vegetable farming.  A few techniques are \nuseful to both.  Animal farming already uses the process of in vitro \nfertilization to increase the number of offspring.  In this process, \nscientists remove the egg from the female's body and fertilize it with \nsperm from the male.  After a few days, the egg is placed in the \nuterus grows to term and has a normal birth.  Another process that \nfarmers and scientists may eventually use to produce offspring is \ncloning.  In cloning, offspring develop from the cells of one parent \nonly and become physically identical to that parent.  Although it may \nbe many years before it become some simpler creatures such as frogs.  \nFarther into the future, researchers hope to use a process called cell \nfusion, or selfing, to produce offspring in a way that rapidly then be \ncorrected by genetic engineering.  And research is already producing \nnew or improved species of cattle and other farm animals.\n     Genetically engineered species will also benefit fruit and \nvegetable farming, which has made use of cloning and species mixing \nfor many years.  The relatively simple process of grafting enables \nfarmers to clone plants or to produce hybrids that are ideally suited \nto local conditions.  This technique brought about the so-called Green \nRevolution, which greatly increased the food production of many Third \nWorld countries.  The next major genetic breakthrough in cereal crop \nproduction will perhaps be a new species of wheat that does not \nrequire expensive fertilizer.\n     Unlike genetic agriculture, genetic food processing has used \nbiotechnology for centuries.  People have used yeasts to ferment foods \nfor at least six thousand years.  Today genetic food technology has \ntwo main purposes; to create the modern sweeteners, flavors, and \nfragrances of mass-produced convenience foods; and to develop new \nkinds of foods.  Scientists have found ways not only to improve the \nuser of yeasts but also to create new sources of nutrition.  In the \nnear future, scientists expect to use the new technology to create \ncompletely new kinds of food.  Already they have created a nutritional \nsubstance called single-cell protein, which is commonly added to \nanimal feeds.\n     Genetic techniques have found applications in at least two other \nimportant fields; the mining industry and pollution control.  Miners \ncould use genetically produced bacteria with appetites for heavy \nmetals and oil to extract these minerals from mines and wells.\n     However important genetic engineering may be for industry, its \neffects are likely to be still greater in the field of medicine.  \nMedical biotechnology is providing both new medical substances and \nnew medical techniques.  Among the substances, the most important \nhave been drugs, enzymes, and hormones.  Human insulin, a hormone \nproduced by genetically engineered bacteria, became, in 1988, the \nfirst genetically engineered product to be licensed for human use.  The \nincluded the human growth hormone, which is helpful in treat in certain \nkinds of growth defects in children, and an improved version of the \nantibiotic penicillin.  Eventually, new antibiotics have learned how to \nuse biotechnology to produce cortisone cheaply and in large quantities.  \nDoctors use this substance to treat arthritis and several other \ndisorders.  To control pain, genetic engineering has yielded a group \nof chemicals called endorphins.  These are twelve hundred morphine, \nthey are very safe to use, improved version of the enzyme L-asparaginase, \na chemical used to treat leukemia.\n     As the advances in biotechnology occur at an ever increasing \nrate, public attitudes toward recombinant DNA technology will have \ntrouble keeping up with the latest knowledge.  Polls indicate that a \nsignificant number of people have little understanding of current \nbiotechnology.  And the breakthroughs are coming with such speed that \neducation and the media will have difficulty keeping the public \ninformed.  A widening gap between scientific knowledge and public \nunderstanding could, at the very least, have serious ethical and legal \nconsequences."}, {"title": "英文 T38", "content": "     Western Europe is already sick of its poor relations.  This summer, \nthousands of holidaymakers from Poland and Czechoslovakia crammed \nonto dilapidated tour buses and headed west for a break, much to the \nchagrin of local hoteliers, the cashstrapped visitors also used their \nbuses as dormitories.  Nor did restaurateurs fare much better.  Fearing \nwestern European prices, the tourists brought their own food with them.\n     Dubbed \"Trabant tourists\" in a slighting reference to the infamous \nEast German box-shaped car, the visitors received a frosty welcome.  Paris \nmayor Jacques Chirac ordered police to clear out those sleeping in the \ncity's parks.  The exasperated keeper of the Notre Dame souvenir kiosk \ncommented, \"They don't buy less than other tourists; they simply don't \nbuy at all.\"\n     After protracted discussions, U.S. and Philippine negotiators agreed \non a proposal allowing the U.S. military to use its bases for then more \nyears.  Under the terms of the agreement, the U.S.A. would have given $23 \nmillion in annual aid.  In addition, President Bush promised to provide \nsurplus military equipment and medical supplies.\n     However, a majority of Philippine senators joined forces to throw \nout the new treaty.  They argued that the aid figure was almost insult-\ningly low.  Equally, the very presence of American forces on Philippine \nsoil rankled them as an infringement of national sovereignty.  Even so, \nthe Philippines' poverty could ultimately leave it at the mercy of East \nAsia's economic giants.  Countries like Japan, concerned about regional \nsecurity, are continuing to use their financial leverage to urge the \nPhilippines to extend the bases' lease.\n     The DPP's draft constitution for a \"Republic of Taiwan\" has quickly \nfallen afoul of KMT criticism.  Legislators called the proposed consti-\ntution \"infantile,\" arguing that it would merely fuel tension across \nthe Taiwan Straits.  Huang Chu-wen, leader of the KMT's Wisdom Coalition \ncautioned that with the R.O.C. already standing on the threshold of \nhistoric constitutional reform, this was the wrong time to rock the boat.\n     At nearly 90 years of age, W. Eduards Deming is one of the seminal \nforces in world business.  He is the man credited with remaking the me-\naning of the words \"Made in Japan.\"\n     Deming is the person who taught quality control to Japanese industry.  \nOver a 40-year period, he has taught his management philosophy to thou-\nsands of Japanese executives, engineers and business people.  The emperor \nhimself presented a medal to Deming in acknowledgment of the hefty con-\ntribution he made to Japan's postwar recovery.  A prestigious annual in-\ndustrial quality award \"The Deming Prize\" was named after him.\n     You can see the results of Dr. Deming's teaching in the glittering ar-\nrays of consumer goods all around you marked \"Made in Japan.\"  Oh, he's the \nperson responsible for all of this, Yes.  In the United States, the Malcolm \nBaldridge National Quality Award was inspired by the success of the Deming \nPrize.\n     The product may be stamped \"Made in Japan,\" but the techniques for \ncreating the quality were made in the U.S.A..  It is the result of applying \na management philosophy which might be called a \"Deming Doctrine.\"  It \nhas 14 points in all but boils down to a single key word:  quality.\n     According to Deming, quality is the key to success.  Not cost, quotas \nor faster production.  Quality.  Furthermore, quality isn't expensive.  \nDesigning a manufacturing system to produce quality actually lowers costs \nand increases productivity.\n     Deming's philosophy trashes some hallowed icons of American manage-\nment practice:  merit bonuses, performance reviews, management by object \nand (that cherished old wheeze) \"competition is the secret to better \nservice and lower costs.\"\n     Not so, according to Deming.  Quality itself is what lowers costs.  \nBy building quality into the system, you eliminate waste in the form of \nrejects.  And you end up with a product customers are eager to buy.\n     Deming's philosophy stems from the work of a man who influenced him \nenormously.  A Bell labs scientist named Walter A. Shewart developed a \nnew way of using statistics to control manufacturing processes.  Deming \nchampioned Shewart's ideas to anyone who would listen.  The basics are \nquite simple.  All processes suffer from some degree of variation.  By \nusing statistical analyses, you can measure these variations and find \nout what causes them.  Then by eliminating the causes of the variations \nyou improve the quality of the finished product by making sure it comes \nout right to begin with.\n     Obvious, perhaps, but not common practice.  The usual method was to \ninspect the finished product and throw out the subjects.  This idea drives \nDeming right up the wall.  According to him, if you're inspecting the \nfinished product, you're too late.  The damage has already been done.\n     The bottom-line types in the U.S. loved \"planned obsolescence.\"  \nNobody cared to listen to Dr. Deming, or his 14 points, or his ideas \nabout quell.\n     But the Japanese were listening.  Rebuilding industries that had been \nflattened during the war, and with a huge population to feed, they were \ndesperate to find a way to make their products saleable in the world \nmarketplace.  They were well aware that the words \"Made in Japan\" evoked \nthe image of ticky-tacky paper flowers that opened when you dropped them \nin water.\n     In 1950, the Union of Japanese Scientists and Engineers invited Deming \nto Japan because of his reputation for using statistics to improve the \nquality of data at the United States Census Bureau, where he had been \nworking.\n     Deming assured his hosts they could use statistical analyses to im-\nprove their manufacturing, and if they concentrated on producing products \nof high quality, they would find a ready market for them around the world.\n     The rest, as they say, is history.  So historical, in fact, that even \nAmerican industry, tired of taking a shellacking from Japanese competitors, \nis starting to listen to Dr. Deming's ideas about quality.  The coveted new \nMalcolm Baldridge National Quality Award proves it.\n     And listening they are.  Deming is now one of the most sought-after \nconsultants in American business, which is eager now to play catch-up-ball \nwith Japanese.\n     A checklist of management must for creating quality.  In his book out \nof Crisis, published by MIT, Dr. Deming elaborates on his famous 14 mana-\ngerial points.  Some of the most important:  Purchase materials on the \nbasis of quality, not cost.  Don't let low bid be the basis for choice.  \nEstablish long-term ties with suppliers who provide the best material.  \nDon't rely on inspections to provide quality control.  Instead, improve and \ncontrol the manufacturing process itself.  Use statistical evidence not \nmanagerial judgment to upgrade the system.\n     Eliminate numerical quotas as the benchmark for success.  You can \nsacrifice quality striving to meet quotas.  And a quota creates a mental \ncutoff point.  Make quality itself the goal.  Eliminate barriers between \ndepartments so that, for example, the sales department isn't designing pro-\nducts the factory can't produce.  Encourage teamwork, not competition.  Get \nrid of merit bonuses and ratings that encourage bickering and backstabbing.\n     Provide managers and workers with all the training and retraining they \nneed to participate in the improvement process.\n     Finally, but most important of all, management must commit itself to a \nthat the customer and the level of customer satisfaction are the most \ncommit itself to a continuing and never ending quest for quality, based \non the realization that the customer the level of customer satisfaction \nare the most critical elements in the entire production cycle.\n     Quality and productivity are everybody's job, but management must \nlead in establishing the pursuit of quality as a corporate goal and in \ncharting good strategies for continuous improvement and as our venerable \nfounder came to the chapter which deals with Scooge's change of heart and \ncharity to his clerk's family, he broke down in tears and vowed that he \nwould never have the reputation of a Scrooge.\n     The gruff yet polished voice droned on.  Sitting around the boardroom \nsolid oak table, the 12 partners of Benson and Benson Law Offices tried \nhard not to doze off.  A few too many generous glasses of cognac made the \ntask difficult.\n     \"Josiah Benson stipulated in his will that every Christmas Eve, \nBenson and Benson would randomly select a needy family in this city of \nLondon and give them a turkey.  Today, that honor has fallen to our \nyoungest partner, Michael.\"\n     This announcement stirred a halfhearted ripple of applause.  To \nthe senior partners, Michael, with his Gucci shoes and riviera tan, was a \nyuppie, not a gentleman.  His aggressive selfpromotion did not go down \nwell.  There was no doubt, however, that his dynamism had dragged the \ndusty old firm into the modern age with a corresponding upswing in profits.\n     \"This wretched turkey,\" Michael hissed under his breath.  It was six \no'clock, and he had been stuck in traffic for over an hour.  He'd never set \nfoot in east London before.  To Michael, it was one of those places you \navoided for fear of being mugged.\n     He glanced at his watch.  His girlfriend, Natasha, expected him \nfor dinner at her parents' country mansion at seven.  Blocked by yet \nanother red light, he dreamed of what she might be warming tonight her \nlong, silk skirts from Bond Street, her Pierre Cardin accessories, the \nenticing hint of Chaneel No.5.  And of the presents heaped around the tree, \nsteaming plum pudding, and Natasha's adoring smile...\n     Am impatient honk jolted him out of his reverie.  He did not belong \nhere.  At least he had found the address.  Fishing the turkey out of his \nBMW's trunk, Michael checked the card his boss had given him."}, {"title": "英文 T39", "content": "     There can be few destinations more appealing and exotic than Nepal's \nKathmandu Valley.  A fertile plain, ringed by mist-shrouded mountains, set \nin the flanks of the snowy Himalayas, the valley encompasses three royal \ncities of unworldly beauty.  Here, in the only Hindu kingdom on-earth, the \ncultural traditions of South and Central Asia meet and merge in a rich, \nfascinating and uniquely Nepalese whole.\n     Most travelers to Nepal arrive by air at the bright, new terminus of \nTribhuwan International Airport, just five kilometers east of Kathmandu \ncity center.  But you can choose to make the harder (and more adventurous) \njourney overland from the Indian border at Birganj.  In either case, your \nfirst experience of the country--before starting on a trek into the steep \nmountain valleys, or heading west to relax by Pokhara's Lake Phewa--is \nlikely to be of Kathmandu Valley.\n     This is as it should be; Kathmandu is more than just a point of \narrival; it is an experience in its own right, a destination few visitors \ncan ever forget.\n     According to legend, Kathmandu Valley was once a holy lake, encircled \nby mountains.  To this body of sacred water, inhabited by giant serpents \nand nags, came the first Buddha who threw a lotus seed into the lake.  The \nseed sprouted and burst into a lotus flower with a thousand petals.  When \nthe Buddha saw the beauty of the flower, he caused the hills to part, \ndraining the lake and forming a valley, so that the lotus would always be \nprotected by a rim of mountains.\n     Many years later a Buddhist monk came to settle in the valley, where \nhe built a tall pagoda with a gilded spire.  This became the central stupa \nof Swayambhunath, which stands on a hill just west of the city of \nKatmandu, marking the site where the first lotus flower bloomed.  To this \nday, the people of Kathmandu believe, the spire of Swayambhunath radiates \na sacred light that protects the entire valley.\n     Swayambhunath, set on a conical hill, provides a fine vantage to see \nthe expanse of Kathmandu Valley.  From its sacred steps, looking east and \nsouth, you can see broad valley vistas, from the snaking Bagmati River to \nthe pagodas of distant Bhaktapur.\n     Kathmandu is an enigma.  It seems caught in mediaeval times, yet it \noffers a wide variety of regional and international cuisine.  First-class \nhotels compete with an array of mid-priced guest houses in the popular \ntourist district of Thamel--yet if you walk five minutes in any direction \nyou'll find yourself in another city, rich in the smell of spices and as \nold as time.  This is the real Kathmandu:  an unexpected, extravagant mix-\nture of people and religion, child goddesses, barefoot porters and sacred \ncows.\n     The Kathmandu most people come to see is the \"Old City\", a tangled \nnetwork of narrow alleys, stores and temples located around central durbar \nsquare.  The rambling Hanuman Dhoka, or royal palace, takes up most of the \neastern side of the square.  It was built almost four centuries ago by \nNepal's Malla kings; today only a small part of the complex is open to the \ngeneral public.\n     At the left of the entrance stands a statue of Hanuman, the monkey god \nof Hindu mythology, after which the palace is named.  Just within the main \ncourtyard is a likeness of Narasimha, the half-man, half-lion incarnation \nof Vishnu, disemboweling a demon.  Directly in front of the gates stands a \nlarge, tiered pagoda dedicated to Jagannath, a manifestation of Vishnu as \nLord of the Universe, bearing an inscription dated \"1564\".  Clearly, you \nare within the royal precincts of a Hindu kingdom.\n     On the southern side of the square you'll find the ornate doorway to \nthe Kumari Bahal.  This entrance leads to one of the loveliest and most \nelegant courtyards in the capital.  All four sides are lavishly decorated \nwith wooden fretwork windows, ornamental brackets and carved doorways.\n     This is the home of Nepal's living goddess, the Kumari.  According to \ntradition, a virgin female child of the Shakya community, carrying appro-\npriate auspicious signs, is chosen to become the Kumari and carried in \nstate to her new home at the Kumari Bahal.  Here she remains as a living \ngoddess, appearing in state to bless the people of Kathmandu and the royal \nfamily on festive occasions or at important religious ceremonies.\n     As soon as she reaches puberty, she ceases to be considered divine, \nand is returned to normal life.  Meanwhile, another little girl will have \nbeen chosen to become the Kumari, extending continuing protection and \nblessing over the Kingdom of Nepal.\n     Passing back into Durbar Square, directly opposite the Kumari Bahal, \nis a gorgeous temple dedicated to the lovers Shiva and Parvati.  Statues \nof the divine couple stand at a first-floor window, gazing down onto the \ncrowded square as though watching those passing by.\n     Nearby stands the huge Kasthamandap, the oldest building in Kathmandu, \nfrom which the name of the city is derived.  Other Hindu elements closely \nassociated with this central area are an octagonal temple dedicated to \nKrishna, and--of abiding interest--a four-metre-high figure of Kalabhairab, \na manifestation of Shiva as the terrifying \"Lord of Time\".  This figure is \nrenowned throughout Kathmandu Valley as a true test of honesty:  Local \npeople are convinced that its is impossible to tell a lie in Kalabhairab's \npresence.\n     Before leaving the durbar square, take note of a stone inscription \nset in the outer wall of the royal palace just to the side of the main \nentrance.  Carved in 1664 by King Pratap Malla, who was clearly something \nof a linguist, the text includes 15 languages (including two words in \nFrench and one in English), and is supposed to be a poem to the goddess \nKali.  According to legend, should anyone manage to translate the entire \npoem correctly, milk will gush from the spout in the middle of the \ninscription.\n     However, intoxicating though the durbar square may be, Kathmandu's \nappeal is not limited to palaces and religious monuments.  Immediately \nsouth of the square, lying behind the Kasthamandap, lies the quarter known \nto the Nepalese as Jhochhen, and to a generation of young western \ntravelers as \"Freak Street\".  Once the haunt of wide-eyed hippies high on \na strange mixture of marijuana and mysticism, the area has been cleaned up \nsince King Birendra came to the throne in 1972, and \"sightings\" of genuine, \nold-fashioned hippies have become rare indeed.  Still, a walk through \nJhochhen is a stroll down memory lane, where designer cafes and \"pie shops\" \nstrive to recreate the atmosphere of the late '60s.\n     Ganga Path is the place to go for shopping. Fine handicrafts are on \nsale everywhere, including statues of deities, cast in brass and bronze or \ncarved in scented woods.  Thanka, Tibetan religious paintings, are endur-\ningly popular--though genuinely antique specimens are hard to find.  Hand-\nwoven textiles in linen, cotton and silk are everywhere, piled high on \ncounters, and Tibetan, Chinese and Nepalese carpets hang from walls and \ndoors.  The Nepalese are renowned for their jewellery-making skills, and \nthe gold and silver rings, necklaces and bracelets of the Patan smiths \ncarry intricate traceries that shown a complete mastery of the art.\n     Kathmandu may be the largest town in Nepal and capital of the realm, \nbut it is only one of three \"royal cities\" clustered at the heart of \nKathmandu Valley.  Just to the south of the capital directly across the \nBagmati River, Patan--also known as \"Lalitpur\", or \"city of beauty\"--is the \nsecond largest town in the valley.  While Kathmandu provides a unique com-\nbination of Hinduism and Buddhism both in its architecture and in its daily \nlife, Patan has a distinctly Buddhist flavour.\n     Easily reached by taxi, bicycle or on foot, Patan is best visited on \nday trips from Kathmandu; hotels here are markedly inferior to those of the \ncapital.  Accommodation aside, Patan is a treat for the senses--visual, \nspiritual and (in the spice market, at least) olfactory.  It's literally \npacked with historical monuments.\n    Where Patan is Buddhist, Bhaktapur--\"city of the pious\"--is predomi-\nnantly Hindu.  Located about 16 km east of Kathmandu on the old trade route \nbetween India and Tibet, Bhaktapur is the least modernised of the three \nroyal cities.  Strict controls ensure that new buildings are rarely con-\nstructed, and then only in an appropriate style.  Motor traffic rarely \nenters Bhaktapur.\n     The city was devastated by a powerful earthquake in 1934; since that \ntime dedicated work by the Nepalese-German Bhaktapur Development Project \nhas restored much that was damaged or destroyed, and at the same time pro-\ntected the city from unrestrained development.  Bhaktapur is an essential \nexperience--a trip back to mediaeval Nepal.\n     Finally, no visit to Kathmandu Valley would be complete without a \nvisit to Bodhnath, also known as Bauha, the largest and most important \nTibetan Buddhist monument outside Tibet.  Set squarely in the siddhi--a \nzone of supernatural power where guardian deities dwell and wishes are \ngranted--Bodhnath is just 6 km east of Kathmandu, along the old trade road \nto Lhasa. Known to the Tibetans as Chorten Chempo, or \"Great Stupa\", it has \nbeen the Mecca of Tibetans in exile since the occupation of their homeland \nin 1950."}, {"title": "英文 T40", "content": "     On the book jacket, author John Naisbitt is said to be \"one of the \nworld's top social forecasters\".  What's more, says the blurb, Naisbitt's \nfirst book, Megatrends, \"accurately predicted 10 major patterns of change \nwhich would shape the world in the 1980s\".\n     But away from the context of book jackets and promotional blurbs, \nNaisbitt, the man, brushes side any notion that he is a modern-day prophet.\n     \"People think I'm talking about the future, but my books are not about \npredicting what lies ahead.  Rather I see myself as an observer of global \ntrends and a teacher.\"\n     \"Not many people have the interest to see what's going on in the \nworld, what are the big shifts taking place and where, and to study what \nthe connections are.  It's actually hard work and painstaking research,\" \nhe notes.\n     But the returns have been worth the trouble.  Naisbitt has since \ndeveloped the megatrends franchise into a lucrative book formula--the \noriginal Megatrends, written in 1982, has spawned Megatrends 2000 and \nMegatrends Women, among others.  His latest and eighth book highlights \neight developments in Asia that will position the region as the centre of \nthe world.  It's appropriately titled Mgeatrends Asia.\n     Naisbitt, like many other thinkers, believes that an Asian renaissance \nis dawning.  In his book, the new Asia will be a network of nations working \ntogether for mutual economic gain.  The old Asia was divided by culture, \nlanguage, political ideology, religious philosophies an geography.  The new \nAsia, he believes, will be forged by economic integration, technology--in \nparticular telecommunications--and intra-Asia mobility.\n     The eight big sociological shifts listed in Megatrends Asia focus on \nan evolution--of networks from nation states; of a consumer-driven economy \nfrom an export-led economy; of Asian values from Western values; of market-\ndriven trade from government-controlled trade; of supercities from \nvillages; of high-tech industries from labour-intensive industries; of \nwomen as a societal force from the traditional male-dominated society; and \nthe shifting of the global axis of influence from the West to the East.\n     \"What's happening in Asia is by far the most important development in \nthe world today.  Nothing else comes close,\" says Naisbitt .  \"The \neconomic growth of Asia will drive the global economy to the benefit \nof all as we move through the balance of this century.  The Moder-\nnisation of Asia--as opposed to the westernisation of Asia--will reshape \nthe world as we move towards the next millennium.\"\n     But Naisbitt is more than rhetoric.  He's well grounded in his \nknowledge of the region, and his book is well researched and backed up with \ncredible examples and statistics.\n     A political scientist by training, Naisbitt first came to Asia in 1967 \nas advisor to the Thai government on accelerating agricultural growth in \nthe northeastern part of Thailand.  Since then, he has been a regular visi-\ntor, well regarded by various Asian governments.  Malaysia's think tank, \nthe Institute of Strategic and International Studies, has made him a \nDistinguished International Fellow of the institute.\n     Reflecting on his first impressions of the region, Naisbitt says:  \n\"Thirty years ago, Thailand was what you'd call the third world in the old \nvocabulary.  Just look at how far Bangkok has come today.\"\n     And as the region has grown, so have the people.  \"Asians are today \nmuch more assertive and confident.  After years of playing second-fiddle \nto the West, they now have a real sense of self and a sense of destiny,\" \nhe notes.\n     Naisbitt says it is the new Asians--primarily the overseas Chinese \nentrepreneurs--who will drive and fuel the growth or the region.  He's put \nhis money squarely on the increasing trade links between overseas Chinese \nand the hitherto slumbering dragon, China, as the key to Asia's future \nprosperity.\n     \"The metaphor for the overseas Chinese network is the Internet,\" he \npoints out.  \"Like the 'Net, the overseas Chinese network is totally \ndecentralised; no one is in charge, everyone is at the centre.  People \noperate out off self-interest and cooperate with each other with the aim \nto mutually profit.\n     \"When I'm on the Internet in my mountain home in Tellurid, Colorado, \nI experience being in the centre of the 'Net.  What is so powerful about \nthe Internet is that each of the 50 million people experience the same \nthing.  Similarly that's what makes the 57 million overseas Chinese (54 \nmillion of whom are in Asia) network so powerful.\"\n     No discussion on Asia today would be complete without the controver-\nsial debate over Asian values--a topic described by many Western opinion-\nmakers as a \"red herring\" or \"fire blanket\" or issues such as democracy and \nhuman rights.  Naisbitt injects his perspective:  \"Hearing the debate over \nAsian values reminds me of the worlds of Marshall McLuhan, a famous Cana-\ndian sociologist.  He said he did not know who discovered water but he was \nsure it was not a fish.  When you're on the inside, you can't see the \nforest for the trees.\n     \"Looking from the outside, it's so clear to me what Asian values are:  \nthey're about family, hard work, thrift, and the importance of a good edu-\ncation for the children.  That's not radically different from the West and \nelsewhere.\"\n     The difference, he notes, lies in the execution.  \"People live their \nbeliefs and values in different ways, to different intensities, and they \nhave different fidelities to their values.  Asians truly live family \nvalues.\"\n     In Naisbitt's opinion, the welfare state in the West has gone a long \nway toward undermining qualities such as hard work, self reliance, and \npersonal and family responsibilities.  \"That's an unintended consequence.  \nAsians now have a model that the West never had; so they can learn from \nit,\" he adds.\n     \"Some politicians do raise Asian values as a blanket to shut up \ndebate on issues such as democracy, human rights and so on.  But the point \nis, democracy is not a neat product that you buy or don't buy.  It is a \nprocess that involves everyone.  You can't rush it; it has a life and speed \nof development of its own and that differs from country to country.\"\n     Naisbitt observes that Asia is moving from traditions to options.  \n\"The more Asian value are challenged by change, the more Asians are \nlearning about what those values are and what they mean.\"\n     Interestingly, Naisbitt does not count Japan as an Asian country.  \n(\"It's part of the G7 yet it is not part of the West; it's in a class by \nitself.\")  He argues that Japan's economic dominance has peaked and unless \nit reinvents itself, the country is heading on a downward slide.\n     \"What Japan has achieved is unbelievable.  But the world has changed, \nand so has Japan, which is currently in a spiritual crisis.  The generation \nthat built the economy of Japan is now retiring.  They worked hard and were \nextremely dedicated.  Unfortunately, their successors are mere caretakers \nwho are not willing to take risks.  The world has since gone on; the young \ngeneration have different values, they are not willing to sacrifice their \nlives on the altar of commerce.\"\n     It's clear that Megatrends Asia is written for a Western audience.  \nSays Naisbitt:  \"The majority of people in the West don't have a clue about \nwhat's going on in Asia.  Indeed, there are far greater numbers of people \nin positions of power and influence in Asia who know about the West than \nthere are in the West who know about the East.\"\n     But for all that, Naisbitt maintains that he wrote the book from an \nAsian point of view.\n     \"After working to get a sense of how Asians feel and think, I some-\ntimes feel I've become Chinese.  Indeed, in the process of studying global \ntrends and changes, I've become a free-floating citizen of the world.  \nAfter all, when you come to think of it, people are more similar than they \nare different.  But we tend to focus on the differences because they're \nmore interesting.\""}, {"title": "英文 T41", "content": "     He is part priest, part scientist and part interior designer.  He will \nwill work for anyone from the humblest hawker to the grandest millionaire.  \nThere are centuries of tradition behind his art.  And as technology makes \nmore inroads into modern living, even the feng shui master, a seemingly \ntradition-bound figure, is moving with the times.\n     The modern practitioner is more likely to be wearing a Versace suit \nthan traditional robes.  He will carry a portable phone in one hand and his \ncompass, or lo p'an in the other.  And for many masters a computer is al-\nmost as important for calculations and analysis as his \"ten-thousand-year \ncalendar\".  The ancient art is getting a facelift.\n     Skeptics may dismiss it as hocus-pocus but feng shui (literally \nwind-water), or geomancy, is a thriving multimillion dollar business.  \nProperty developers pay homage--not to mention hundreds of thousands of \ndollars--to the art of balancing physical and spiritual forces, as do the \nbusinessmen and other believers.  Even the Hong Kong Government is prag-\nmatic about its existence--the 1994 report by the Director of Audit \nrevealed that HK$515,000 (US$67,000) was paid out for two tun fu ceremonies \nbecause local villagers were upset that construction projects in the New \nTerritories had unbalanced the feng shui.\n     British retailer Marks and Spencer also subscribes to the principles \nand practice of geomancy.  Lucky gold coins are buried is strategic \npositions beneath the floors of all Marks and Spencer stores in Hong Kong.  \nFloor plans are drawn up in consultation with geomancy experts to ensure \nthat the buildings are feng shui friendly, which in turn will promote a \ncontinuous ringing of the cash registers.\n     The design of Hong Kong's five-star Regent Hotel was governed by feng \nshui.  Proponents of the art believed that the hotel was blocking the path \nof the nine dragons of Kowloon as they made their way from the mountains to \nthe sea for their morning bath.  To counter this, a fountain was construct-\ned at the main entrance of the hotel and floor-to-ceiling windows replaced \nthe solid walls at the side of the building facing the waters.\n     The rationale was that the dragons would be lured to the hotel by the \nfountain, which symbolised a pearl.  Having snatched the pearl, the \ncreatures would march through the hotel lobby and through the windows to \nthe sea, but only after making generous deposits of wealth at the hotel \nreception.\n     For the unbeliever these are fanciful notions.  But support for the \nidea of feng shui comes from the most prosaic organizations.  Secretary-\ngeneral of the International Urban Development Association, Michael \nSudarkis, says:  \"The cultural element of feng shui has a sound principle, \nwhich is to try to accommodate man and nature.  This is one of the lessons \nwe [the West] could learn.  The Chinese have a cultural instinct for living \nin the community and, therefore, to organize space, but alway with nature.  \nIndeed, the first settlement in the world to have a master plan was a town \nin China.\"\n     Lai Kar-fai, who works in the science department of a Hong Kong Uni-\nversity would be expect to have an explanation for all natural phenomena.  \nYet he, too, is a believer.  When family pressure mounted on him to get \nmarried, he ignored the small ads and introductions by friends and went \nstraight to the feng shui man instead.\n     \"I believe in destiny and it mutability,\" he says.  \"I don't believe \nthat we are born with a defined destiny and I wanted to know what could be \ndone to find a wife.\"\n     Not content to let the augur do his job, Lai installed a program in \nhis computer which checked the compatibility of potential brides.  By \nmarrying the ancient art with new tech he is echoing a trend in feng shui.  \nScores of Taiwanese computer software companies have designed various \nprograms for feng shui and astrology buffs.  Instead of shunning such \ndevices as gimmicks, many feng shui masters have incorporated technology \ninto their repertoire.  After all, most younger practitioners are computer-\nliterate and would be lost without the modern necessities of life, such as \na mobile telephone.\n     \"Technology can help us up to a point,\" says Kwong Wai-hung, a feng \nshui and divination master.  \"There are various calculations which can be \nperformed more accurately and faster using a software program, but I would \nnever trust a computer to perform any type of analysis because it is not a \nliving entity.  Prediction depends upon the experience of years.\n     \"It is easier to use the software, especially if I have to prepare a \nnumber of horoscopes.  It saves time and reduces the human error factor \nbecause you just plug in the necessary information and the computer comes \nup with the numbers.\"\n     One of Kwong's clients is a prominent textile magnate heading his own \nconsortium.  His group's profits began sliding and Kwong was called in to \nfix the problem.  Here, he points out, is the quintessential case of \nexperience and tradition winning over technology--the site of the textile \nmagnate's office was facing a massive land reclamation and construction \nproject and that was disturbing the feng shui.  Kwong told the businessman \nthat he would have to relocate his entire head office.  The tycoon moved to \na new office with sweeping views of the harbour.  Profits began to rise \nagain.\n     Despite the convenience of computer software, Kwong still swears by \nhis traditional ten-thousand-year calendar.  The calendar not only helps \nhim calculate the four numbers of a client's birth date but also allows him \nto analyse them, something which the computer cannot do--yet.  For absolute \naccuracy, Kwong seeks help from another scientific source--the local \nobservatory--for precise information on astronomical positions at an \nperson's time of birth.\n     Kwong will use technology when it helps him but he is no slave to it.  \nHe insists that all mobile telephones and pagers be switched off while he \nis working on a reading--the vibrations generated by electronic and tele-\ncommunications equipment could affect the accuracy of his geomancer's \ncompass, the lo p'an.\n     For feng shui master Chan Ho-yan it is a case of have lo p'an will \ntravel.  Chan regards the compass as an essential tool and is never without \nit.  But in a bow to technology, he acknowledges he has used software for \nascertaining the four birth reference points.  He has also incorporated the \nuse of crystals in his practice--this new age addition is prompted by the \nbelief that crystals possess various beneficial properties.\n     When called to perform feng shui calculations, Chan usually packs \ninstruments to measure electromagnetic signals and, in one case, levels of \nradiation.\n     \"Modern technology has affected the art of feng shui,\" he says.  \"We \nare dealing with invisible forces.  A satellite TV receiver receives \nsignals--that affects feng shui.  So I use other instruments to check \nbefore using the lo p'an to ensure as accurate a reading as possible.\"\n     Not all feng shui and divination masters have taken to technology.  \nEschewing the trend is Lam Ping-Nam who studied with successive masters in \nChina before setting out on his own.  He is a proponent of the traditional \n(if somewhat slower) methods of augury.  Lam maintains that any feng shui \nmaster worth his salt uses his experience to compensate for any incorrect \nreadings arising from disturbances in natural forces.  \"I do not use soft-\nware programs to calculated horoscopes.  I do not believe that it is any \nfaster than using the traditional calendar.  I just use the compass when \nlooking at feng shui,\" he says.\n     Just as today's seers have changed with the times by adopting and \nadapting to technology, it is now rare to find a feng shui master dressed \nin the traditional robes.  More often than not, feng shui masters are clad \nin a designer suit.\n     Audrey Norton, an aficionado of feng shui, recalls how disappointed \nshe was when a feng shui master turned up to check her new apartment.  He \nwas dressed like a bank executive.  \"The fellow was in a business suit and \nspent half of the time taking readings with his compass and the other half \ntalking on his mobile telephone.  I was expecting more ceremony so it was \nall rather disappointing,\" she says.\n     \"I think that employing technology in this area goes a long way to de-\nmystifying everything.  In a way it is good because it puts modern science \nto work with something which many believe has no scientific basis but it \nalso takes the fun out of it.\"\n     Advertising professional Sally Chow also misses the mysticism.  \nSoftware programs are too scientific, she observes.\n     \"The ability to read feng shui is like second sight, a gift which one \nhas or one hasn't,\" she says.  \"It's intuitive.  I would be very skeptical \nabout a feng shui man who used a software program to run his readings.  I'd \nwant a second opinion.\"\n     But for a growing number of followers the ancient art needs a shot of \nyouth.\n     \"Why not use computers or whatever type of technology is available so \nthat predictions can be more precise?\" says 78-year-old grandmother Chan \nLai-Keun.  \"That way I can be better informed as to what the new year holds \nand can make appropriate arrangements.\""}, {"title": "英文 T42", "content": "     Going through the paper this morning, I came on a story that said that \na large urban development in the New Jersey meadows near New York City \nwould not upset the ecology of the region.  A few pages further on I came \non another story that said that the importation of tropical fish into \nFlorida, where many of them escaped into the local waterways, was upsetting \nthe ecology of the area.  There was another story about an oil spill; and \nstill another about the development of a car that would not pollute the \nair.  On the editorial page, both the cartoon and the lead editorial urged \nthe development of exhaust-free automobiles as soon as possible; if not, \nthey warned, we wouldn't have to worry about ecology at all any more.\n     Ecology is the branch of science which deals with the balance between \nliving things and their natural environment; as it was used in the news-\npaper stories that I had been reading, it referred to the balance of nature \nitself.  It is something that everyone is concerned with nowadays because \nwe've suddenly awakened to find that man is destroying the balance of \nnature more rapidly than nature can repair the damage.  The wastes from our \nfactories, from our automobiles, from our airplanes, from our power plants \nare polluting our lakes and rivers, the soil of our farms, the animals who \nshare the earth with us, and even the great oceans and the air we breathe.  \nWe face a great danger to our world, and we have discovered it at the \neleventh hour.\n     Let's take some examples.  The industrial cities on the Great Lakes \npour out their wastes into the streams that run into the lakes.  One, of \nthese streams, the Guyahoga River that goes through Cleveland, Ohio is so \nfilled with oil that it is a fire hazard!  Think of that--a river that \ncan catch on fire, indeed that has caught on fire!  Many of the wastes that \nrun into the lakes are rich in the foods that are used by algae, plants \nthat live in the water; they become too well fed.  As they multiply and \nthen die, they choke off the oxygen which is needed by the fish and other \nliving things in the lakes.  The great Lakes are dying, in other words; \ntheir waters are polluted and fewer and fewer forms of life can survive in \nthem.\n     Another killer that we release in the water is oil.  A huge tanker \nbroke up off the south coast of England.  Not only did it dirty the \nbeaches, some of the best in England, but it caused the death of thousands \nof birds and fish.  The birds, their feathers coated with oil so that they \ncould not fly, were a particularly sad sight.  Many people tried to clean \nthe oil from the birds, but many of them were beyond saving.  A similar \ndisaster occurred when an off-shore oil well spilled thousands of gallons \nof oil into the Santa Barbara Channel, off the coast of California.\n     California, with its rapid growth and its millions of automobiles, has \nhad some particularly bad problems with air pollution.  In fact, the word \n\"smog\" was coined to describe the combination of smoke and fog which often \nfills the air over Los Angeles to the extent that the eyes hurt and brea-\nthing becomes difficult.  The word may come from Los Angeles, but the con-\ndition is familiar to such other cities as Tokyo, Mexico City, Dusseldorf, \nand London.  The chief cause of smog is the exhaust from cars and smoke \nfrom factory chimneys or apartment house incinerators.  When you fly over \nany large city nowadays, you are aware of the dirty haze that hangs over it \n-- and into which you must descend.\n     But what to do about it?  Give up automobiles and airplanes?  Give up \nelectricity (for power plants are major sources of air pollution)?  Give up \nfactories?  None of these seems very probable; yet it is necessary to cut \nthe amount of pollution in the air or the earth may become uninhabitable.  \nPeople have already died when smog has settled too long and too thickly \nover one area.  Some scientists believe that the haze in the air is block-\ning out some of the heat from the sun, and that may cause great changes in \nthe climate.  Perhaps we are bringing a new Ice Age upon ourselves.\n     DDT seemed to be one of man's great success stories.  DDT is an \ninsecticide, a chemical used for killing insects.  It first came into wide \nuse about the time of World War II.  It promised to bring two of mankind's \nmost dreaded diseases, typhus and malaria, under control because it killed \nthe insects that carried those diseases.  It was part of the \"green \nrevolution\" in the United States; it was used to spray the fields and kill \nthe insects that might damage the growing crops.  The huge agricultural \nproduction of the united States was in part due to this new protection \nagainst insects.  An enormous part of the earth's surface was sprayed with \nDDT at one time or another.\n     After a few years, however, the first notes of warning were sounded.  \nWonderful as DDT was, it was also doing harm.  One of the most dramatic \nwarnings came from the American naturalist Rachel Carson in a book called \nThe Silent Spring.  She warned that a day might come when there would be no \nmore birds because DDT was killing the insects which were the natural food \nof the birds.  By changing one part of the natural environment, we were, \nwithout realizing it, causing other changes to take place--and we might \nnot find these changes desirable.  When her book appeared, most people did \nnot take Miss Carson's warning too seriously.  The good that DDT was doing \nwas too apparent; its bad effects had not yet become evident.\n     Now, however, no one is inclined any more to say that she was seeing \ndanger where there was none to see.  The effects of DDT have been dis-\ncovered in the last few years to be even more damaging than she predicted.\n     In nature, there is usually a natural balance between living things.  \nOne kind of insect will keep another from becoming too common by using the \nfirst one as food; or the birds will help to keep the insect population \nfrom growing too large.  Other kinds of animals will keep the birds from \nmultiplying too rapidly.  All this natural balance of one life form against \nanother is endangered in the places where DDT has been used too much \nbecause the natural food supply has been changed or destroyed.\n     But DDT has had another unexpected effect.  Many chemicals break down \ninto their different elements very quickly.  DDT is not one of these.  When \nit enters into the \"food chain,\" it can be passed along from one plant or \nanimal to another without disappearing or breaking down into less harmful \nchemicals.  Cows feeding on grass that has been sprayed with DDT take \nsome of it into their systems, and they pass it along to us in their milk:  \nAnd remember that DDT is a poison.\n     The most widely publicized example of DDT'S toxic, or poisonous, \neffect is on birds.  Many birds have taken in large amounts of the insecti-\ncide.  The eggs that are laid by some of these birds have shells that are \nthinner than normal.  Many of the eggs are broken or do not develop, so \nthat some species, or kinds, of birds are in danger of not being able to \nreproduce themselves.  We may indeed some day have a silent spring, without \nthe familiar songs of the birds.\n     The dangers of DDT have become so well known that it is now against \nthe law to use it in many places.  But what substitutes are taking its \nplace, and what will they do to our environment?  We don't really know.\n     The chemical effect of DDT was to kill insects; it also had \nside-effects other than those for which it was used.  Aspirin, to take a \nfamiliar chemical, has the effect of stopping pain; with many people, \nhowever, it has the side-effect of inducing nausea of stomach disorder.  We \nhave often rushed in with chemicals and processes or engineering projects \nwhich were intended to have particular effects.  But almost as often we \nhave not waited long enough to find out what their side-effects would turn \nout to be.  And often enough they have turned out to be harmful.\n     The ecological picture isn't one of total disaster, though it will \ntake centuries perhaps to make sure that we can live on our own earth with-\nout destroying ourselves and the other living things that share our world \nwith us.  Probably the most hopeful part of the ecological picture is that \npeople throughout the world are aroused to the problem.  The word ecology \nis no longer unfamiliar; it occurs frequently on the pages of newspapers, \non the radio and television, in magazine articles.  When I was in college, \nnot so many years ago, ecology was a very specialized branch of biology; in \nfact, I had never heard the term before I went to college--and I forgot \nit for a good many years thereafter.\n     When the people become aware of a problem, and further, aroused by it, \ngovernment and industry are much more apt to take action.  A new power \ngenerating plant is being considered for New York, for example.  A few \nyears ago the major considerations would have been cost, location, and \ncapacity.  Nowadays, however, another factor is sure to be discussed--what \nthe plant will do to the environment.  What kind of fuel will it use?  \nHow much pollution will waste from that fuel add to the air and the water?  \nWill the plant heat up the water surrounding it so that fish will be \nendangered?  Are all possible steps being taken to cut down pollution from \nthe plant?  All these questions will be publicly debated, and the harmful \neffects of the proposed plant will be weighed against the benefits that it \nwill bring."}];
